Let us now describe the structure of the Hamiltonian we consider that satisfies the above constraints. We assume that the system Hamiltonian $H_S$ (rescaled by $m$) is a linear combination of strings of Pauli operators given by
$$
H_S=\sum_{j=1}^{L_S} \lambda_j P_j/m,
$$
such that $\beta_S=\sum_{j}|\lambda_j|$. In our case, the environment is a discrete sum of $m$ sub-environments, such that $H_{E_j}=\beta_{E_j} \sigma^z$ (equivalent to the number operator up to an energy shift). Each sub-environment is prepared in the single qubit thermal state (at some inverse temperature $\omega$), i.e.,
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:sub-environment-thermal}
\rho_{E_j}=\dfrac{\ket{0}\bra{0}+e^{-\omega}\ket{1}\bra{1}}{1+e^{-\omega}}.
\end{equation}
This state can be prepared efficiently by first preparing the entangled pure state
$$
\ket{\psi}=\dfrac{\ket{00}+e^{-\omega/2}\ket{11}}{\sqrt{1+e^{-\omega}}},
$$
and then tracing out the second qubit. Henceforth, we will assume that preparing $\rho_{E_j}$ is a constant-depth unitary procedure.

