\section{Related Work} \label{sec:related_work}



\paragraph{AI co-scientists.} \citet{ifargan2024autonomousllmdrivenresearchdata} built a fully automatic pipeline that generates human-verifiable papers from input datasets. Similarly, \citet{lu2024aiscientistfullyautomated} proposed AI agents that can complete the full research cycle—from idea generation to code, experiments, and paper writing—starting from a template. \citet{gottweis2025aicoscientist} introduced a multi-agent system for hypothesis generation in biomedical research, designed to assist human scientists.




\paragraph{Co-scientists benchmarks.}
Several benchmarks evaluate AI systems across stages of scientific research. CiteME~\cite{press2024citeme}, LitSearch~\cite{ajith2024litsearchretrievalbenchmarkscientific}, and PaperQA2~\cite{skarlinski2024languageagentsachievesuperhuman} focus on citation accuracy for claims. \citet{si2024llmsgeneratenovelresearch} evaluate AI research idea generation through human evaluation. Many benchmarks focus on experiment replication and code generation from papers~\cite{bogin2024superevaluatingagentssetting, huang2024mlagentbenchevaluatinglanguageagents, tian2024scicoderesearchcodingbenchmark, xiao2025csrbenchbenchmarkingllmagents, nathani2025mlgymnewframeworkbenchmark, starace2025paperbenchevaluatingaisability, siegel2024corebenchfosteringcredibilitypublished, seo2025paper2codeautomatingcodegeneration}, or solving scientific tasks like Kaggle challenges~\cite{chan2025mlebenchevaluatingmachinelearning, wijk2024rebenchevaluatingfrontierai, gu2024bladebenchmarkinglanguagemodel}. We build our benchmark on top of prior work on code replication, with a focus on ablation studies in empirical AI research. To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to propose a benchmark centered on ablation studies in scientific research.

\paragraph{LM agents for scientific research.} 
LM agents for scientific tasks often build on ReAct~\cite{yao2023react}, where the model iteratively generates thoughts, performs actions, and receives observations in a containerized environment. Examples include MLAgentBench~\cite{huang2024mlagentbenchevaluatinglanguageagents}, AutoKaggle~\cite{li2024autokagglemultiagentframeworkautonomous}, and AgentK~\cite{grosnit2024largelanguagemodelsorchestrating}. More recently, MLGym~\cite{nathani2025mlgymnewframeworkbenchmark} introduced a framework based on SWE-agent~\cite{yang2024sweagent}, an agent built to automate software engineering using specialized agent-computer interfaces. MLGym is tailored for AI research tasks with built-in agent-computer interfaces for that matter. We also adopt SWE-agent as our baseline agent for evaluation in our benchmark.

\paragraph{LMs as evaluation judges.}
Evaluating model-generated output is inherently ambiguous—different plausible outputs may vary in structure or wording, even if they capture the same underlying idea.
To address this, prior work has researched LMs-as-judges~\cite{Liu2023GEvalNE, zheng2023judging, Fu2023GPTScoreEA, Chan2023ChatEvalTB}, often with chain-of-thought (CoT) prompting~\cite{wei2023chainofthoughtpromptingelicitsreasoning}, showing performance on par with or better than human evaluations. \citet{zhuge2024agentasajudgeevaluateagentsagents} introduced Agent-as-a-Judge for evaluating agentic systems, outperforming previous LM judges. We follow this line of work to design our automatic evaluation system.




\section{Overview of \benchmark{}} 

\benchmark{} is designed for two tasks: First, for generating ablation plans based on a paper’s method section, we built a component called \textit{\researcherbench{}}.  Second, to identify missing ablations in a full paper, similar to what a reviewer would do, we built   \textit{\reviewerbench{}}.

For each task, we start by collecting a set of papers matching the task setup. Next, we design \textit{LM planners} to generate ablation plans for each of these two tasks. Finally, we develop \textit{LM judges} to evaluate the quality of the generated plans by comparing them to gold labels. The judges are used to automate the evaluation of our benchmark, inspired by the judge used in PaperBench~\cite{starace2025paperbenchevaluatingaisability}.
To evaluate how well the judges perform, we also provide a dedicated dataset, \textit{\judgedataset{}}, consisting of \textit{\researcherjudgedataset{}} and \textit{\reviewerjudgedataset{}}, with manually labeled examples for both tasks.

We report the overall \benchmark{} score as the average performance across the two tasks, for a complete view of both the author and reviewer perspectives in ablation planning.
\Cref{fig:1} shows an overview of \benchmark{}. We now describe each component in detail.

\section{\researcherbench{}: Ablation benchmar for authors} \label{sec:researcher_benchmark}
The first \benchmark{} component is designed for the task of proposing ablation experiments given a paper including its method section but without the experiments or result sections. We collect 83 papers and annotate each with the ablation experiments that appear in the full paper, serving as gold labels.

We develop two baseline planners for this task. For a given instance they each generate $k$ proposals for ablation experiments. We also design an automatic judge to evaluate planners. To assess the quality of the judge itself, we created a separate dataset of 63 plans that is human annotated, so we can compare the decisions of the judge with human evaluation of the ablation proposals. 

In the following, we describe the construction, planner, and judge for \researcherbench{} component.


\subsection{Constructing \researcherbench{}} \label{sec:researcher_bench_construction}

\researcherbench{} is built from 83 machine learning conference papers that include ablation studies and open-source code. We collect these papers from prior benchmarks—CSR-Bench (100 papers), SUPER-Expert (45 papers), and PaperBench (20 papers), and supplement them with 38 best paper awardees from CVPR and ICCV (2020–2024) to increase topic and conference diversity.

We filter papers by checking for the presence of ablation sections, then truncate each to include only content up to and including the method section. The ablation plan from the full paper is manually extracted into a structured JSON format to serve as ground truth (GT). Papers are split into 21 for development and 62 for testing. See~\Cref{app:researcher_bench_construction} for full construction details.


\subsection{\researcherbench{} Planner}  \label{sec:researcher_bench_planner}
The planner in \researcherbench{} is tasked with generating an ablation plan given a paper's title, abstract, and truncated content up to the method section. The output is a structured JSONL file containing up to $k$ ablation entries. Each entry should describe the removal or modification of a specific component of the proposed method, aiming to assess its individual contribution.

We implement two planner variants as baselines. The first, \textsc{LM-Planner}, is using a single CoT style prompt to an LM for generating ablation plan. The LM receives the title, abstract, and aggregated \TeX{} source of the paper, and outputs $k$ ablations along with its reasoning.

The second, \textsc{Agent-Planner}, is an agent-based planner built on SWE-agent~\cite{yang2024sweagent}, a ReAct-style LM agent. This planner operates in a containerized environment where it has access to the paper files and basic tools for file inspection and command execution. The agent is prompted similarly but can take intermediate steps, perform actions on the environment, and refine its output before submitting the final ablation plan.

\subsection{\researcherbench{} Judge} \label{sec:researcher_bench_judge}

We evaluate the quality of ablation plans by checking whether each GT ablation is correctly predicted by the model. For each GT ablation we assess whether there is a matching ablation in the generated plan.
A match means that the proposed ablation captures the same idea as the GT one. This includes targeting the same part of the method and applying a similar type of change, such as removing, replacing, or adding something. \Cref{fig:researcher_bench_match_criteria} provides an example of the matching criteria.

\begin{figure}[tb]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.9\textwidth]{assets/match-labels.pdf}
  \caption{Comparison of three generated ablations (blue) with the ground-truth (gold) for a \researcherbench{} instance. (\textbf{Left}) the match is exact; (\textbf{Middle}) the action differs but the generated ablation captures the same GT ablation; (\textbf{Right}) although the component is similar, the replacement does not align with the GT ablation.} \label{fig:researcher_bench_match_criteria}
\end{figure}

We report three metrics for this binary classification task: first, \textit{precision@$k$} measures how many of the top $k$ proposed ablations are in the GT; and second, \textit{recall@$k$} measures how many of the GT ablations are correctly predicted. Lastly, we report the \textit{F1-score@$k$} to measure the balance between the precision and recall.

To support automatic evaluation, we design an LM-based judge that compares the GT ablations to the generated ones and decides whether each one has a match. We implement two scaffoldings for the LM-based judge: (1) \textsc{LMJudge}, a CoT-style prompt to an LM; and (2) \textsc{AgentJudge}, a ReAct-based agent judge allowing multiple steps of reasoning and interaction with an environment before generating the final decision.

To evaluate judge quality and select the final judge for scoring the \researcherbench{} test set, we create a dataset of generated ablation plans for papers in the development set of our benchmark with correct match labels. This dataset,  \textit{\researcherjudgedataset{}}, is used to assess judge performance.

\section{\reviewerbench{}: Ablation benchmark for reviewers} \label{sec:reviewer_bench}

The second \benchmark{} component focuses on suggesting missing ablations for a full paper submission. The goal is to identify important ablations that were not reported but should have been. We collect 350 ICLR submissions along with their official reviews, serving as the gold labels. 

We develop two baseline planners for this task. Given a paper submission they each generate $k$ proposals for missing ablation experiments. We also design an automatic judge to evaluate planners. To assess the quality of the judge itself, we created a separate dataset of 60 plans that is human annotated, so we can compare the decisions of the judge with human evaluation of the ablation proposals. 


In the following, we describe the benchmark, planner, and judge for \reviewerbench{}.

\subsection{Constructing \reviewerbench{} } \label{sec:reviewer_bench_construction}
\reviewerbench{} is built from ICLR submissions from 2023 -- 2025, where reviewers suggested missing ablation experiments. This setting enables the development of reviewer-assistive models that can detect gaps in a paper's empirical validation.

We apply a three-stage pipeline: (1) collect $\sim$89,100 reviews from $\sim$22,800 OpenReview~\cite{openreview} submission forums and filter for reviews mentioning “ablat,”; (2) use a CoT-prompted LM to identify reviews with concrete suggestions for new ablations; and (3) retrieve the submitted PDFs and convert them to markdown for standardized model input. This automatic pipeline makes it easier to extend the benchmark to other conferences and years—unlike \researcherbench{}, which relies on human annotation of GT ablations.

This process results in 5,960 papers in \reviewerbench{}. We randomly sample 50 papers for development and 300 for testing. Full construction details are provided in~\Cref{app:reviewer_bench_construction}.



\subsection{\reviewerbench{} Planner} \label{sec:reviewer_bench_planner}

The \reviewerbench{} planner is given a paper’s title, abstract, and full preprint, and is tasked with generating $k$ missing ablations—experiments that were not reported in the paper but should have been included. This simulates a reviewer’s perspective, where the goal is to identify important omissions in the empirical evaluation.

We implement two planner variants as baselines for this task, both follow the same setup described in \Cref{sec:researcher_bench_planner}: the LM planner uses a single CoT-style prompt to generate up to $k$ missing ablations, while the agent planner uses a ReAct-style agent to iteratively construct its suggestions.

\subsection{\reviewerbench{} Judge}  \label{sec:reviewer_bench_judge}
We evaluate the quality of generated missing ablations by checking whether they match the suggested ablations by reviewers. For each generated ablation, we assess whether there is a matching ablation in one of the reviewer's comments.
A match means that the generated ablation captures the same underlying idea as the reviewer's suggestion. The ablated component must align in both, but specific actions (e.g., remove, replace) or precise modification details are only required when they are explicitly stated in the review.

We report three metrics for this binary classification task: \textit{precision@$k$}, which measures how many of the top $k$ generated ablations match the GT; \textit{recall@$k$}, which measures how many of the GT reviewer suggestions are correctly predicted; and the \textit{F1-score@$k$}, which balances precision and recall.

To enable automatic evaluation, we use two LM-based judges architecture as in~\Cref{sec:researcher_bench_judge}. Each judge compares a generated ablation to the reviews' suggestions and decides whether it has a match.

To evaluate judge quality and select the final judge used for scoring the \reviewerbench{} test set, we construct a dataset of generated ablation plans for papers in the development set, human annotated with match labels against the reviewer suggestions. This dataset,  \textit{\reviewerjudgedataset{}}, is used to assess judge performance.

