In \reviewerbench{}, nearly half (48\%) of all ICLR submissions between 2023 and 2025 include at least one review mentioning the keyword ``ablat'', with about one out of five reviews mention ablations explicitly. This indicates that ablations are a frequent point of feedback throughout the review process. Among the 300 submissions that comprise the test set of the benchmark, 31\% were accepted—matching the overall ICLR acceptance rate—suggesting that reviewer attention to ablations appears independent of final decisions.





\section{Experiments and Results} \label{sec:experiments}

We first describe the experimental setup for the judges and the planners in \researcherbench{} and \reviewerbench{}. We then present the results of the judges and select the best-performing judge for each benchmark to evaluate the test split. Finally, we report and analyze the main results of the baseline planners across both tasks. Additional details are in~\Cref{app:ext_results}.


\subsection{Experimental Setup: Judges}


\paragraph{Agent judges.} 
For the agent judges, we use SWE-agent~\cite{yang2024sweagent} with several frontier LMs: GPT-4o~\cite{openai2024gpt4o}, Claude 3.5 Sonnet~\cite{anthropic2024claude35}, and o3-mini~\cite{openai2025o3mini} (high reasoning). We use tools only for file creation and editing. We also supply a tool for generating the submission file in the required format and a submit command to verify evaluation formatting. Memory is managed using SWE-agent’s default context handling, which keeps the last five observations and collapses earlier ones. Prompts are configured per benchmark's task, based on the matching criteria defined in~\Cref{sec:researcher_bench_judge,sec:reviewer_bench_judge}, to create separate judges for \researcherbench{} and \reviewerbench{}.

\paragraph{LM judges.}
For the LM judges, we use the same LMs as in the agent judges. Each LM is prompted to output a decision for every ablation, along with a reasoning field explaining its choice.

\paragraph{Metrics.}
We report precision, recall, and F1-score for the judges. We also report the average cost (in \$) per evaluated instance. The cost per instance reflects the total API call charges by the model provider.\footnote{We use OpenRouter~\cite{openrouter} as the model provider for all models in our evaluation.} Evaluation is done on \researcherjudgedataset{} and \reviewerjudgedataset{} for the \researcherbench{} judge and \reviewerbench{} judge, respectively. The overall \judgedataset{} score is calculated as the average performance across the two tasks.


\subsection{Experimental Setup: Planners}

\paragraph{Number of generated ablations ($k$).}
We set $k = 5$ for \researcherbench{} and $k = 2$ for \reviewerbench{}, based on the benchmarks statistics presented in~\Cref{table:dataset_stats}.


\paragraph{Agent planners.}
For the agent planners, we use SWE-agent~\cite{yang2024sweagent} with several frontier LMs: GPT-4o~\cite{openai2024gpt4o}, Claude 3.5 Sonnet~\cite{anthropic2024claude35}, and Llama 3.1 405B Instruct~\cite{meta2024llama31}. SWE-agent is configured with default tools for file inspection, editing, and command execution. We provide it with a specialized command to submit the ablation plan and verify that it matches the required format. Prompts are tailored to each benchmark task, as described in~\Cref{sec:researcher_bench_planner,sec:reviewer_bench_planner}, resulting in two planners: one for \researcherbench{} and one for \reviewerbench{}.

\paragraph{LM planners.}
For the LM planners, we use the same LMs as in the agent planners, with two additional smaller, reasoning-oriented models: o3-mini~\cite{openai2025o3mini} and Gemini 2.5 Flash~\cite{google2025gemini25}. Each LM is prompted to output an ablation plan based on the task definition, along with a reasoning field that explains its planning process.

\paragraph{Metrics.}
For both approaches, \textsc{Agent-Planner} and \textsc{LM-Planner}, and for both tasks, \researcherbench{} and \reviewerbench{}, we report macro-average precision@$k$, recall@$k$, and F1-score@$k$. We also report the average cost (in \$) per evaluated instance. The overall \benchmark{} score is calculated as the average performance across the two tasks.



\subsection{Results of Judge Evaluation} \label{sec:judge_results}

\Cref{table:judge_results} shows results for the judges on \judgedataset{}. \textsc{AgentJudge} does not outperform \textsc{LMJudge}. This suggests that for short-context settings, a single CoT step is more effective than multi-step reasoning. \textsc{LMJudge} is also about 5× cheaper than \textsc{AgentJudge}. Based on these results, we use \textsc{LMJudge} with Claude 3.5 Sonnet for evaluation on \benchmark{}.

\Cref{table:researcher_judge_results,table:reviewer_judge_results} show detailed results of the judges across tasks; on \researcherbench{}, both perform similarly, while on \reviewerbench{}, the LM judge with o3-mini performs better, whereas the agent judge performs close to random. \researcherbench{} judges' better performance may be attributed to its structured input format (JSON ablations), whereas the unstructured review text in \reviewerbench{} presents a more difficult evaluation setting. 


