\Cref{table:main_results} presents the main results for \benchmark{}. \Cref{table:researcher_main_results,table:reviewer_main_results} presents separate results for each task. Below, we analyze the performance of the baseline planners, highlighting where they succeed and what factors may limit their current capabilities.


\paragraph{\textsc{LM-Planner} outperforms \textsc{Agent-Planner}.}
On \researcherbench{}, the \textsc{LM-Planner} consistently outperforms the \textsc{Agent-Planner}. Using Claude 3.5 Sonnet, the best-performing agent model, the LM planner achieves an F1 score that is 6 percentage points higher—while also being 4 times cheaper. This difference is statistically significant, with a $p$-value of 0.01 based on a paired t-test of the F1 scores. Moreover, agent's F1 score does not correlate with longer trajectories (Spearman: $-9\%$). Although agents can iterate through files and take multiple reasoning steps, prompting the LM once with the full context and a single CoT step leads to better results. This suggests that future agent systems may require tools better tailored to this task.
On \reviewerbench{}, the planners perform similarly overall; however, the \textsc{LM-Planner} is approximately 3 times cheaper than the agent-based alternative across most models.


\paragraph{For \researcherbench{}, models are more likely to identify ablations that completely remove key components of the method, rather than those that modify them.}
In~\Cref{fig:action_comparison} we observe that recall@5 is consistently higher for removal-type ablations across planners and models, suggesting that LMs are more effective at identifying core components of a method. In contrast, modification-type ablations require not only identifying the component but also proposing relevant and feasible substitutions—often informed by domain-specific knowledge—which remains a challenge for current models. For GPT-4o, this difference is statistically significant, with a $p$-value of 0.01 based on a paired t-test of recall@5 scores per paper across both planner types.

\paragraph{For \reviewerbench{}, we found no clear correlation between recall and review or submission metadata.}
We tested whether planner performance (recall) correlates with various metadata features, including year of submission, paper acceptance, number of reviews, and review length. We found no correlation with any of these variables, suggesting that model performance is not explained by surface-level attributes of the review process (See~\Cref{app:ext_results} for details).

