\section{Conclusion} \label{sec:conclusion}

We introduced \textbf{\benchmark{}}, a suite for evaluating ablation planning in empirical AI research. It includes \textbf{\researcherbench{}}, which tasks models with generating ablations from a paper's method, and \textbf{\reviewerbench{}}, which focuses on suggesting missing ablations from full submissions—capturing both author and reviewer perspectives.

Each benchmark includes a baseline planner -- that performs the task, and an LM judge -- for automatic evaluation of planners. CoT prompting outperforms agent-based methods in both accuracy and cost. The best models identify 34\% of ablations in \researcherbench{} and 26\% in \reviewerbench{}. 

\textbf{Limitations:} Our current evaluation framework 
has two key limitations. First, the framework relies on LM-based judges which, while achieving strong classification performance, still leave room for improvement. Second, models may produce meaningful and feasible ablations that are not included in the ground-truth. We hope \benchmark{} will support future research toward developing stronger LM agents capable of robust and creative ablation planning.

