\paragraph{Results by year.}
\Cref{table:results_per_year} compares recall@2 on submissions from before 2025 and from 2025. The 2025 submissions were not available during any of the models' training, so they help assess generalization. Results are similar across both splits and show no consistent trend across models. This suggests that model performance is not strongly affected by the submission year, indicating generalization beyond the training data.

\paragraph{Results per acceptance status.}
\Cref{table:results_per_decision} reports recall@2 scores by acceptance status. Performance is broadly similar across accepted and rejected submissions, with some models (e.g., o3-mini, Gemini Flash) performing better on rejected papers. This suggests that model success does not correlate with paper acceptance, and that ablation-relevant information is present in both groups. It may also indicate that reviewers tend to suggest similarly structured ablations regardless of the submission’s overall quality.

\subsection{Qualitative Analysis}
We analyze the behavior of the \textsc{LM-Planner} with o3-mini on a paper from \researcherbench{}, where the model failed completely (0 on all metrics).

GPT-GNN~\cite{hu2020gptgnngenerativepretraininggraph} introduces a framework for generative pre-training of GNNs by factorizing graph likelihood into two components: (1) attribute generation and (2) edge generation. The paper was published at KDD 2020.

GPT-GNN has six ablations in the GT:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Removing \textbf{attribute generation} component.
    \item Removing \textbf{edge generation} component.
    \item Removing \textbf{attribute generation} and \textbf{node generation} components.
    \item Removing \textbf{edge generation} and \textbf{adaptive queue}.
    \item Replacing the \textbf{GNN architecture} with: GCN, GAT, RGCN, HAN.
    \item Varying the \textbf{amount of labeled data during fine-tuning}.
\end{enumerate}

Surprisingly, although the first two GT ablations target the main components of the method and appear in the abstract, introduction, and method sections, the \textsc{LM-Planner} with o3-mini fails to identify either. Instead, it generates the following ablation plan:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Removing the \textbf{dependency-aware factorization} that jointly models attribute and edge generation to capture their inherent dependency.
    \item Replacing the \textbf{separation of nodes into Attribute Generation and Edge Generation types} to a unified node representation.
    \item Removing the \textbf{adaptive negative queue}.
    \item Varying the \textbf{ratio of observed edges versus masked edges} used during the factorized generation process.
    \item Replacing the \textbf{subgraph sampling strategy} with: LADIES, uniform sampling, HGSampling.
\end{enumerate}

While the proposed ablations are technically relevant, they are overly focused on fine-grained aspects of the method. This results in missing the core architectural components central to the paper’s contribution. As shown in~\Cref{fig:gpt_gnn_reasoning}, the model’s reasoning reflects a tendency to overfit to implementation-level details rather than capturing the high-level goals of ablation studies.


{
\begin{researcherpromptbox}{Reasoning}
In our proposed ablation plan, we target the core components that contribute to the performance of GPT‐GNN. First, we evaluate the importance of the dependency‐aware factorization that couples attribute generation and edge generation by completely removing the coupling to see how much the interaction contributes to capturing structure–attribute dependencies. Second, we investigate the design choice of separating node representations into two types (Attribute Generation vs. Edge Generation) by replacing it with a unified representation to understand if avoiding information leakage is essential. Third, we examine the effect of the adaptive negative sampling queue in the edge generation task by removing it, which assesses how global negative samples improve the contrastive loss. Fourth, we study the sensitivity of the observed versus masked edge ratio used in the factorized generation process by experimenting with different ratios. Finally, we explore how subgraph sampling strategies for large-scale graphs affect performance by replacing the LADIES/HGSampling method with alternatives like uniform sampling. We report downstream metrics such as node classification accuracy and link prediction AUC (as reported in the paper) as well as intrinsic generation losses (e.g., attribute generation perplexity and edge contrastive loss) when applicable.
\end{researcherpromptbox}
