\section{Complexity and Convergence Analysis}\label{J3 Section Analysis}

In this section, we analyze the proposed DGD algorithm first in terms of its computational complexity and then in terms of the convergence behavior of the alternating approach.
\par\smallskip\noindent\textbf{Parameter complexity.} The parameters to be estimated are $\left(\frac{N-1}{2} + 2T\right)NR + (R+2N)T$. These come from two sources: (i) $\left(\frac{N-1}{2} + 2T\right)NR$ parameters associated with the $R$ matrices $\bbA_r$, which are symmetric with zero diagonals, and the remaining parameters are related to the additional variables $\bbP$ and $\bbLambda_r$; and (ii) $(R+2N)T$ parameters of the $R$ temporal signatures $\bbC$ and the additional variables $\bbQ$ and $\bbLambda$.
\par The number of parameters of the original problem [cf.\eqref{eq.General}] is $N^2T+TR$ 
\par\smallskip\noindent\textbf{Computational complexity.} Recalling that $I$ is the number of alternating iterations, the computational complexity of DGD is of order $\ccalO((N^3R + N^2TR + R^2N^2+ T^2R + NTR)I)$. For each iteration $i=1,...,I$, the computational complexity is broken down as follows. The computations for estimating $\bbA_r$ are governed by the gradient $\nabla_{\bbA_r}L(\bbA_r)$ in \eqref{ADMM_A} and the updates of the auxiliary variables $\bbP$ in \eqref{ADMM_A_P} and $\bbLambda_r$ in \eqref{ADMM_A_Dual}. We update the $R$ matrices $\bbA_r$, $\bbP$, and $\bbLambda_r$, leading to a total computational cost of $\ccalO((N^3 + N^2T)R)$. In addition, computing $\bbC$ has a cost of order $\ccalO((R^2T^2 + T^2R))$, and each update of the auxiliary variables $\bbQ$ and $\bbLambda$ has a complexity of order $\ccalO(NTR)$.
\begin{remark}
While the updates for $\bbA_r$ and $\bbC$ in \eqref{ADMM_A} and \eqref{ADMM_C} are carried out via projected gradient descent, one could alternatively derive a closed-form solution from the unconstrained problem and then apply a projection. However, this closed-form solution involves an inverse with complexity of order $\ccalO(N^3)$ for each $\bbA_r$ per ADMM iteration and $\ccalO(R^3T^3)$ for each $\bbC$ update since vectorization is required to obtain the optimal value. Moreover, projecting onto $\ccalS_A$ and $\ccalS_C$ afterward may introduce large adjustments, potentially affecting the convergence of Algorithm \ref{Algorithm TGD}. By contrast, a gradient step before projection is computationally less demanding, enforces more gradual change (due to the convex nature of the functions), and works effectively in practice.
\end{remark}
\subsection{Convergence analysis}\label{J3 Analysis}
In this section, we discuss the convergence of the proposed ADMM-based approach in Algorithm \ref{Algorithm TGD} to a stationary point w.r.t. the $\bbA_r$s and $\bbC$.
% We have one alternating optimization procedure involving $R+1$ updates ($R$ for the $\bbA_r$s and $1$ for $\bbC$).
\begin{assumption}\label{J3 Assumption 1}
For all $r$, $\sum_{t=1}^T[\bbC]_{t,r}^2\bbone^{\top}\bbm_t>0$, i.e., the sum of the product of the squared temporal signature coefficient with the number of available observations across time is greater than zero.
\end{assumption}
This condition holds as long as the temporal signature of the $r$-th latent graph $\bbc_r\neq\bbzero_T$ across time and we have at least one available observation at each time instant. In practice, we make several observations at each time step, i.e., $\bbone^{\top}\bbm_t>0$ is easy to satisfy. A nonzero temporal signature $\bbc_r$ indicates that the corresponding latent adjacency matrix $\bbA_r$ is relevant to expressing the structural evolution.
\smallskip
\par\noindent\textbf{Convergence.} Since our decomposition approach is a block-wise minimization, we consider the block-coordinate minimizer as shown in \cite{buciulea2024polynomial,xu2013block}. Let $\chi$ be the set comprising all the latent variables $\{\bbA_1,\ldots,\bbA_R,\bbC\}$. The variables $\{\barbA_1,\ldots,\barbA_r,\barbC\}$ are a block coordinate minimizer of $f(\cdot)$ if we have
\begin{align}
&f(\barbA_1,\ldots,\barbA_r,\ldots,\barbA_R,\barbC)\leq f(\barbA_1,\ldots,\bbA_r,\ldots,\barbA_R,\barbC) \nonumber \\
&~\forall~r,\bbA_r\!\in\!\ccalS_{\bbA}\,\text{and}\,\{\barbA_1,\ldots,\bbA_r,\ldots,\barbA_R,\barbC\}\!\in\!\chi,\\
&f(\barbA_1,\ldots,\barbA_r,\ldots,\barbA_R,\barbC)\leq f(\barbA_1,\ldots,\barbA_r,\ldots,\barbA_R,\bbC)\nonumber\\
&~\forall~\bbC\in\ccalS_{\bbC}~\textnormal{and}~\{\barbA_1,\ldots,\barbA_R,\barbC\}\in\chi
\end{align}
\begin{proposition}\label{J3 Proposition Stationary Convergence}
    Let Assumption \ref{J3 Assumption 1} hold. Then, the sequence $\{\bbA_1^{k},\ldots,\bbA_R^k,\bbC^k\}_{i=1}^I$ generated by Algorithm \ref{Algorithm TGD}, as $K\rightarrow \infty$, converges to a stationary point, which is also known as the block coordinate minimizer of Problem \eqref{Opt prob basic}.
\end{proposition}
\par\noindent\textit{Proof.} See Appendix \ref{app:sectionB}.\qed
\begin{comment}
\par To recap what we have so far, we solve Problem \eqref{Opt prob basic} with an alternating minimization approach. We perform two block coordinate descents. The first, $\textnormal{BCD-1}$ refers to the sequential updates between the $\bbA_r$s.  The second, $\textnormal{BCD-2}$ involves the alternating update between $\bbA$ and $\bbC$. To analyze the loss function in \eqref{Opt prob basic} we can write down the equivalent formulation in terms of $\bbA_0=[\text{vec}(\bbA_1),\ldots,\text{vec}(\bbA_R)]$. We do this by re-writing some of the terms in the original loss function in terms of $\bbA_0$. The data-incorporating prior becomes $\delta\tr(\bbA_0\bbC^{\top}\bar{\bbZ}_T)$, while the term enforcing non-overlapping support becomes $\beta\tr(\bbA_0^{\top}\bbA_0\circ\bbM\mathbf1\mathbf1^{\top})$ with $\bbM=\mathbf 1_R\mathbf 1_R^{\top}-\bbI_{R}$. The optimization problem we will analyze becomes
\begin{align}
    \begin{split} &\underset{\bbA_0,\bbC}{\text{minimize}}\quad  f(\bbA_0,\bbC)=\frac{1}{2}||\bbA_{\textnormal{vec}}-\bbA_0\bbC^{\top}||_F^2\!\!+\gamma\mathbf 1_{N^2}^{\top}\bbA_0\mathbf 1_R\\&\! +\!\delta\tr(\bbA_0\bbC^{\top}\bar{\bbZ}_T)\!\!+\!\! \mu||\bbD\bbC||_F^2+\!\!\beta\tr(\bbA_0^{\top}\bbA_0\circ\bbM\mathbf1\mathbf1^{\top})
    \\&+\frac{\eta}{2}||\bbA_0||_F^2+\frac{\rho}{2}||\bbC||_F^2\\
    &\text{subject to}~\bbA_0=[\text{vec}(\bbA_1),\ldots,\text{vec}(\bbA_R)],\\&~\bbA_r\geq\bbzero,~\tr(\bbA_r)=0,~\bbA_r=\bbA_r^{\top}
    \end{split}
\end{align}where $\frac{\eta}{2}\sum_{r=1}^R\tr(\bbA_r^{\top}\bbA_r)=\frac{\eta}{2}\tr(\bbA_0^{\top}\bbA_0)=\frac{\eta}{2}||\bbA_0||_F^2$. To proceed, we impose a few assumptions and draw upon results from alternating optimization.
\begin{assumption}\label{Assumption bounded optimal variables}
 The  variables $\bbA_0$ and $\bbC$ belong to a bounded set., i.e.,
    \begin{align}
        ||\bbA_0||_F\leq A,~||\bbC||_F\leq C.
    \end{align}
\end{assumption}890-The variable $\bbA_0$ is bounded in its Frobenius norm as each column of it represents the elements of an adjacency matrix, which are bounded. We constrain $\bbC$ to be slowly varying temporal signals, which are usually bounded in their Frobenius norm. As a consequence, the sequence of variables $\{\bbA_0^k,\bbC^k\}$ generated via Algorithm \ref{Algorithm TGD} are also bounded in their Frobenius norm.
Bi-convexity of the fuction in both variables is of importance to establish convergence of Algorithm \ref{Algorithm TGD} to a local minima. 
\subsection{Convergence to Stationary point}
A stationary point is one where the gradients w.r.t. $\bbA_0$ and $\bbC$ equal zero. To show convergence to a stationary point, we need the following assumption.
\begin{assumption}\label{A0 rank R}
    For a stationary point $\bbA_0$ and $\bbC$, both have rannk $R$.
\end{assumption}
\begin{assumption}\label{sylvester criterion}
    For a stationary point $\bbA_0$, the matrices $\bbD^{\top}\bbD$ and $-\bbA_0^{\top}\bbA_0$.
\end{assumption}Assumption \ref{sylvester criterion} is required for the uniqueness of the $\bbC$ update [cf. \eqref{C update}], which corresponds to the uniqueness of the solution to Matrix Sylvester-type Equations \cite{wu2008solution}. Note that the matrices $\bbD^{\top}\bbD$ and $\bbA_0^{\top}\bbA_0$ are positive semi-definite in general, so the only eigenvalue $\bbD^{\top}\bbD$ and $-\bbA_0^{\top}\bbA_0$ can share is zero. $\bbD^{\top}\bbD$ has one of its eigenvalues as zero. When $\bbA_0$ obeys Assumption \ref{A0 rank R}, Assumption \ref{sylvester criterion} holds. We have the following Lemma:
\begin{proposition}\label{lemma stationary}
    Algorithm \ref{Algorithm TGD} converges to a stationary point in the variables $\bbA_r$ and $\bbC$ under Assumption \ref{sylvester criterion}.
\end{proposition}
\noindent\textbf{Proof}: Check Appendix \ref{app stationary}.\qed
\subsection{Convergence to Local Minima}
While convergence to a stationary point is desirable, the alternating algorithm can lead to a saddle point or a local maxima. For convergence to local minima, we rely on the criterion established for alternating minimization in \cite{li2019alternating}. Before we elaborate further.
\begin{assumption}\label{Assumption rank}
    After each update step, the updates $\bbA_0{k}$ and $\bbC^{k} $ have rank $r$.
\end{assumption}
\begin{assumption}\label{assumption A0 feasibility}
   For each update in $\bbA_0$ (equivalently $\bbA$), the optimal $\bbA_0$ lies in the feasible set. 
\end{assumption}This assumption implies that in the update step for finding the adjacency modes, the optimal modes exist in the feasible set, in the form of symmetric adjacency matrices. 


\begin{proposition}\label{Proposition Local Convergence}
Under Assumptions \ref{Assumption bounded optimal variables}, \ref{assumption A0 feasibility} and Lemmas \ref{lemma bi convexity} and \ref{lemma stationary}, if for any stationary point $(\tilde{\bbA}_0,\tilde{\bbC})$, the matrix $(\tilde\bbC\tilde\bbA_0^{\top}-\bbA_{\textnormal{vec}}^{\top}+\delta\bar{\bbZ}_T)$ has rank $T$, then the alternating minimization algorithm \ref{Algorithm TGD} converges almost surely to a local minima, except when $\bbC$ is non-negative.
\end{proposition}
\par\noindent\textbf{Proof.} Check Appendix \ref{app local minima}.\hfill\qed \\
The Proposition states that the matrix $(\tilde\bbC\tilde\bbA_0^{\top}-\bbA_{\textnormal{vec}}^{\top}+\delta\bar{\bbZ}_T)$ plays a key role in the convergence to local minima.
The term $(\tilde\bbC\tilde\bbA_0^{\top}-\bbA_{\textnormal{vec}}^{\top}$ is the residue obtained at the stationary point. The second is the matrix $\bar{\bbZ}_T$. Thus, we have a condition which links the reconstruction of $\bbA_{\textnormal{vec}}$ along with a matrix which provides the observed data $\bar{\bbZ}_T$. Recall that the $t$ row of $\bar{\bbZ}_T$ is $\text{vec}(\bbZ_t)^T$. The matrix $\bbZ_t$ of size $N\times N$ contains the squared Euclidean distance between the feature vectors at the nodes at time $t$. 
\par Thus, $\bar\bbZ_T$ captures the temporal signal variation across all nodes over time. If the graph signals are entirely static across time, i.e., $\bbX_t$ is constant, then $\bbZ_t$ is also constant, thus, $\bar\bbZ_t$ has rank one. 
\par Note that if $\bbC$ is non-negative, the optimum of the unconstrained and the constrained problem w.r.t. $\bbC$ are not necessarily the same. This is verifiable from the update of $\bbC$. Thus, there is no guarantee of convergence to local minima.
\subsection{Uniqueness}
We want the recovered modes $\{\bbA_r\}$ to be uniquely determined. That is, if we run Algorithm \ref{Algorithm TGD} multiple times and obtain a different set of $\bbA_r$s each time, they are related through permutation and scaling. We note that the proposed decomposition is akin to a matrix factorization with constraints on each factor. Uniqueness of matrix factorization is studied for non-negative factorization. Thus, to study uniqueness, we need to impose non-negativity on $\bbC$. We rely on the following necessary criterion given in Theorem 3 in \cite{huang2013non}. We re-write it in our formulation as follows:
\begin{corollary}\label{J3 corollary uniqueness}
    Consider the non-negative factorizatio $\bbA_{\textnormal{vec}}=\bbA_0\bbC^{\top}$. Let the support of column $i$ or $\bbA_0$ be $\ccalI_i$. If the decomposition is unique, then for any two distinct $i$, $j$, $\ccalI_i\nsubseteq \ccalI_j$ and $\ccalI_j\nsubseteq \ccalI_i$. 
\end{corollary}
\par\noindent\textbf{Proof.} Check Appendix B of \cite{huang2013non}.\qed\\
The above corollary states that the support of all columns of $\bbA_0$ have to be non-overlapping for uniqueness. Each column of $\bbA_0$ corresponds to the vectorized version of one of the mode adjacency matrices, thus its support denotes the edges, i.e, the topology. The necessary criterion implies none of the $\bbA_r$s have the same edge set. Note that in our approach, the loss $\beta\tr(\bbA_0^{\top}\bbA_0\circ\bbM\mathbf1\mathbf1^{\top})$ penalizes shared support. Thus, our approach promotes the necessary criterion for uniqueness.
