\title{\vspace{-1.5cm}Policy Optimization and Multi-agent Reinforcement Learning for Mean-variance Team Stochastic Games }
\author{Junkai Hu, Li Xia\thanks{J. Hu and L. Xia are both with the School of Business, Sun Yat-Sen University, Guangzhou 510275, China. (email: xiali5@sysu.edu.cn)}}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle
\vspace{-0.2cm}
\begin{abstract}
    We study a long-run mean-variance team stochastic game (MV-TSG), where agents act independently to optimize a shared mean-variance objective. MV-TSG presents two key challenges: (1) the variance metric's non-additivity and non-Markovian nature in dynamic settings, and (2) non-stationary environment due to simultaneous policy updates of all agents. Both challenges render dynamic programming inapplicable. In this paper, we propose a novel Mean-Variance Multi-Agent Policy Iteration (MV-MAPI) algorithm for MV-TSGs based on the sensitivity-based optimization theory and a sequential update scheme. We prove that MV-MAPI converges monotonically to a first-order stationary point, and derive conditions under which such points correspond to (local) Nash equilibria or even strict local optima. We further develop a multi-agent reinforcement learning algorithm based on MV-MAPI. Numerical experiments on energy management in multiple microgrid systems  validate our main results. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to propose theoretically provable algorithms for MV-TSGs.

\end{abstract}

\textbf{Keywords}: Team stochastic game, mean-variance, policy optimization, multi-agent reinforcement learning

\section{Introduction}
Stochastic games, proposed by Nobel laureate \cite{shapley1953stochastic} and also known as Markov games \citep{littman1994markov}, combine matrix games and Markov chains, and offer a general framework for sequential decision problems involving multiple agents (decision makers). In these games, agents act \textit{independently} based on state-dependent policies, with game states transitioning according to a state transition probability matrix influenced by the joint actions of all agents. As a consequence of the joint action and state transition, each agent receives an immediate reward at each time step. Agents aim to maximize their accumulated rewards (either discounted or undiscounted) within a given time horizon. According to the reward relationship among agents, stochastic games are categorized into three groups: team stochastic games (TSGs, also known as cooperative Markov games \citep{zhong2024heterogeneous}), zero-sum stochastic games, and general-sum stochastic games. These categories correspond to cooperative, competitive, and mixed settings in the field of multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) \citep{yang2020overview}. The Nash equilibrium (NE) is the most common solution concept, indicating that no agent has an incentive to change its policy, given that the policies of the other agents are fixed.

TSGs, a subclass of Markov potential games \citep{leonardos2022global, zhang2024gradient}, model cooperative systems where all agents share a common reward function. These systems are prevalent in practical applications such as supply chain coordination \citep{oroojlooyjadid2022deep}, resource balancing in logistics \citep{li2019cooperative}, and optimal multi-order execution in finance \citep{fang2023learning}, making the study of TSGs valuable and attractive.

Risk attitudes are key in decision-making processes, particularly in stochastic environments \citep{cavazos2023average}. As a
fundamental measure of reward variability, variance has been widely adopted to quantify risk in
diverse applications. \cite{nikolova2014mean} and \cite{lianeas2019risk} study stochastic congestion games for transportation networks, with agents optimizing mean-variance tradeoffs in travel times, and a risk-averse NE is derived. \cite{gupta2020dual} model the dual-sourcing problem as a Stackelberg game, where suppliers act as leaders with the mean-variance utility of profits, and the retailer is risk-neutral. Closed-form expressions for the NE solution to this problem are derived. \cite{slumbers2023game} study a risk-averse equilibrium where each player aims to minimize the reward variance induced by the policies of other players. However, their algorithms lack convergence guarantees. Moreover, the studies above primarily focus on static or normal-form games, rather than on stochastic dynamic games. 

Limited research investigates stochastic games with risk-sensitive objectives. One group of works
focuses on establishing the equilibrium existence for specific risk measures such as exponential utility \citep{basu2014zero} and conditional value at risk (CVaR) \citep{liu2023zero}, while the algorithmic study is not presented.  \cite{etesami2018stochastic} and \cite{wu2024relationships} propose heuristics for NE computation in prospect-theoretic stochastic games, yet their algorithms may traverse all possible policies in the worst-case, which is computationally exhaustive. Recent progress by \cite{mazumdar2024tractable} demonstrates convergence guarantees for risk-averse quantal response equilibria in finite-horizon settings, suggesting promising directions for tractable solutions.
As highlighted in the above literature, solving stochastic games with risk objectives has garnered increasing attention and presents significant challenges.

This paper explores mean-variance team stochastic games (MV-TSGs), where agents seek policies that collectively maximize the long-run mean-variance of the common rewards. Two challenges arise in addressing MV-TSGs: (1) the non-additive and non-Markovian nature of the variance metric, as the variance depends on both current and future joint actions in a dynamic setting; and (2)  non-stationarity, since each agent's policy is part of the environment of other agents, and the simultaneous policy updates of all agents make the environment non-stationary for each agent. These two challenges prevent the direct use of dynamic programming for this problem.

\subsection{Main Results and Contributions}
In this paper, we propose a novel approach to deal with the aforementioned challenges in MV-TSGs. Our main contributions are twofold as follows. 

\begin{itemize}
    \item We propose a Mean-Variance Multi-Agent Policy Iteration (MV-MAPI) algorithm built upon the results of the sensitivity-based optimization theory and the sequential update scheme, which is stated in Algorithm~\ref{alg:MVMAPI}. %The algorithm inherits the advantages of the sequential update scheme, and its framework is presented in Algorithm~\ref{alg:MVMAPI}. 
    We prove that the algorithm converges to a first-order stationary point of the objective function with a monotonicity property. Moreover, we describe the local geometry of the first-order stationary points and derive verifiable conditions for these points to be (local) NEs or strict local optima. We further develop a modified MV-MAPI algorithm, as stated in Algorithm~\ref{alg:mod_MVMAPI}, which prevents agents from getting trapped in certain types of first-order stationary points and converges to a strict local optimum.

    \item We extend the idea of trust region methods to MV-MAPI and propose a MARL algorithm called Mean-Variance Multi-Agent Trust Region Policy Optimization (MV-MATRPO), which is presented in Algorithm~\ref{alg:MVTRPO}. This data-driven algorithm is developed to handle MV-TSGs with larger scales and unknown environmental model parameters. We prove that the algorithm enjoys a performance lower bound for the joint policy update in each iteration, which ensures monotonic improvements when the trust region is sufficiently tight. 
\end{itemize} 
We validate our algorithms in an energy management problem for multiple microgrid systems (MMSs), optimizing the mean-variance objective of the exchanged power between the MMS and the main grid. Numerical results confirm the effectiveness of our algorithms. To the best of our knowledge, this work is the first that develops a policy iteration-type algorithm and a MARL algorithm to optimize the mean-variance metric in TSGs.

\subsection{Related Works}

The study of team games has been a prolific research topic for decades. Early works, such as \cite{marschak1955elements} and \cite{radner1962team}, study single-stage cooperative team models, while later studies investigate related issues in dynamic systems and model them as team theory problems \citep{marschak1972economic,ho1980team}. 
% Team games have been a subject of study in diverse fields, including economics \citep{kim2022games}, management \citep{durr2020incentives}, operation research \citep{sanjari2023optimality}, control \citep{sanjari2020optimal}, etc. 
Given the prevalence of team collaboration scenarios in practice and the remarkable success of RL in recent years, TSGs and cooperative MARL have attracted widespread attention.

One straightforward approach for addressing TSGs involves agents independently updating their policies using single-agent methods. However, this leads to the issue of non-stationarity, as agents' policy updates alter the dynamics of the environment, violating the stationary environment assumption required by single-agent algorithms. Consequently, the direct application of single-agent methods is unsuitable for TSGs. 

MARL provides a framework to approximately address TSGs, especially in scenarios where the environmental model parameters are unknown. The policy of each agent is updated based on data collected from their interactions with the environment. Early cooperative MARL algorithms, such as Team-Q \citep{littman2001value}, Distributed-Q \citep{lauer2000algorithm}, JAL (Joint Action Learner) \citep{claus1998dynamics}, and OAL (Optimal Adaptive Learning) \citep{wang2002reinforcement}, target specific scenarios but are limited by assumptions such as centralized decision-makers, deterministic state transitions, or single-stage problems. More recent decentralized algorithms, such as those by \cite{arslan2016decentralized} and \cite{yongacoglu2021decentralized}, address discounted TSGs and almost surely converge to NEs or optimal NEs (achieving the optimal value of the objective function). The algorithms proposed in these studies rely on random exploration and comparison mechanisms, which may lead to low sample efficiency and slow convergence rates. 

Over the past decade, the centralized training with decentralized execution (CTDE) paradigm \citep{kraemer2016multi} has become a prevalent MARL framework. During the on-line decentralized execution phase of CTDE, agents act independently based on individual policies. In the off-line centralized training phase, all agents have access to the full trajectory data to update policies. Despite its popularity, algorithms that follow the CTDE paradigm still face non-stationarity challenges.

CTDE-based cooperative MARL algorithms are categorized into value decomposition and policy gradient methods. Value-decomposition algorithms factorize the joint action-value function $Q^{tot}$ into individual action-value functions  $Q^{ind}_i$ for each agent $i$. The following Individual-Global-Maximum (IGM) condition \citep{sunehag2018value} is necessary for the validity of these algorithms: 
\begin{equation*}
    \mathop{\arg\max}_{\bm{a}}Q^{tot}(s,\bm{a})= 
    \begin{pmatrix}
    \mathop{\arg\max}\limits_{a_1}Q^{ind}_1(s,a_1) \\
    \vdots \\
    \mathop{\arg\max}\limits_{a_N}Q^{ind}_N(s,a_N)
    \end{pmatrix} 
    \qquad (\text{IGM condition}),
    \notag
\end{equation*}
where $N$ is the number of agents, $a_i$ is the action taken by agent $i$, and $\bm{a}=(a_1,\cdots,a_N)$ is the joint action of all agents. 
However, it is challenging to identify individual action-value functions $Q^{ind}_i$ for each agent $i$ that satisfy the IGM condition. The value-decomposition algorithms usually design specific neural networks to express corresponding action-value functions \citep{rashid2020monotonic}, which suggests that these algorithms often lack rigorous theoretical analysis.
On the other hand, policy gradient methods, originally developed for Markov decision processes (MDPs) or single-agent RL problems, are extended to multi-agent settings. The environmental
non-stationarity is still the key issue in corresponding algorithms \citep{kuba2022trust}.

Although extensive research has been conducted on TSGs, most research focuses on risk-neutral settings with discounted accumulated rewards. Recent studies, \cite{qiu2021rmix} and \cite{shen2023riskq}, investigate cooperative MARL with risk-sensitive utility functions. However, their focus is on designing neural networks with specific structures to approximately satisfy certain conditions. Due to the inherent non-interpretability of neural networks, their algorithms lack comprehensive theoretical analysis. To the best of our knowledge, no existing work provides algorithms with theoretical analysis and convergence guarantees for risk-sensitive TSGs or cooperative MARL. 

\subsection{Organization}
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section~\ref{sec:preliminary} introduces MV-TSGs and provides preliminary knowledge on average-reward trust region methods. Section~\ref{sec:MAPI} presents the MV-MAPI algorithm and analyzes its convergence. Subsequently, the MV-MATRPO algorithm is developed in Section~\ref{sec:MV-MARL}.  Section~\ref{sec:exp_results} validates the effectiveness of our algorithms in an energy management problem of MMSs. Finally, Section~\ref{sec:conclusion} concludes this paper.


\section{Problem Setting}
\label{sec:preliminary}

In this paper, we focus on an infinite-horizon discrete-time TSG, which is denoted as $\langle\mathcal{N},\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, P, r \rangle$. Here, $\mathcal{N}=\{1,\cdots, N\}$ is a finite set of agents, $\mathcal{S}$ is a finite set of system states, $\mathcal{A}=\prod_{i\in \mathcal{N}}\mathcal{A}_i$ is the finite joint action space of all agents, $\mathcal{A}_i$ is the action space of agent $i\in \mathcal{N}$,  $P:\mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \mapsto \Delta(\mathcal{S})$ is the transition probability function ($\Delta(\mathcal{S})$ is the probability distributions over $\mathcal{S}$), and $r:\mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{A} \mapsto \mathbb{R}$ is a common reward function shared by all agents. In infinite-horizon TSGs, a stationary policy for each agent $i \in \mathcal{N}$ is $\mu_i:\mathcal{S} \mapsto \Delta(\mathcal{A}_i)$ and its policy space is defined as $\mathcal{U}_i$. We define the joint policy as $\bm\mu=(\mu_1,\cdots,\mu_N)$ and the joint policy space is $\mathcal{U} = \prod_{i\in \mathcal{N}}(\mathcal{U}_i)$. 
Given state $s$, the probability of all agents choosing the joint action $\bm{a}=(a_1,\ldots,a_N)$ is $\bm\mu(\bm{a}|s)=\prod_{i\in\mathcal{N}}\mu_i(a_i|s)$. In addition, if $\bm\mu$ is a deterministic rule, i.e., $\bm\mu:\mathcal{S}\mapsto\mathcal{A}$, we call $\bm\mu$ a deterministic joint policy. Let $\mathcal{D}$ denote the deterministic joint policy space and we have $\mathcal{D} \subset \mathcal{U}$. We make the following ordinary ergodic assumption in this study.

\begin{assumption}\label{assum1}
The Markov chain induced by any joint policy $\bm\mu \in \mathcal{U}$ is ergodic.
\end{assumption}

We define the long-run average reward of TSGs under a joint policy $\bm\mu$ as 
\begin{equation*}
    \eta^{\bm{\mu}}:=\lim\limits_{T \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{T} \mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mu}} \Big[ \sum\limits_{t=0}^{T-1}r(s_t,\bm{a}_t)  \Big], \notag
\end{equation*}
where $\mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mu}}$ stands for the expectation with $\bm{a}_t \sim \bm{\mu} (\cdot|s_t)$, $s_{t+1} \sim P(\cdot | s_t,\bm{a}_t)$. When $T \rightarrow \infty$, $\eta^{\bm{\mu}}$ is independent of the initial state $s_0$, with ergodic Assumption~\ref{assum1}. We denote $\pi^{\bm\mu}: \mathcal{S} \mapsto \Delta(\mathcal{S})$ as the steady state distribution under the joint policy $\bm\mu$. The long-run average reward can be rephrased as 
\begin{equation}
    \eta^{\bm{\mu}}= \mathbb{E}_{s\sim \pi^{\bm{\mu}}, \bm{a} \sim \bm{\mu}}[r(s,\bm{a})]  
    = \sum\limits_{s \in \mathcal{S}} \pi^{\bm\mu}(s) \sum\limits_{\bm{a} \in \mathcal{A}} r(s,\bm{a})\bm\mu(\bm{a}|s).  \label{eq:eta-2} 
\end{equation}
Additionally, we are concerned with the long-run mean-variance of the common reward. which describes the variance of the steady reward distribution and has been studied by \cite{filar1989variance} and \cite{xia2020risk} in single-agent settings. In TSGs, the long-run variance is defined as
\begin{equation*} 
    \zeta^{\bm{\mu}} := \lim\limits_{T \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{T} \mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mu}} \Big[ \sum\limits_{t=0}^{T-1}(r(s_t,\bm{a}_t) - \eta^{\bm{\mu}} )^2   \Big]. 
\end{equation*}
In this paper, we focus on MV-TSGs, aiming to optimize a combined mean-variance metric
\begin{align}\label{eq:mean-variance}
     J^{\bm{\mu}} &:= \lim\limits_{T \rightarrow \infty} \frac{1}{T} \mathbb{E}_{\bm{\mu}} \Big\{ \sum\limits_{t=0}^{T-1} [r(s_t,\bm{a}_t) - \beta(r(s_t,\bm{a}_t) - \eta^{\bm{\mu}} )^2]   \Big\} \\
     &=  \eta^{\bm{\mu}} - \beta \zeta^{\bm{\mu}}, \notag
\end{align}
where $\beta \ge 0$ is the parameter for the trade-off between mean and variance. In the context of operations, we denote $J^{\bm\mu}$ and $\eta^{\bm\mu}$ as $J(\bm\mu)$ and $\eta(\bm\mu)$ for clarity, if necessary.

