\section{Mean-Variance Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning}\label{sec:MV-MARL}

Algorithm~\ref{alg:MVMAPI} can solve the MV-TSG problem with small scales when the environmental model is exactly known. However, as the number of agents increases, the computation and storage resources required become extremely large because the size of the joint state space increases exponentially with the number of agents. In this section, we follow the algorithm paradigm introduced by \cite{zhong2024heterogeneous} and propose a mean-variance MARL algorithm based on Algorithm~\ref{alg:MVMAPI} to tackle MV-TSGs in a data-driven framework. When environmental parameters, such as the transition and reward functions, are unknown, agents iteratively update their policies by interacting with the environment. Moreover, the policy and value functions are parameterized by neural networks, enabling the MARL algorithm to handle MV-TSGs with larger state spaces.
We note that \cite{zhong2024heterogeneous} focus exclusively on discounted TSGs, which are largely different from the  MV-TSGs in our work.
% The policy function of each agent and the value function are parameterized by neural networks, respectively, which makes the MARL algorithm well-suited for scenarios with many agents.


\subsection{Sequential Update Scheme in Centralized Training}

In Algorithm~\ref{alg:MVMAPI}, each iteration of the inner loop (or sequential update) involves $N$ policy evaluations, each of which corresponds to an independent sampling and estimation process. Given the importance of sample efficiency in MARL algorithms, our MARL algorithm follows the CTDE framework. In the off-line centralized training phase, agents' policies are sequentially updated following a specified order. These updates rely on trajectory data collected under a joint policy $\bm\mu$ during on-line decentralized execution. Additionally, each agent can access and consider all previous agents' updates when updating its policy.
% During the centralized training phase, the policies of agents are updated sequentially in a specified order using trajectory data collected under a joint policy $\bm\mu$ during decentralized execution. 

In order to describe the centralized training process with the sequential update scheme and to develop our MARL algorithm, we first introduce the following definitions. 

\begin{definition}
     For a subset of agents $i_{1:h}$ and its complement subset $-i_{1:h}$, we define the \textbf{multi-agent state-action value function for MV-TSGs} as
    \begin{equation*}
        Q_{f,i_{1:h}}^{\bm{\mu}}(s,\bm{a}_{i_{1:h}}):=\mathbb{E}_{\bm{a}_{-i_{1:h}}\sim \bm{\mu}_{-i_{1:h}}}\left[Q_{f}^{\bm{\mu}}(s,\bm{a}_{i_{1:h}},\bm{a}_{-i_{1:h}})\right]. \notag
    \end{equation*} 
    When $h=0$, let $Q_{f,i_{1:0}}^{{\bm{\mu}}}(s,\bm{a}_{i_{1:0}}) = V_{f}^{\bm{\mu}}(s)$. For $h=N$, $Q_{f,i_{1:N}}^{\bm{\mu}}(s,\bm{a}_{i_{1:N}}) = Q_{f}^{\bm{\mu}}(s,\bm{a})$, $\bm{a}=\bm{a}_{i_{1:N}}$.

    Furthermore, for disjoint sets $j_{1:k}$ and $i_{1:h}$, the \textbf{multi-agent advantage function} is
    \begin{equation*}
        A_{f,i_{1:h}}^{\bm{\mu}}(s,\bm{a}_{j_{1:k}},\bm{a}_{i_{1:h}}):= Q_{f,j_{1:k},i_{1:h}}^{\bm{\mu}}(s,\bm{a}_{j_{1:k}},\bm{a}_{i_{1:h}})-Q_{f,j_{1:k}}^{\bm{\mu}}(s,\bm{a}_{j_{1:k}}). \notag
    \end{equation*}
    \label{def:ma-QA function}
\end{definition}
Note that these definitions differ from the variables specified in Algorithm~\ref{alg:MVMAPI}, as they are associated with actions of a set of agents and one common joint policy $\bm\mu$. The state-action value function $Q_{f,i_{1:h}}^{\bm{\mu}}(s,\bm{a}_{i_{1:h}})$ evaluates the value of agents $i_{1:h}$ taking actions $\bm{a}_{i_{1:h}}$ in state $s$ while other agents follow the joint policy $\bm\mu_{-i_{1:h}}$. The advantage function $A_{f,i_{1:h}}^{\bm{\mu}}(s,\bm{a}_{j_{1:k}},\bm{a}_{i_{1:h}})$ evaluates the advantage of agents $i_{1:h}$ taking actions $\bm{a}_{i_{1:h}}$ in state $s$ given that agents $j_{1:k}$ have taken actions $\bm{a}_{j_{1:k}}$ and the rest of agents follow the joint policy $\bm\mu_{-(j_{1:k}\cup i_{1:h} )}$.

With these definitions, we can directly derive the multi-agent advantage decomposition lemma for MV-TSGs.

\begin{lemma}[Multi-Agent Advantage Decomposition for MV-TSGs]\label{lemma:advantage decomposition}
    For any state $s \in \mathcal{S}$, subset of agents $i_{1:h} \subseteq \mathcal{N}$, and joint action $\bm{a}_{i_{1:h}}$, the following identity holds
    \begin{equation*}
        A_{f,i_{1:h}}^{\bm\mu}\big(s,\bm{a}_{i_{1:h}}\big)=\sum_{j=1}^h A_{f,i_j}^{\bm\mu}\big(s,\bm{a}_{i_{1:j-1}},a_{i_j}\big). \notag
    \end{equation*}
\end{lemma}

The proof of Lemma~\ref{lemma:advantage decomposition} is based on Definition~\ref{def:ma-QA function} and is presented in Appendix~\ref{sec:apped_lemma4}. 
With Lemma~\ref{lemma:advantage decomposition}, we can rewrite the first term of the r.h.s in Equation~(\ref{eq:MVPDF-2}) as $\mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{\bar{\bm{\mu}}}, {\bm{a}} \sim \bar{\bm{\mu}}}[A_{f}^{\bm{\mu}}(s,{\bm{a}})]=\mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{\bar{\bm{\mu}}}, \bm{a}\sim \bar{\bm\mu}}[\sum_{j=1}^N A_{f,i_j}^{\bm\mu}\big(s,\bm{a}_{i_{1:j-1}},a_{i_j}\big)]$. Then, we can obtain an effective approach to achieve performance improvement in the centralized training process when using the sequential update scheme. Specifically, for each state $s$, let agents take actions sequentially by following an arbitrary order $i_{1:N}$, assuming agent $i_1$ takes action $\Bar{a}_{i_1}$ such that $A_{f,i_1}^{\bm\mu} (s, \Bar{a}_{i_1}) \ge 0$, and then, for the rest $h = 2, \cdots, N$, each agent $i_h$ takes action $ \bar{a}_{i_h}$ such that $A_{f,i_h}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bar{\bm{a}}_{i_{1:h-1}}, \bar{a}_{i_h}) \ge 0$. For the induced joint action $\bar{\bm{a}}_{i_{1:N}}$, Lemma~\ref{lemma:advantage decomposition} ensures that $A_{f,i_{1:N}}^{\bm\mu}(s, \bar{\bm{a}}_{i_{1:N}})$ is non-negative and any $A_{f,i_h}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bar{\bm{a}}_{i_{1:h-1}}, \bar{a}_{i_h}) > 0$ can make $A_{f,i_{1:N}}^{\bm\mu}(s, \bar{\bm{a}}_{i_{1:N}})>0$, thereby guaranteeing the MV-TSG performance is improved.
Consequently, in the training phase, the policy of agent $i_h$ can be updated by 
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:max_A}\mathop{\arg\max}_{a_{i_h}\in \mathcal{A}_{i_h}}A_{f,i_h}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bar{\bm{a}}_{i_{1:h-1}}, a_{i_h}), \quad \forall s \in \mathcal{S}.
\end{equation}

The remaining key step is to estimate the optimization objective $A_{f,i_h}^{\bm\mu}$ for each agent $i_h$ with the trajectory data collected under the joint policy $\bm{\mu}$. We provide the following proposition.

\begin{proposition}
    Let $\bm\mu = (\mu_1,\cdots,\mu_N)$ be a joint policy, and $A_{f}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a})$ be its advantage function. Let $\Bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}} = (\Bar{\mu}_{i_1},\cdots, \Bar{\mu}_{i_{h-1}})$ be some other joint policy of agents $i_{1:h-1}$, and $\hat{\mu}_{i_h}$ be some other policy of agent $i_h$. Then, for every state $s$,
    \begin{align}
        \mathbb{E}_{\bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}} \sim \Bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}, a_{i_h} \sim \hat{\mu}_{i_h}} &[A_{f,i_h}^{\bm\mu}(s, \bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}},a_{i_h})] \notag\\
        &= \mathbb{E}_{\bm{a}\sim \bm\mu} \Big[
        (\frac{\hat{\mu}_{i_h} (a_{i_h}|s)}{\mu_{i_h} (a_{i_h}|s )}-1)  \frac{\bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}(\bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}}|s)}{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}(\bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}}|s)} A_{f}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a})  \Big]. \notag
    \end{align}
    \label{prop:A_estimator}
\end{proposition}
The proof is presented in Appendix~\ref{sec:apped_prop2}. Proposition~\ref{prop:A_estimator} indicates that  given an estimator $\hat{A}_{f}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a})$ of the advantage function $A_{f}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a})$, we can estimate $\mathbb{E}_{\bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}} \sim \Bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}, a_{i_h} \sim \hat{\mu}_{i_h}} [A_{f,i_h}^{\bm\mu}(s, \bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}},a_{i_h})]$ with an estimator of 
\begin{equation*}
  \mathbb{E}_{{\bm{a}}\sim \bm\mu}[(\frac{\hat{\mu}_{i_h}\left(a_{i_h}|s \right)}{\mu_{i_h}\left(a_{i_h}|s \right)}-1 )M_{f,i_{1:h}}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a})], \quad \text{where} \quad M_{f,i_{1:h}}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a})=\frac{\bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}(\bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}}|s)}{\bm{\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}(\bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}}|s)} \hat{A}_{f}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a}). \notag
\end{equation*}

These definitions and results lay the foundation for centralized training with the sequential update. 

\subsection{Multi-Agent Trust Region Policy Optimization for MV-TSGs}
In the approximate setting, due to the estimation and approximation error, it is unavoidable that given a joint policy $\bm\mu$, the advantage function is negative for some agent $i_h$ and state $s$, i.e., $A_{f,i_h}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bar{\bm{a}}_{i_{1:h-1}}, a_{i_h}) < 0, \forall a_{i_h}\in\mathcal{A}_{i_h}$. Moreover, in the first term of the r.h.s of Equation~(\ref{eq:MVPDF-2}), i.e., $\mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{\bar{\bm{\mu}}}, {\bm{a}} \sim \bar{\bm{\mu}}}[A_{f}^{\bm{\mu}}(s,{\bm{a}})]=\mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{\bar{\bm{\mu}}},\bm{a}\sim \bar{\bm\mu}}[\sum_{j=1}^N A_{f,i_j}^{\bm\mu}\big(s,\bm{a}_{i_{1:j-1}},a_{i_j}\big)]$, $\pi^{\bar{\bm\mu}}$ depends on the next joint policy $\bar{\bm\mu}$. These observations make it difficult to optimize policies directly by Equation~(\ref{eq:max_A}). 

To address these issues, we extend the idea of trust region methods \citep{schulman2015trust,zhang2021policy} to the sequential update scheme during training and propose the MV-MATRPO algorithm. For the policy update of each agent, our goal is to maximize a surrogate objective within a local trust region. 

Before introducing the surrogate objective function for each agent and presenting the MV-MATRPO algorithm, we first present the average-reward trust region lemma in TSGs, derived directly from the results in \cite{zhang2021policy}. We then present a key theorem for MV-TSGs that builds upon this lemma.

\begin{lemma}
    \label{lemma:trpo_joint}
        Let $\bm\mu$ be the current joint policy and $\bar{\bm\mu}$ be any other joint policy. We define $\mathcal{L}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu}) = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{\bm\mu}, \bm{a} \sim {\bar{\bm\mu}}} [A^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a})]$, where $A^{\bm\mu}$ is the average-reward advantage function.
        With the ergodicity assumption, the following bounds hold:
    \begin{equation*} 
        \eta(\bar{\bm\mu})-\eta(\bm\mu) \ge \mathcal{L}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu}) - 2(\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_{\eta}D_{\emph{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu},\bm\mu), 
    \end{equation*}
    where $\epsilon_\eta=\max\limits_s |\mathbb{E}_{\bm{a}\sim \bar{\bm\mu}}[A^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a})] |$,  $\kappa^*=\max\limits_{\bm\mu}\kappa^{\bm\mu}$, $\kappa^{\bm\mu}$ is the Kemeny's constant \citep{kemeny1969finite} for a given $\bm\mu$, $D_{\emph{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu},\bm\mu)= \mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{\bm\mu}} D_{\emph{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu}(\cdot | s) \| \bm\mu(\cdot |s))$ is the total variation divergence, which is a measure of distance between two distributions.
    \end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}
    Let $\bm\mu$ be the current joint policy and $\bar{\bm\mu}$ be any other joint policy. We define the surrogate objective function $\mathcal{L}_{f}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu}) = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{\bm\mu}, \bm{a} \sim {\bar{\bm\mu}}} [A_{f}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a})]$. The following bounds hold in MV-TSGs:
    \begin{equation}
        J(\bar{\bm\mu}) - J(\bm\mu) 
        \ge \mathcal{L}_{f}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu}) - 2(\kappa^* - 1) \epsilon_f D_{\emph{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu},\bm\mu) + \beta H^2, 
        \label{eq:mv_bound}
    \end{equation}    
    where $\epsilon_{f}=\max\limits_s |\mathbb{E}_{\bm{a} \sim \bar{\bm\mu}}[A_{f}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a})]|$, $H =\max(0, \mathcal{L}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu})-2(\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_{\eta}D_{\emph{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu},\bm\mu), -\mathcal{L}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu})- 2(\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_{\eta}D_{\emph{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu},\bm\mu))$, $\mathcal{L}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu}), \epsilon_{\eta}, \kappa^*$ as defined in Lemma~\ref{lemma:trpo_joint}.
    \label{threm:mv_bounds}    
\end{theorem}
\proof{}
We start our analysis from the mean-variance performance difference formula of Equation (\ref{eq:MVPDF-2})
\begin{equation*}
    J^{\bar{\bm{\mu}}} - J^{\bm{\mu}} 
        =\mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{\bar{\bm{\mu}}}, \bm{a} \sim \bar{\bm{\mu}}}[A_{f}^{\bm{\mu}}(s,\bm{a})] + \beta (\eta^{\bar{\bm\mu}} -\eta^{\bm\mu})^2.
\end{equation*}

The result indicates that the performance difference can be decomposed into two terms. The first term, associated with the mean-variance reward function $f$, can be addressed using the standard average trust region method. With Lemma~\ref{lemma:trpo_joint}, we have
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:mv_bounds_term1}
    \mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{\bar{\bm\mu}}, \bm{a}\sim \bar{\bm\mu}}[A_{f}^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a})] - \mathcal{L}_{f}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu}) 
    \ge - 2(\kappa^* - 1) \epsilon_f D_{\text{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu},\bm\mu) 
    % &\ge - 2(\kappa^* - 1) \epsilon_f \epsilon.  \notag
    % \label{eq:bounds_Af}
\end{equation}

To bound the second term, we want to find a quantity $H\ge0$ such that 
\begin{equation*}
    (\eta^{\bar{\bm\mu}}-\eta^{\bm\mu})^2 \ge H^2. \notag
\end{equation*}
The square term can be lower bound by 0, or by the square of a lower bound of its argument if the latter is greater than 0. Due to its convexity, the square function can have a lower bound that is greater than 0 in two cases: when an upper bound of its argument is lower than 0, or when a lower bound of its argument is larger than 0.

\cite{zhang2021policy} demonstrate that the bounds of the average reward trust region method as
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{L}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu})-2(\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_{\eta}D_{\text{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu},\bm\mu) \le \eta^{\bar{\bm\mu}}-\eta^{\bm\mu} \le \mathcal{L}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu})+ 2(\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_{\eta}D_{\text{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu},\bm\mu),
\end{equation*}
where $\mathcal{L}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu})=\mathbb{E}_{s\sim \pi^{\bm{\mu}},\bm{a}\sim \bar{\bm\mu}}[A^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a})]$ and $\epsilon_{\eta}= \max\limits_s \mathbb{E}_{\bm{a}\sim \bar{\bm\mu}}[A^{\bm\mu}(s,\bm{a})]$ (see Lemma~\ref{lemma:trpo_joint}).

We then obtain the best lower bound by taking the maximum among the argument lower bound, the opposite of the argument upper bound and 0, i.e., $H=\max(0, \mathcal{L}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu})-2(\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_{\eta}D_{\text{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu},\bm\mu), -\mathcal{L}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu})- 2(\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_{\eta}D_{\text{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu},\bm\mu))$, finally taking the square of this quantity. Then, we arrive at
\begin{equation*}
    J^{\bar{\bm\mu}} - J^{\bm\mu} \ge \mathcal{L}_{f}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu}) - 2(\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_f D_{\text{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu},\bm\mu) + \beta H^2,
\end{equation*}
where $H =\max(0, \mathcal{L}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu})-2(\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_{\eta}D_{\text{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu},\bm\mu), -\mathcal{L}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu})- 2(\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_{\eta}D_{\text{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu},\bm\mu))$.
\endproof

Theorem~\ref{threm:mv_bounds} suggests that as the trust region tightens, i.e., $\epsilon_f \to 0$, the sign of performance difference can be determined by the first-order term $\mathcal{L}_{f}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu})$. This provides the theoretical foundation for applying trust region methods to long-run mean-variance optimization problems.

Next, inspired by Lemma~\ref{lemma:advantage decomposition}, we want to express the r.h.s of Equation~(\ref{eq:mv_bound}) as the sum of individual objectives for all agents. Consequently, we define the following surrogate objective for each agent.

\begin{definition}
    Let $\bm\mu$ be a joint policy, $\Bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}= (\bar\mu_{i_1},\cdots,\bar\mu_{i_{h-1}})$ be some other joint policy of agents $i_{1:h-1}$, and $\hat{\mu}_{i_h}$ be some other policy of agent $i_h$. Then 
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{L}_{f,i_{1:h}}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}, \hat{\mu}_{i_h}) = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{\bm\mu}, \bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}}\sim \Bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}, a_{i_h} \sim \hat{\mu}_{i_h}} [A_{f,i_h}^{\bm\mu}(s, \bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}}, a_{i_h})]. 
    \end{equation*}
    \label{def:ma_surrogate}
\end{definition}
% Note that, for any $\Bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}$, we have
% \begin{align}
%     \mathcal{L}_{f,i_{1:h}}^{\bm\mu}(\Bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}, \mu_{i_h}) 
%     & = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{\bm\mu}, \bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}}\sim \Bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}, a_{i_h} \sim \mu_{i_h}} [A_{f,i_h}^{\bm\mu}(s, \bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}}, a_{i_h})] \notag\\
%     & = \mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{\bm\mu}, \bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}}\sim \Bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}}  \big[\mathbb{E}_{a_{i_h} \sim \mu_{i_h}} [A_{f,i_h}^{\bm\mu}(s, \bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}}, a_{i_h})]\big] = 0 .
%     \label{eq:ma_surrogate_2}  
% \end{align}

Combining Lemma~\ref{lemma:advantage decomposition}, Theorem~\ref{threm:mv_bounds}, and Definition~\ref{def:ma_surrogate}, we replace the TV divergence with KL divergence as most trust region methods do, and derive the bound for the joint policy update as follows.

\begin{theorem}
    Let $\bm\mu$ be a joint policy. Then, for any joint policy $\Bar{\bm\mu}$, we have
    \begin{equation*}
        J(\Bar{\bm\mu})\ge J(\bm\mu) + \sum\limits_{h=1}^N [\mathcal{L}_{f,i_{1:h}}^{\bm\mu}(\Bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}, \bar\mu_{i_h}) - W(\bar{\mu}_{i_h}, \mu_{i_h})]. \notag
    \end{equation*}
    where $W(\bar{\mu}_{i_h}, \mu_{i_h}) = (\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_f  \sqrt{2\mathbb{E}_{s\sim \pi^{\bm\mu}} D_{\emph{\text{KL}}}(\bar{\mu}_{i_h}(\cdot|s)\|\mu_{i_h}(\cdot|s)) }$.
    \label{threm:ma_bounds}
\end{theorem}
\proof{}
    We start this proof from Theorem~\ref{threm:mv_bounds}. Since the variable $H$ in Theorem~\ref{threm:mv_bounds} is difficult to consider and is always positive, we can bound the mean-variance performance by neglecting it. In this case, together with Equation (\ref{eq:mv_bounds_term1}), we have
    \begin{equation}
        J^{\bar{\bm\mu}} - J^{\bm\mu} \ge \mathcal{L}_{f}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu}) - 2(\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_f [ \mathbb{E}_{s\sim \pi^{\bm\mu}} D_{\text{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu}(\cdot|s) \| \bm\mu(\cdot|s))] .
        \label{eq:mv_bounds_bref}
    \end{equation}

The bound in Equation (\ref{eq:mv_bounds_bref}) is given in terms of the TV divergence; however the KL divergence is more commonly used in practice. The relationship between the TV divergence and KL divergence is given by Pinsker’s inequality \citep{Tsybakov2009introduction}, which demonstrates that for any two distributions $p$ and $q$: $D_{\text{TV}}(p\|q)\le \sqrt{D_{\text{KL}}(p\|q)/2}$. We can then show that 
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}_{s\sim \pi^{\bm\mu}}[D_{\text{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu}(\cdot|s) \| \bm\mu(\cdot|s))] 
    & \le \mathbb{E}_{s\sim \pi^{\bm\mu}} \Big[\sqrt{D_{\text{KL}}(\bar{\bm\mu}(\cdot|s) \| \bm\mu(\cdot|s))/2} \Big] \\
    & \le \sqrt{\mathbb{E}_{s\sim \pi^{\bm\mu}}[D_{\text{KL}}(\bar{\bm\mu}(\cdot|s) \| \bm\mu(\cdot|s))]/2} ,
\end{align*}
where the second inequality comes from Jensen’s inequality. Substituting the result into Equation (\ref{eq:mv_bounds_bref}) and giving an ordered subset $i_{1:N}$, we have
\begin{align*}
       J^{\bar{\bm\mu}} - J^{\bm\mu} 
       &\ge \mathcal{L}_{f}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu}) - 2(\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_f [ \mathbb{E}_{s\sim \pi^{\bm\mu}} D_{\text{TV}}(\bar{\bm\mu}(\cdot|s) \| \bm\mu(\cdot|s))] \\
       &\ge \mathcal{L}_{f}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu}) - (\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_f 
       \sqrt{2\mathbb{E}_{s\sim \pi^{\bm\mu}}[D_{\text{KL}}(\bar{\bm\mu}(\cdot|s) \| \bm\mu(\cdot|s))]} \\
       & \ge \mathcal{L}_{f}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu}) - (\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_f 
       \sqrt{2\mathbb{E}_{s\sim \pi^{\bm\mu}} \Big[ \sum\limits_{i=1}^N D_{\text{KL}}(\bar{\bm\mu}(\cdot|s) \| \bm\mu(\cdot|s))} \Big] \\
       & \ge \mathcal{L}_{f}^{\bm\mu}(\bar{\bm\mu}) - (\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_f
       \sum\limits_{i=1}^N \Big[ \sqrt{2\mathbb{E}_{s\sim \pi^{\bm\mu}} D_{\text{KL}}(\bar{\bm\mu}(\cdot|s) \| \bm\mu(\cdot|s)) } \Big] \\
       & = \sum\limits_{h=1}^N \Big\{  \mathcal{L}^{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h}}(\bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}},\bar{\mu}_{i_h}) - (\kappa^*-1)\epsilon_f  \sqrt{2\mathbb{E}_{s\sim \pi^{\bm\mu}} D_{\text{KL}}(\bar{\mu}_{i_h}(\cdot|s) \| \mu_{i_h}(\cdot|s)) }  \Big\} .    
\end{align*}
The third inequality follows the Lemma 8 in \cite{kuba2022trust}, the fourth inequality follows the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality and the last equality follows the results of Definition~\ref{def:ma_surrogate}, and Lemma~\ref{lemma:advantage decomposition}. Then, we complete this proof.
\endproof

Theorem~\ref{threm:ma_bounds} provides theoretical guarantees for the multi-agent trust region method based on the sequential update mechanism for MV-TSGs. Namely, if agents sequentially update their policies by
\begin{equation}
    \mathop{\arg\max}_{\bar{\mu}_{i_h}} [\mathcal{L}_{f,i_{1:h}}^{\bm\mu}(\Bar{\bm\mu}_{i_{1:h-1}}, \bar\mu_{i_h}) - W(\Bar{\mu}_{i_h}, \mu_{i_h})],
    \label{eq:mv_improvement}
\end{equation}
the joint policy is guaranteed to be improved, or at least remain non-decreasing by retaining the
current policy, i.e.,  $\bar{\mu}_{i_h} = \mu_{i_h}$.


\subsection{Implementation with Neural Network Parameterization}\label{sec:implementation details} 
To implement the policy update process in practical settings, for the ($k+1$)th iteration of the sequential update, the policy $\mu_i^{(k)}$ of each agent $i$ and the value function $V_{f}^{\bm\mu^{(k)}}$ are parameterized by neural networks with parameters $\theta^{(k)}_i$ (actor networks) and $\phi^{(k)}$ (critic networks), respectively.  To ensure the algorithm is computationally tractable, the unconstrained optimization problem (\ref{eq:mv_improvement}) is reformulated as a constrained problem. Let  $\mu^{\theta_{i}^{(k)}}$ denote $\mu_i^{(k)}$, $V_f^{\phi^{(k)}}$ denote $V_{f}^{\bm\mu^{(k)}}$ and $\theta^{(k)}=(\theta_1^{(k)},\cdots,\theta_N^{(k)})$. With Theorem~\ref{threm:ma_bounds},  given a permutation of agents $i_{1:N}$, agent $i_{h \in \{1,\cdots,N \} }$ can sequentially optimize its policy parameter $\theta_{i_h}^{(k+1)}$ by maximizing the constrained objective:
\begin{align}
    \theta_{i_h}^{(k+1)} = \mathop{\arg\max}\limits_{\theta_{i_h}} \mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{{\theta^{(k)}}}, \bm{a}_{i_{1:h-1}}\sim \bm\mu^{\theta_{i_{1:h-1}}^{(k+1)}}, a_{i_h} \sim \mu^{\theta_{i_h}}} [A_{f,i_h}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}(s,\bm{a}_{1:h-1}, a_{i_h})], \notag \\
    \text{s.t.} \quad \mathbb{E}_{s \sim \pi^{{\theta^{(k)}}}}[D_\text{KL}(\mu^{\theta^{(k)}_{i_h}}(\cdot|s), \mu^{\theta_{i_h}}(\cdot|s))] \le \epsilon,
    \label{eq:trust_indiv}
\end{align}
where $\epsilon$ is the threshold hyperparameter.

We use the method proposed by \cite{zhong2024heterogeneous} to solve the above optimization problem. More details are provided in Appendix~\ref{sec:appd_implementation details}.
With Proposition~\ref{prop:A_estimator}, the objective functions for each agent can be estimated with no bias based on the advantage function $\hat{A}_{f}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}(s,\bm{a})$,
which can be estimated using the generalized advantage estimation \citep{schulman2016high}. Typically, we have
\begin{equation}
    \hat{A}_{f}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}(s_n,\bm{a}_n)=\sum\limits_{t=n}^{T-1}\lambda_{t-n}(f^{\theta^{(k)}}(s_t,\bm{a}_t) - \hat{J}^{{{\theta^{(k)}}}}+V_f^{\phi^{(k)}}(s_t) - V_f^{\phi^{(k)}}(s_{t+1})),
    \label{eq:compute_advantage}
\end{equation}
where $\lambda$ is the hyper-parameter to trade-off bias and variance, $T$ is the length of trajectories. With trajectory data, we can directly calculate the estimates of the long-run average reward and variance, denoted by $\hat{\eta}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}$ and $\hat{\zeta}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}$, respectively. The mean-variance performance function is estimated as $\hat{J}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}=\hat{\eta}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}-\beta\hat{\zeta}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}$. Subsequently, $\hat{A}_{f}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}$ can be obtained by Equation~(\ref{eq:compute_advantage}).

% \newpage



Since we consider the long-run average performance in this paper, we adopt the average value constraint (AVC) proposed by \cite{maaverage} to assist in estimating the target value function $\hat{V}_f^{\phi^{(k)}}$ and stabilizing the value learning. The value function network is updated using the loss function
\begin{equation*}
     \frac{1}{BT}\sum\limits_{b=1}^B\sum\limits_{t=0}^T \big( V_f^{\phi}(s_t) - \hat{V}_f^{\phi^{(k)}}(s_t)  \big)^2, \notag
\end{equation*}
where $B$ is the number of trajectories collected. The detailed pseudo code of MV-MATRPO is presented in Algorithm~\ref{alg:MVTRPO}.

\begin{algorithm}[H]
    \label{alg:MVTRPO}
    \caption{Mean-variance multi-agent trust region policy optimization with parameterization}
    \SetAlgoLined
    \setstretch{0.9}

    \textbf{Input:} Stepsize $\alpha$, number of agents $N$, iterations $K$, episode length $T$.

    \textbf{Initialize:} Policy function (actor) networks $\{\theta^{(0)}_i, \forall i \in \mathcal{N} \}$, value function (critic) network $\{\phi^{(0)} \}$, replay buffer $\mathcal{B}$. Set $\hat{\eta}=0, \hat{\zeta}=0, \hat{J}=0$.

    \For{$k=0,\cdots,K-1$}
    {

        Collect a set of trajectories by running the joint policy $\bm\mu^{\theta^{(k)}} = (\mu^{\theta^{(k)}_1}, \cdots, \mu^{\theta^{(k)}_N})$.

        Push transitions $\{(s_t,\{a_{i,t} \}_{i=0}^N,s_{t+1},r_t), t \in \{0,1,\cdots,T-1\}$ into $\mathcal{B}$.

        Update $\hat{\eta} \gets (1-\alpha)\hat{\eta}+\alpha\frac{1}{BT} \sum\limits_{b=1}^B \sum\limits_{t=0}^{T-1}r_t $.

        Update $\hat{\zeta} \gets (1-\alpha)\hat{\zeta}+\alpha\frac{1}{BT} \sum\limits_{b=1}^B \sum\limits_{t=0}^{T-1}(r_t-\hat{\eta})^2 $. 

        Compute the average mean-variance performance function $\hat{J}$.   

        Compute $f^{\theta^{(k)}}(s_t, \bm{a}_t)$ and $\hat{A}_f^{{\theta^{(k)}}}(s_t, \bm{a}_t)$, respectively, at all time steps.

        Compute $\hat{V}_f^{\phi^{(k)}}(s_t)$ for all time steps using AVC.

        Draw a random permutation of agents $i_{1:N}$.

        Set $M_{f,i_1}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}(s,\bm{a}) = \hat{A}_{f}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}(s,\bm{a}), \forall s \in \mathcal{S}, \forall \bm{a}\in \mathcal{A}$.

        \For{agent $i_h=i_1,\cdots,i_N$}
        {
        Solving the optimization problem (\ref{eq:trust_indiv}) and get the policy $\mu^{\theta^{(k+1)}_{i_h}}$.    

            \If{$i_h \neq i_N$}
            {Compute $M_{f,i_{1:h+1}}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}(s,\bm{a})= \frac{\mu^{\theta^{(k+1)}_{i_h}}\left(a_{i_h}|s\right)}{\mu^{\theta^{(k)}_{i_h}}\left(a_{i_h}|s\right)} M_{f,i_{1:h}}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}(s,\bm{a}), \forall s \in \mathcal{S}, \forall \bm{a}\in \mathcal{A} $.
            }
        }
        Update the critic network by following the formula:
        \begin{equation*}
            \phi_{k+1} = \mathop{\arg\min}_{\phi} \frac{1}{BT}\sum\limits_{b=1}^B\sum\limits_{t=0}^T \big( V_f^{\phi}(s_t) - \hat{V}_f^{\phi^{(k)}}(s_t)  \big)^2.
        \end{equation*}       
    }

\end{algorithm}

Moreover, in scenarios with larger state and action spaces, we can follow the route of the PPO algorithm to mitigate the computational burden. The problem listed in Equation (\ref{eq:trust_indiv}) can be solved by focusing solely on the first-order derivative and sequentially optimizing the policy parameter $\theta^{(k+1)}_{i_h}$ through maximizing the clipping objective of 
\begin{equation}
\mathbb{E}_{s\sim \pi^{{\theta^{(k)}}},\bm{a} \sim \bm{\mu}^{\theta^{(k)}}}\left[\min
\left(\frac{\mu^{\theta_{i_h}}\left(a_{i_h}|s\right)}{\mu^{\theta^{(k)}_{i_h}}\left(a_{i_h}|s\right)}M_{f,i_{1:h}}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}\left(s,\bm{a}\right),\text{clip}\left(\frac{\mu^{\theta_{i_h}}\left(a_{i_h}|s\right)}{\mu^{\theta^{(k)}_{i_h}}\left(a_{i_h}|s\right)},1\pm\epsilon\right)M_{f,i_{1:h}}^{{\theta^{(k)}}}\left(s,\bm{a}\right)\right)\right], \notag
\end{equation}
where the \textit{clip} function clips the first argument by the lower and upper bounds denoted by the second and third arguments, respectively. 


\section{Application Examples for Energy Management}\label{sec:exp_results}
In this section, we use the energy management problem of MMSs as an example to demonstrate the effectiveness of our algorithms. We have significantly simplified the parameter settings and engineering constraints, ensuring the key idea of this example is concise and easy to follow.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=12cm]{networked-grids.eps}
    \caption{Architecture of a grid-connected multiple microgrids system.}\label{fig:networked microgrids}
    \end{figure}
    \vspace{-3mm}

We consider a grid-connected MMS comprising $N$ microgrids, illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:networked microgrids}. Each microgrid may be equipped with a renewable energy generator, demand load unit, and controllable storage unit. The renewable energy generator, such as the wind turbine or a solar panel, generates power by harvesting energy from renewable resources. The demand load unit represents participants consuming power. The controllable storage unit is the key equipment for energy management, aiding in power shift across various time steps. Consequently, microgrids with mentioned facilities can both generate and consume power. 



In MMSs, microgrids can coordinate to regulate the mean-variance metric of the exchanged power between the MMS and the main grid. Positive exchanged power means that the MMS sells the power to the main grid and gets profits, while negative exchanged power indicates that it buys power and incurs costs. The long-run variance reflects the volatility of exchanged power, which indicates power supply stability. We assume that abandoning generated power is allowed if necessary, and each microgrid $i$ in the MMS has an energy management policy $\mu_i$ for its facilities, including renewable energy generators and storage units. The energy management policies of agents correspond to a time scale of hours. All the continuous variables  are discretized properly.

For each microgrid $i$ at time step $t=1,2,\cdots$, we denote the power generated by renewable energy generators by $G_{i,t}$, the power of demand loads by $L_{i,t}$ and the energy level of storage units by $E_{i,t}$. Since the power generated by renewable energy generators and consumed by demand load units depend on various random factors such as climate conditions, $G_{i,t}$ and $L_{i,t}$ are non-negative random variables. We assume that $G_{i,t}$ and $L_{i,t}$ follow a stationary stochastic process, and their dynamics are modeled using Markov chains.
% The transition probability matrix of $G_{i,t}$ and $L_{i,t}$ can be estimated from statistics \citep{xia2020risk,yang2020fluctuation}. 
Subsequently, the system state is defined as $s_t=(G_{1,t},L_{1,t},E_{1,t},\cdots,G_{N,t},L_{N,t},E_{N,t})$. Because the power abandonment is allowed for each microgrid, the action for microgrid $i$ is denoted as $(c_{i,t},v_{i,t}) $, where $c_{i,t}$ denotes the discharging power of the battery at time $t$ ($c_{i,t}<0$ means the charging power), $v_{i,t}$ denotes the power abandoned at time $t$ and $0\le v_{i,t} \le G_{i,t}$. Variable $c_{i,t}$ must satisfy the following constrains: (1) $ C^{\text{min}}_i \le c_{i,t} \le C^{\text{max}}_i$, where $C^{\text{min}}_i$ and $C^{\text{max}}_i$ represent the maximum charging and discharging power, respectively. (2)  $E_{i,t} - E^{\text{max}}_i\le c_{i,t} \le E_{i,t}$, where $E^{\text{max}}_i$ represents the maximum capacity of the storage unit of microgrid $i$. If $c_{i,t}$ is adopted, the energy level of the storage unit at the next time step is updated by $E_{i,t+1}=E_{i,t}-c_{i,t}$.

Although $c_{i,t}$ and $v_{i,t}$ are both decision variables, we can only use $a_{i,t}$ as the decision variable after using the following reasonable assumptions. If $0\le a_{i,t}\le \min\{C^{\text{max}}_i, E_{i,t} \}$, then we have $v_{i,t}=0$ and $c_{i,t}=a_{i,t}$, which means that the storage is discharging and the generated power should not be abandoned. If $\max\{C^{\text{min}}_i, E_{i,t} - E^{\text{max}}_i \} \le a_{i,t} < 0$, then we have $v_{i,t}=0$ and $c_{i,t}=a_{i,t}$, which means that the storage has potential charging capacity unused and the generated power should not be abandoned. If $ -G_{i,t} \le a_{i,t} < \max \{C^{\text{min}}_i, E_{i,t} - E^{\text{max}}_i \}$, then we have $c_{i,t} = \max \{C^{\text{min}}_i, E_{i,t} - E^{\text{max}}_i \}$ and $v_{i,t}=c_{i,t}-a_{i,t}$, which means that the storage is using full charging power capacity and some generated power is abandoned. In summary, the value domain of the decision variable $a_{i,t}$ is $-G_{i,t} \le a_{i,t} \le \min\{C^{\text{max}}_i, E_{i,t} \}$ and it has the rule as follows: if $\max\{C^{\text{min}}_i, E_{i,t} - E^{\text{max}}_i \} \le a_{i,t}\le \min\{C^{\text{max}}_i, E_{i,t} \}$, then $v_{i,t}=0$ and $c_{i,t}=a_{i,t}$; if $-G_{i,t} \le a_{i,t} < \max \{C^{\text{min}}_i, E_{i,t} - E^{\text{max}}_i \}$, then $c_{i,t} = \max \{C^{\text{min}}_i, E_{i,t} - E^{\text{max}}_i \}$ and $v_{i,t}=c_{i,t}-a_{i,t}$. Therefore, the optimization is focused solely on the variable $a_{i,t}$, while the variables $c_{i,t}$ and $v_{i,t}$ are determined according to the aforementioned rule. The control policy $\mu_i$ is a mapping from $s_t$ to $a_{i,t}$. The exchanged power between the MMS and the main grid is given by $r_t= \sum\limits_{i=1}^N (G_{i,t} -L_{i,t} + a_{i,t})$. The objective of the MMS is to establish optimal energy management policies for each microgrid to maximize the mean-variance of the exchanged power.


For simplicity, we assume that renewable energy generators, demand load units, and storage units among all microgrids share the same specifications and their stochastic characteristics are independent and identically distributed. Specifically, we focus on the wind turbine as a renewable energy generator. For each microgrid, the storage maximum capacity is $E^{\text{max}}=5$, and the maximum charging and discharging power are $C^{\text{min}}=-2$ and $C^{\text{max}}=2$. The states of wind power, demand load, and storage energy level are all divided into six states, respectively. The state details of these facilities are presented in Table~\ref{tab:state_para}.
