%Unlike traditional contrastive learning approaches such as SimCLR~\cite{DBLP:conf/icml/ChenK0H20}, which rely heavily on both positive and negative samples and require pairwise feature comparisons within large batches, SwAV reduces the need for explicit negative pairs and large batch sizes by learning consistent cluster assignments across multiple augmentations (views) of the same data item. By aligning the cluster mappings of these views, SwAV reduces the need for explicit negative samples and reduces the reliance on large batch sizes, lowering computational demands during training. In our framework, this property allows SwAV to produce attribute embeddings that preserve semantic consistency across variations while effectively separating attributes from different semantic domains in the embedding space.
%Figure \ref{fig:swav} (adapted from \cite{DBLP:conf/nips/CaronMMGBJ20}) provides a high-level view of SwAV. 
%Although SwAV can support multiple views, we only use 2 views in Figure \ref{fig:swav} and Algorithm \ref{alg:swav2}. The overall process of SwAV is:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textit{Data Augmentation.} Given a batch of table columns \( X = \{x_1, x_2, \dots, x_B\} \), where \( B \) is the batch size, we apply an operator \( OP \) iteratively to generate multiple transformed views for each column. Specifically, \( OP \) samples different proportions ($f$) of the cells in each column, either uniformly or based on their TF-IDF scores. This process is repeated \( \nu \) times, resulting in \( \nu \) batch-level views, each containing \( B \) transformed columns. Next, a serialization strategy ($Serial$) is applied to each transformed view, converting it into a specific serialized format. $Serial$ can be configured to include only column values or both the values and the headers. This process ultimately produces a series of semantics-preserving views \( \{X_1, X_2, \dots, X_\nu\} \).
%as depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:swav} and detailed in Line~\ref{line:aug} of Algorithm~\ref{alg:swav2}.



\item \textit{Model}. A pre-trained LLM $M$ maps batch views into embeddings (\( Z_1, Z_2 \)) with an output dimension \( d \). \( K \) trainable cluster centers (prototypes \( C \)) represented as a \( d \times K \) parameter matrix are maintained. During each iteration, \( M \) performs online clustering by assigning views to prototypes and updating the prototypes, enabling continuous refinement.

\item \textit{Online Cluster Assignment.} During the online clustering process, the assignment of views to prototypes is denoted as \( q \), and the framework computes the optimal mappings \( Q = \{q_1, q_2, \dots, q_N\} \) based on their embeddings. This process is formulated as an entropy-regularized optimal transport problem, which ensures smooth and balanced assignments, and is efficiently solved using the Sinkhorn-Knopp algorithm \cite{sinkhornNIPS13}. By enforcing an even distribution of views across prototypes, the method prevents imbalanced assignments and aligns features effectively with prototypes, enhancing clustering quality. 
%To address potential data imbalance, additional regularization strategies can be applied.
Prototypes are dynamically updated after each batch based on the assignment results, enabling continuous refinement and efficient online clustering of similar views.


\item \textit{Calculate Loss and Back Propagation.}
After assigning the views, the assignments $Q$ are used to compute the loss by predicting the assignment of one view from the features of another view of the same instance. This is known as a \textit{swapped prediction problem}. 
%See Appendix \ref{append:loss} for the loss function definition.
The framework then uses the loss to fine-tune the model $M$ by back propagation.  The detailed loss function definition, due to space constraints and, is provided in Appendix \ref{append:loss}.
\end{enumerate}

