\subsubsection{Metrics applied to the Top-level Types.}
Top-level types, as the most fundamental output of schema inference, include important meta information of the table set, and also influence the quality of the constructed taxonomy. 
We regard the top-level type inference as a problem of table clustering, and accordingly calculate the widely used metric
%
% We use the following two widely adopted metrics for clustering.\\
%\textit{Rand Index (RI).} We use Rand Index (RI) measures the similarity between clustering results and ground-truth clusters:
%\begin{equation}
$\text{Rand Index (RI)} = \frac{\text{TP} + \text{TN}}{\text{TP} + \text{FP} + \text{FN} + \text{TN}}$,
%\label{eqn:rand}
%\end{equation}
where TP (resp. TN) is the number of pairs of tables that belong to the same (resp. different) top-level type {in both the output taxonomy $\mathcal{H}_o$ and the GT taxonomy $\mathcal{H}_{gt}$}, and FP (resp. FN)  is the number of pairs of tables that belong to the same (resp. different) top-level type in $\mathcal{H}_o$ but different (resp. same) top-level types in $\mathcal{H}_{gt}$.
We also calculate Purity of each top-level type $t$ in $\mathcal{H}_o$ as the ratio of the tables whose top-level type in $\mathcal{H}_{gt}$
%\footnote{A table's top-level type in $\mathcal{H}_{gt}$ is inferred by its most specific entity type annotated.} 
is $m(t)$ (i.e., matched with $t$), among all the tables associated to $t$.
With the Purities of all the top-level types in $\mathcal{H}_o$, we average them as the final Purity of top-level types of $\mathcal{H}_{o}$.  


