\begin{abstract}
The collapse of reinforced autoclaved aerated concrete (RAAC) panels has attracted considerable public and academic interest. As detailed experimental data are not yet available and replicating the natural corrosion process requires years or decades, computational modelling is essential to understand under which conditions corrosion remains concealed. The very high porosity of RAAC is widely suspected to be a major contributing factor. However, current corrosion-induced cracking models are known to struggle with capturing the role of concrete porosity. To remedy this critical deficiency, we propose to enrich corrosion-induced cracking modelling with the analytical solution of reactive transport equations governing the precipitation of rust and a porosity-dependent description of diffusivity. With this, the corrosion concealment in RAAC panels is studied computationally for the first time, revealing that RAAC panels can suddenly collapse before any warning of corrosion-induced surface cracking and allowing to map the conditions most likely to result in sudden collapse.
%In this study, we investigate corrosion-induced cracking of reinforced autoclaved aerated concrete (RAAC) panels. Although being an adverse effect, corrosion-induced cracking provides a valuable warning of reinforcement corrosion, which is otherwise difficult to detect and can lead to sudden structural collapse. However, intrusive surveys on RAAC panels suggest that rebars in some panels can significantly corrode without any indication of cracking on the concrete surface. As detailed experimental data are not yet available and the replication of the natural corrosion process requires years or decades, computational modelling is essential to understand under which conditions corrosion remains concealed. The very high porosity of RAAC is widely suspected to be a major contributing factor. However, current corrosion-induced cracking models are well-known to struggle with capturing the role of concrete porosity, which is traditionally described by a simplified concept of fictitious porous zone. To remedy this critical deficiency, we propose to enrich the corrosion-induced cracking model with the analytical solution of reactive transport equations governing the precipitation of rust and a porosity-dependent description of diffusivity. With this modelling framework, the corrosion concealment in RAAC panels is studied computationally for the first time, revealing that RAAC panels can suddenly collapse before any warning of corrosion-induced surface cracking and allowing to map the conditions most likely to result in sudden collapse. 
\end{abstract}
