\subsection{Topic modeling using Latent Dirichlet Allocation (LDA)}
\label{Ch:Latent_Dirichlet_Allocation}
Topic modeling is a technique used to discover hidden themes or topics within a collection of texts~\cite{Kherwa2019_topicModelling}. Among various topic modeling approaches, LDA \cite{blei2003latent} has become one of the most widely used methods. LDA works by treating each document as a mixture of topics and each topic as a mixture of words. For example, in a collection of technology news articles, one article might be 70 percent about AI development and 30 percent about business implications, while another might be 60 percent about technical implementation and 40 percent about ethical concerns.
A critical step in applying LDA is determining the optimal number of topics. This is typically done by evaluating topic coherence scores across different numbers of topics. Topic coherence measures how semantically similar the words within each topic are, providing a quantitative way to assess topic interpretability~\cite{kapadiaEvaluateTopicModels2022}. 
% Higher coherence scores generally indicate more meaningful topics that humans can easily understand and label. 

% After training the LDA model, the high-dimensional topic distributions can be difficult to visualize and interpret directly. For this, t-Distributed Stochastic Neighbour Embedding (t-SNE), a dimensionality reduction technique specifically designed for visualization of high-dimensional data as clusters, can be used to understand this topic distribution from LDA~\cite{erdemburnpiroTSNEClearlyExplained2022}. It is important to note that the quality of t-SNE visualization is controlled by a parameter called perplexity, which determines how to balance attention between local and global aspects of the data~\cite{wattenbergHowUseTSNE2016}. t-SNE of different perplexity values can be evaluated using metrics such as trustworthiness and silhouette score. Trustworthiness measures whether points that appear close in the low-dimensional space visualization were actually close in the original high-dimensional space~\cite{trustworthinessTSNE}, and silhouette score quantifies how well-separated the resulting clusters are~\cite{gultekinWhatSilhouetteScore2023}.


