	We first characterize the total number of demanded mini-subfiles $W_{d_{U},S,T_{i,i\in[t_p]}}$ having an intersection set $ I=\{\bigcap\limits_{j=1}^{t_p} T_j\}\cap U$ of cardinality $ i $, where $i\in[1,r-1]$. 

	Consider a specific subfile-index set $ {S} $ of cardinality $ t_a $ and an intersection set $ I $ of cardinality $ i $. The problem of determining the number of mini-subfiles of a given subfile $ {S} $ having an intersection set $I$ of cardinality $ |I| = i $ reduces to identifying $ (t_p+1) $ sets $ A_{j,j\in[t_p+1]}$ of cardinality $ r $ such that their intersection satisfies $ \bigcap\limits_{j=1}^{t_p+1} A_j = I $. Once these sets are identified, one set is chosen as the user-index set $U$ and the remaining sets will form the mini-subfile-index sets $T_{j,j\in[t_p]}$. Moving forward, we denote the cardinality of the intersection $I$ as $|I|$. We now outline the procedure for calculating the number of collections of such sets. 

