\subsection{The induced chain \label{s:induced}}

% where $\pi$ is the stationary distribution satisfying $\One^\t \pi = 1$, $R^\t \pi = 0$, and $\Diag(\pi) R = R^\t \Diag(\pi)$.
% \cref{eq:p-intro} clearly implies that any distribution will converge to $\pi$ in the long-time limit.
In this section, we motivate \cref{eq:oblique-p} in terms of an \emph{induced} Markov chain $\InducedChain$ over a non-empty subset of states $\I \subset \Indices$. We introduce the nomenclature ``induced chain'' as a generalization of the construction appearing in \cite{Aldous1995-reversible}, which is recovered as a special case if $\Cu$ is a strict partitioning, i.e., 0-1 entrywise. More generally, the entries in our committor matrix $\Cu$ can lie in $[0, 1]$.

Concretely, any choice of subset $\I$ determines an induced rate matrix
\begin{eqn}
    \Rr &\Eq \Ct R \Cu
    \label{eq:simple-induced}
\end{eqn}
which governs the induced chain $\InducedChain$, where 
\begin{eqn}\label{eq:csharp}
\Ct \Eq \DiagI(\pr) \Cu^\t \Diag(\pi) \in \R^{\Ia \times n}
\end{eqn}
can be viewed as the adjoint of $\Cu$ (cf. \Cref{def:sym-operators}) accounting for the stationary distribution. 
Here $\pr \Eq \Cu^\t \pi$, in accordance with \cref{eq:L-h-def} above. 
% The rate matrix $\hat{R}$ determines a Markov chain over states in $\I$ that we call the induced chain, denoted $\InducedChain$. 
We denote the stochastic process corresponding to $\InducedChain$ as $\Process{\XI}$, which is governed by rate matrix $\Rr$ and is initialized via $\XI_0 \sim \pr$.
