\begin{abstract}
    Condensed mathematics, developed by Clausen and Scholze over the last few years, proposes a generalization of topology with better categorical properties. 
    It replaces the concept of a topological space by that of a condensed set, which can be defined as a sheaf on a certain site of compact Hausdorff spaces. 
    Since condensed sets are supposed to be a generalization of topological spaces, one would like to be able to study the notion of discreteness.
    There are various ways to define what it means for a condensed set to be discrete.
    In this paper we describe them, and prove that they are equivalent.
    The results have been fully formalized in the \Lean proof assistant. 
\end{abstract}

