\section{Semi-Competitive Differential Game Logic}
\label{main}
The logic \dGLsc supports two players called \emph{Angel} and \emph{Demon}. 
They semi-competitively play a game defined in the \dGLsc formula.

After the game has ended, \emph{both} want to have achieved their goal. Each player has a separate goal which is independent of the other player's goal. The game is \emph{non-zero sum}, both players can win or loose at the same time.
\subsection{Syntax}
The syntax of \dGLsc consists of first-order logic and two modalities. The modality $\angel{\alpha}{P}{Q}$ expresses that Angel has a winning strategy to win the game $\alpha$ by achieving her goal $P$ while knowing that Demon's goal is $Q$. $\demon{\alpha}{P}{Q}$ means that Demon has a winning strategy  to achieve $Q$ by playing $\alpha$ while knowing that Angel's goal is $P$.
\begin{definition}[\dGLsc Syntax]
    \label{def:dglsc_syntax}
The following grammar defines the \dGLsc formulas:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\alpha, \beta ::= x:=e \;|\; x'=f(x) \& Q \;|\; ?Q \;|\; \alpha \cup \beta \;|\; \alpha ; \beta \;|\; \alpha^d \;|\; \alpha ^*$
    \item $P, Q ::= e \geq \Tilde{e} \;|\; \lnot P \;|\; P \land Q \;|\; \forall x P \;|\; \exists x P \;|\; \angel{\alpha}{P}{Q} \;|\; [\alpha](P,Q)$
\end{itemize}
where $\alpha, \beta$ are hybrid games, $P, Q$ are \dGLsc formulas, $ x$ is a variable and $e, \Tilde{e}$ are terms.
\end{definition}

The hybrid games in Definition \ref{def:dglsc_syntax} have the following effects:
In the assignment game $x:=e$, Angel assigns the value of term $e$ to the variable $x$. All other variable values remain unchanged.
In the continuous game $x'=f(x)\&Q$, Angel evolves the value of the variable $x$ according to the ODE $x'=f(x)$. This means that the value of $x$ changes over time according to the ODE and Angel decides when to stop. It is possible that Angel stops the time after 0 time. The formula $Q$ is called \emph{evolution domain constraint}. While evolving the ODE, $Q$ must always be true. Therefore, $x$ can only take values that can be reached without violating $Q$. If $Q$ is false at time zero, Angel immediately loses the game.
In the test game $?Q$, Angel has to ensure that $Q$ is true in order to pass the test. If she does not pass, Angel immediately loses the game. Unlike the assignment game and continuous game, the test game does not change any variable values.
In the choice game $\alpha \cup \beta$, Angel chooses whether $\alpha$ or $\beta$ should be played.
In the sequential game $\alpha;\beta$, first $\alpha$ and then $\beta$ is played. There are no choices to make here.
All the games seen so far are under Angel's control. In order to transfer control to Demon, the dual game $\alpha^d$ is used. The game $\alpha^d$ swaps all controls: All of Angel's decisions about the choices in $\alpha$ are now Demon's decisions and also vice versa.
In the repetition game $\alpha^*$, the game $\alpha$ is played repeatedly. After each round of $\alpha$, Angel decides whether she wants to play another round or stop playing. The number of rounds she can play is arbitrary but must be finite. Angel can also decide to play $\alpha$ 0 times, i.e. to not play $\alpha$ at all.
