    \begin{cutout}{2}{0pt}{0.69\linewidth}{10}
    This example showcases how the strategies of the players change depending on the exact circumstances. The initial situation is shown in Fig. \ref{example}. 
    Angel is standing on top of a wall and Demon on the ground. They are connected with a rope that runs over a pulley. Thus, Angel can pull Demon up by jumping down, if Demon is lighter than her. Otherwise, both will not move. Before jumping, Angel may cut the rope. She wants to reach the floor without getting hurt. This might happen if her initial height is too high, and she is not slowed (enough) by  a counterweight. Demon wants to reach the top of the wall. The two players' different (but not opposite goals) are naturally stated in \dGLsc:
    \begin{minipage}{\textwidth}\vspace*{0pt}
        \begin{small}
            \[
                \begin{aligned}
                    h_A = h &\land h > 0\land h_D = 0 \land w_A >0 \land w_D >0 \land r=1 \land x>0 \rightarrow \\
                    &\angel{(r:=0 \,\cup\, ?\top); \{h_A' = min(0, -w_A + r\cdot w_D), h_D'= max(0, r(w_A-w_D))\}\\&}{h_A = 0 \land h\cdot(w_A - r\cdot w_D) < x}{h_D = h}
                \end{aligned}
            \]
        \end{small}
    \vspace*{0pt}\end{minipage}
    Initially, Angel's height $h_A$ is $h>0$ and Demon's height $h_D$ is 0. Both have positive weights $w_A$ and $w_D$. The rope $r$ is connected, and Angel's falling tolerance is $x>0$. In the game Angel can either cut the rope by setting $r$ to 0 or do nothing, indicated by $?\top$. Then, Angel jumps which might change her and Demon's height, depending on $r$. The min/max in the ODE are definable:
    \[{\small\begin{aligned}
            &\{h_A' = min(0, -w_A + r\cdot w_D), h_D'= max(0, r(w_A-w_D))\}\equiv\\
            &(?-w_A +r \cdot w_D < 0; v_A := -w_A + r\cdot w_D\; \cup \;?-w_A +r \cdot w_D \geq 0;v_A := 0);\\
            & (?r(w_A - w_D) > 0; v_D := r(w_A - w_D)\; \cup\; ?r(w_A - w_D) \leq 0; v_D := 0);\\
            &\{h_A' = v_A, h_D' = v_D\}
        \end{aligned}}
        \]
    This prevents Angel and Demon from moving into the wrong direction. Angel's goal is to reach 0 height and to not exceed her falling tolerance. Demon wants his height to be $h$ in the end. Angel's winning strategy depends on the variables. If Demon is heavier than her, she has to cut the rope, in order to win. If she is too high up, she cannot cut it, to not get injured by jumping down.
    \end{cutout}
\end{example}
\subsection{Semantics}
In the following section, the semantics of the formulas is defined. This is done via a function $\winr{\cdot}: Fml \to \mathcal{P}(\s)$ that maps a formula to the set of states where the formula is true. $\s$ is the set of all states. A state is a mapping $\omega: \mathcal{V} \to \mathbb{R}$ that maps all variables to a real number. The state $\omega_x^r$ denotes a state that coincides with the state $\omega$ in all variable values, except for $x$ whose value is replaced by $r$.

\begin{definition}[\dGLsc Semantics]
    The semantics of \dGLsc formulas is defined as the set of states in which they are true as follows:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\winr{e\geq \Tilde{e}} = \{ \omega \in \s \;|\; \omega \winr{e} \geq \omega\winr{\Tilde{e}}\}$ where $\omega \winr{e}$ is the evaluation of term $e$ in the state $\omega$.
        \item $\winr{\lnot P} = \s\setminus \winr{P} = \winr{P}^\C$
        \item $\winr{P \land Q} = \winr{P} \cap \winr{Q}$
        \item $\winr{\forall x P} = \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^r \in \winr{P} \text{ for all } r \in \mathbb{R}\}$
        \item $\winr{\exists x P} = \{ \omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^r \in \winr{P} \text{ for some } r \in \mathbb{R}\}$
        \item $\winr{\angel{\alpha}{P}{Q}} = \winrA{\alpha}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}$
        \item $\winr{\demon{\alpha}{P}{Q}} = \winrD{\alpha}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}} $
    \end{itemize}
    where $e, \Tilde{e}$ are terms, $P,Q$ are formulas, $x$ is a variable and $\alpha$ is a hybrid game.
    The function $\winrA{\alpha}{\cdot}{\cdot}$ is defined inductively as:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\winrA{x:=e}{X}{Y} = \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in X\}$
        \item $\winrA{x'=f(x) \& Q}{X}{Y} = \{\varphi(0) \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}$
        \item $\winrA{?Q}{X}{Y} = \winr{Q} \cap X$
        \item $\winrA{\alpha \cup \beta}{X}{Y}=\winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}$
        \item $\winrA{\alpha;\beta}{X}{Y}=\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}}{\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}}$
        \item $\winrA{\alpha^d}{X}{Y} = \winrD{\alpha}{Y}{X}$
        \item $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{\alpha^*}{X}{Y} &=\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z\subseteq\s\;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\} \\&\cup \bigcap\{Z\subseteq\s\;|\; (X\cap Y) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z}\cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z})\subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}
        \end{aligned}$
    \end{itemize}
    The function $\winrD{\alpha}{\cdot}{\cdot}$ is defined as:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\winrD{x:=e}{X}{Y} = \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in Y\}$
        \item $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{x'=f(x) \& Q}{X}{Y} &= \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\{\varphi(0) \in \s\;|\; \varphi(r) \in Y \text{ for all } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}\\
                &\cup \begin{aligned}[t]
                    &\{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \cap Y \\
                    &\text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}
                \end{aligned}
            \end{aligned}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item $\winrD{?Q}{X}{Y} = \winr{Q}^\C \cup Y$
        \item $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha \cup \beta}{X}{Y} &= \begin{aligned}[t]
                &(\winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X}{Y})\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y})\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} \cap \winrA{\beta}{X}{Y})
            \end{aligned} 
        \end{aligned}$
        \item $\winrD{\alpha;\beta}{X}{Y} = \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}}{\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}}$
        \item $\winrD{\alpha^d}{X}{Y} = \winrA{\alpha}{Y}{X}$
        \item $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha^*}{X}{Y} &= \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcup\{Z\subseteq\s \;|\; Z \subseteq Y \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}\}\\& \cup \bigcap\{Z\subseteq\s\;|\; (X\cap Y) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z}\cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z})\subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}   
          \end{aligned}$
    \end{itemize}
\end{definition}
The semantics of the first-order formulas is as expected.
To define the semantics of Angel's resp. Demon's modality, the function $\varsigma_\alpha$, resp. $\delta_\alpha$ is used. Both functions take two inputs each to reflect that Angel and Demon know each other's goals. These functions describe the winning region for a game. Starting in these states, Angel, resp. Demon can ensure to end up in their goal region after the game, under the assumption that they play semi-competitively.

Angel's winning region for the assignment game $x:=e$ consists of all states from which she can reach a state in her goal $X$ by setting the value of $x$ to the value of $e$.
The states that lie in Angel's winning region for the continuous game $x'=f(x) \&Q$, are the ones from which she can reach a state in her goal $X$ by following the ODE.
To win the test game, Angel needs to be in a state where $Q$ is true to pass the test. Additionally, she must also be in her goal $X$ to win because the test game does not change the state.
A state in Angel's winning region for the choice game $\alpha \cup \beta$ needs to be in the winning region for $\alpha$ or for $\beta$ because then she can pick a game for which she has a winning strategy.
To win the sequential game $\alpha;\beta$, Angel has to reach $\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}$ after the game $\alpha$. In this case, she can reach her goal $X$ after $\beta$. Demon's goal for $\alpha$ is $\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}$ because  he can reach his goal $Y$ from these states after $\beta$.
The dual game $\alpha^d$ swaps the controls of Angel and Demon. The semantics simulates this by letting Angel keep the controls and making her play $\alpha$ for Demon, with his goals. Consequently, their goals are swapped in the definition. Additionally, Angel uses Demons function $\delta_\alpha$ because her own now returns Demon's winning region.
Angel's winning region for the repetition game consists of two fixpoints which cover the cases that players may either compete or cooperate in a semi-cooperative game. The first one includes all states where Angel can win against an adversarial Demon and the second one includes all states where Angel can win by cooperating with Demon. A set $Z$ that is a candidate to be the first fixpoint must fulfill $X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^C} \subseteq Z$ because, Angel can win either now or after one more round. As she is assumed to compete with Demon in this fixpoint, his goal is set to $Z^\C$. The greatest such set is always the set of all states $\s$, but obviously not all repetition games can be won from any state. Therefore, the smallest set that fulfills the conditions is the first fixpoint. It can be computed by intersecting all the candidates $Z$. To be a candidate for the second fixpoint, $Z$ has to fulfill $(X\cap Y) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z$. Angel and Demon can either win now or after one more round of $\alpha$. Intersecting $\winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y}$ with $\winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y}$ rules out that one player wins and fulfills both their goals, but the other one loses because they, for example, fail a test. As explained, the smallest such set is needed because the greatest one fulfilling the conditions is $\s$ which does not qualify.

\begin{remark}
    Only the semantics of the repetition game for Angel directly consider cooperating with Demon. The assignment game, test game, sequential game and dual game do not need to because they involve no choices. For the continuous game and the choice game, the sets of states where Angel and Demon cooperate are already included in the current semantics. As can be seen in Demon's semantics, the set of states for which Angel and Demon cooperate in the continuous game is $\{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \cap Y \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}$. Clearly, this is a subset of $\{\varphi(0) \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}$. Likewise, for the choice game: the part where they cooperate $(\winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y}) \cup (\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} \cap \winrA{\beta}{X}{Y})$ is a subset of $\winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}$. Therefore, and for reasons of simplicity, these are not explicitly written repeatedly in the semantics. 
\end{remark}

Except that $\omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}}$ now has to be in $Y$, Demon's winning region for the assignment game $x:=e$ is similar to Angel's because no choices can be made.
Demon can win the continuous game $x'=f(x)\&Q$, if he can win in any state that can be reached by following the ODE, so he does not care when Angel stops, or a state where both goals are fulfilled can be reached. As Angel and Demon play semi-competitively, Angel will stop in this state to make both win. If $Q$ is false at the beginning, Demon wins automatically because of the universal quantifier used in the first part of the definition.
As the test $?Q$ is for Angel, Demon cannot fail the test. But if Angel fails the test, the course of the game is invalid and the game ends early. These invalid courses of the game should not affect the validity of a formula with Demon's modality.  Therefore, Demon wins automatically, if Angel fails a test. Consequently, all states in $\winr{Q}^C$, are part of Demon's winning region.  If Angel passes the test though, Demon has to be in his goal $Y$ to win the game, as the state does not change.
\begin{remark}
    The order of tests matters. For example, the second player tested in the game $?\bot; (?\bot)^d$ (Demon) will win the game despite his impossible test. Angel fails her test first thus making Demon win automatically. If he had been tested first, he would have lost and Angel would have won. Still, goals can be converted into tests, as proven in Lemma \ref{test_conversion} (see Appendix).

    \begin{lemmaE}[Goals to tests conversion][all end]
        \label{test_conversion}
        Goals can be converted into tests:
        \[{\small
            \angel{\alpha}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow \angel{\alpha; ?P ;(?Q)^d}{\top}{\top} \land \angel{\alpha;(?Q)^d; ?P}{\top}{\top}} 
        \]
        \[{\small
            \demon{\alpha}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow \demon{\alpha; ?P ;(?Q)^d}{\top}{\top} \land \demon{\alpha;(?Q)^d; ?P}{\top}{\top} }
        \]
    To make sure that Angel, resp. Demon not only wins because the other one fails the test, they have to be able to win the game for any sequence of the tests.
    \end{lemmaE}
    \begin{proofE}
        The proofs for Angel and Demon are conducted using the rules and axioms from the proof calculus \ref{dglA_calculus} and the complementarization axioms from Lemma \ref{devRules}. For Angel, the games on the left-hand side of the equivalence are broken down using $\seqA$, $\dA$, $\tA$, $\tD$ first. When only the game $\alpha$ is left, the two modalities are broken into two parts using the first complementarization axiom. After some simplifications, the left-hand side of the equivalence is transformed to $(\angel{\alpha}{P} \land \angel{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)}) \lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P \land Q)}$. Then, $\angel{\alpha}{P} \land \angel{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)}$ is replaced by the equivalent $\angel{\alpha}{P}$. This equivalence is shown in a separate proof tree (marked with \circled{$*$}) using the monotonicity rule $\MAtwo$. Using the first complementarization axiom again on the result yields $\angel{\alpha}{P}{Q}$ which proofs the claim.
        \[\begin{aligned}
        &\angel{\alpha; ?P; (?Q)^d}{\top}{\top} \land \angel{\alpha;(?Q)^d;?P}{\top}{\top}\\
        &\stackrel{\seqA}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}\angel{\alpha; ?P}{\angel{(?Q)^d}{\top}{\top}}{\demon{(?Q)^d}{\top}{\top}} \land \angel{\alpha; (?Q)^d}{\angel{?P}{\top}{\top}}{\demon{?P}{\top}{\top}}\\
        &\stackrel{\dA, \dD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}\angel{\alpha; ?P}{\demon{?Q}{\top}{\top}}{\angel{?Q}{\top}{\top}} \land \angel{\alpha; (?Q)^d}{\angel{?P}{\top}{\top}}{\demon{?P}{\top}{\top}}\\
        &\stackrel{\tA, \tD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha; ?P}{\lnot Q \lor \top}{Q \land \top} \land \angel{\alpha; (?Q)^d}{P \land \top}{\lnot P \lor \top}\\
        &\stackrel{\seqA}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha}{\angel{?P}{\top}{Q}}{\demon{?P}{\top}{Q}} \land \angel{\alpha}{\angel{(?Q)^d}{P}{\top}}{\demon{(?Q)^d}{P}{\top}}\\
        &\stackrel{\dA, \dD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha}{\angel{?P}{\top}{Q}}{\demon{?P}{\top}{Q}} \land \angel{\alpha}{\demon{?Q}{\top}{P}}{\angel{?Q}{\top}{P}}\\
        &\stackrel{\tA, \tD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha}{P \land \top}{\lnot P \lor Q} \land \angel{\alpha}{\lnot Q \lor P}{\top \land Q}\\
        &\stackrel{\text{C.A. 1}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}(\angel{\alpha}{P} \lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P \land (\lnot P \lor Q))}) \land (\angel{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)} \lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{((\lnot Q \lor P) \land Q)})\\
        &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} (\angel{\alpha}{P} \lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P \land Q)}) \land (\angel{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)} \lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P \land Q)})\\
        &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} (\angel{\alpha}{P} \land \angel{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)}) \lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P \land Q)}\\
        &\stackrel{\circled{$*$}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha}{P} \lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}\\
        &\stackrel{\text{C.A. 1}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha}{P}{Q}
        \end{aligned}\]

        \AxiomC{$*$}
        \LeftLabel{id}
        \UnaryInfC{$P \vdash \lnot Q, P$}
        \LeftLabel{${\rightarrow}R$, ${\lor}R$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\vdash P \rightarrow \lnot Q \lor P$}
        \AxiomC{$*$}
        \LeftLabel{$\bot L$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\bot \vdash \bot$}
        \LeftLabel{prop. L., ${\rightarrow} R$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\vdash P \land \lnot P \rightarrow \bot$}
        \LeftLabel{$\MAtwo$}
        \BinaryInfC{\circled{2}  $\vdash \angel{\alpha}{P} \rightarrow \angel{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)}$}
        \DisplayProof

        \vspace{0.5cm}
        \AxiomC{$*$}
        \LeftLabel{${\rightarrow} R$, id}
        \UnaryInfC{$\angel{\alpha}{P} \rightarrow \angel{\alpha}{P}$}
        \AxiomC{\circled{2}}
        \LeftLabel{${\land}R$}
        \BinaryInfC{\circled{1}  $\angel{\alpha}{P} \rightarrow \angel{\alpha}{P} \land \angel{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)}$}
        \DisplayProof

        \vspace{0.5cm}
        \AxiomC{$*$}
        \LeftLabel{id}
        \UnaryInfC{$\angel{\alpha}{P}, \angel{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)} \vdash \angel{\alpha}{P}$}
        \LeftLabel{${\rightarrow} R$, ${\land} L$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\vdash \angel{\alpha}{P} \land \angel{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)} \rightarrow \angel{\alpha}{P}$}
        \AxiomC{\circled{1}}
        \LeftLabel{prop. L., ${\land} R$}
        \BinaryInfC{\circled{$*$}  $\vdash \angel{\alpha}{P} \land \angel{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)} \leftrightarrow \angel{\alpha}{P}$}
        \DisplayProof

        \vspace{0.5cm}
        Demon's proof works similarly to Angels:
        \[\begin{aligned}
            &\demon{\alpha; ?P; (?Q)^d}{\top}{\top} \land \demon{\alpha;(?Q)^d;?P}{\top}{\top}\\
            &\stackrel{\seqD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \demon{\alpha; ?P}{\angel{(?Q)^d}{\top}{\top}}{\demon{(?Q)^d}{\top}{\top}} \land \demon{\alpha; (?Q)^d}{\angel{?P}{\top}{\top}}{\demon{?P}{\top}{\top}}\\
            &\stackrel{\dA, \dD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}\demon{\alpha; ?P}{\demon{?Q}{\top}{\top}}{\angel{?Q}{\top}{\top}} \land \demon{\alpha; (?Q)^d}{\angel{?P}{\top}{\top}}{\demon{?P}{\top}{\top}}\\
            &\stackrel{\tA, \tD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \demon{\alpha; ?P}{\lnot Q \lor \top}{Q \land \top} \land \demon{\alpha; (?Q)^d}{P \land \top}{\lnot P \lor \top}\\
            &\stackrel{\seqD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \demon{\alpha}{\angel{?P}{\top}{Q}}{\demon{?P}{\top}{Q}} \land \demon{\alpha}{\angel{(?Q)^d}{P}{\top}}{\demon{(?Q)^d}{P}{\top}}\\
            &\stackrel{\dA, \dD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \demon{\alpha}{\angel{?P}{\top}{Q}}{\demon{?P}{\top}{Q}} \land \demon{\alpha}{\demon{?Q}{\top}{P}}{\angel{?Q}{\top}{P}}\\
            &\stackrel{\tA, \tD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \demon{\alpha}{P \land \top}{\lnot P \lor Q} \land \demon{\alpha}{\lnot Q \lor P}{\top \land Q}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{C.A. 2}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}(\demon{\alpha}{P} \lor \demon{\alpha^{-d}}{(P \land (\lnot P \lor Q))}) \land (\demon{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)} \lor \demon{\alpha^{-d}}{((\lnot Q \lor P) \land Q)})\\
            &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} (\demon{\alpha}{P} \lor \demon{\alpha^{-d}}{(P \land Q)}) \land (\demon{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)} \lor \demon{\alpha^{-d}}{(P \land Q)})\\
            &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} (\demon{\alpha}{P} \land \demon{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)}) \lor \demon{\alpha^{-d}}{(P \land Q)}\\
            &\stackrel{\circled{$*\!*$}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \demon{\alpha}{P} \lor \demon{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{C.A. 2}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \demon{\alpha}{P}{Q}
        \end{aligned}\]
        \AxiomC{$*$}
        \LeftLabel{id}
        \UnaryInfC{$P \vdash \lnot Q, P$}
        \LeftLabel{${\rightarrow}R$, ${\lor}R$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\vdash P \rightarrow \lnot Q \lor P$}
        \AxiomC{$*$}
        \LeftLabel{$\bot L$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\bot \vdash \bot$}
        \LeftLabel{prop. L., ${\rightarrow} R$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\vdash P \land \lnot P \rightarrow \bot$}
        \LeftLabel{$\MAtwo$}
        \BinaryInfC{$\vdash \angel{\alpha^d}{P} \rightarrow \angel{\alpha^d}{(\lnot Q \lor P)}$}
        \LeftLabel{$\dA$}
        \UnaryInfC{\circled{2}  $\vdash \demon{\alpha}{P} \rightarrow \demon{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)}$}
        \DisplayProof

        \vspace{0.5cm}
        \AxiomC{$*$}
        \LeftLabel{${\rightarrow} R$, id}
        \UnaryInfC{$\vdash \demon{\alpha}{P} \rightarrow \demon{\alpha}{P}$}
        \AxiomC{\circled{2}}
        \LeftLabel{${\land}R$}
        \BinaryInfC{\circled{1}  $\vdash \demon{\alpha}{P} \rightarrow \demon{\alpha}{P} \land \demon{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)}$}
        \DisplayProof

        \vspace{0.5cm}
        \AxiomC{$*$}
        \LeftLabel{id}
        \UnaryInfC{$\demon{\alpha}{P}, \demon{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)} \vdash \demon{\alpha}{P}$}
        \LeftLabel{${\rightarrow} R$, ${\land} L$}
        \UnaryInfC{$\vdash \demon{\alpha}{P} \land \demon{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)} \rightarrow \demon{\alpha}{P}$}
        \AxiomC{\circled{1}}
        \LeftLabel{prop. L., ${\land} R$}
        \BinaryInfC{\circled{$*\!*$}  $\vdash \demon{\alpha}{P} \land \demon{\alpha}{(\lnot Q \lor P)} \leftrightarrow \demon{\alpha}{P}$}
        \DisplayProof
        \qed
    \end{proofE}
\end{remark}
To win the choice game $\alpha \cup \beta$, Demon can either win both games, so he does not care which game Angel chooses, or there is a game which both of them can win. As they play semi-competitively, Angel will choose this game.
Demon's winning region for the sequential game $\alpha;\beta$ is very similar to Angel's because the game does not involve choices. But instead of $\varsigma_\alpha$, $\delta_\alpha$ has to be evaluated to obtain Demon's winning region.
In the dual game $\alpha^d$, Angel simulates Demon being in control by playing for him with his goals. Therefore, Angel and Demon's goals are swapped. Additionally, Demon's winning region is now determined by $\varsigma_\alpha$ because Angel is playing for him.
Like Angel's winning region for the repetition game $\alpha^*$, Demon's winning region also consists of two fixpoints.  The first fixpoint covers the case where Angel and Demon compete. The second fixpoint covers the case where they cooperate. A candidate $Z$ for the first fixpoint needs to fulfill $Z \subseteq Y \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}$ because Demon needs to be in his goal now and after one more round of $\alpha$. This ensures that he is in his goal whenever Angel stops. As Demon competes with Angel, her goal is set to $Z^\C$. The smallest candidate set $Z$ is the empty set which obviously cannot be the right solution. Therefore, the first fixpoint must be the greatest such set. It can be obtained by uniting all candidates $Z$. The second fixpoint is exactly the same as Angel's because it describes the case where both win simultaneously.

\section{\dGLsc Properties and Relation to Zero-sum \dGL}
\label{properties}
In this section, some important results for \dGLsc are proven. The first result proves monotonicity which ensures that the logic behaves in an intuitive way: A larger goal can be reached from a larger winning region.
The first lemma states that the logic is monotone if both goals are increased. In this case, both Angel's and Demon's winning region will increase.
The proof for this lemma is a straightforward structural induction over $\alpha$.

\begin{lemmaE}[Monotonicity][end]
\label{monotonicity}
    The logic \dGLsc is monotone, i.e.
    \[
    X\subseteq A, Y\subseteq B \text{ implies } \winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y} \subseteq \winrA{\alpha}{A}{B} \text{ and } \winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \subseteq \winrD{\alpha}{A}{B}
    \]
\end{lemmaE}
\begin{proofE}
    The proof is conducted by induction over the structure of the game $\alpha$.

    Induction start:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{x:=e}{X}{Y}&\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A}}{=}\{\omega \in \s \;|\;\omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in X\}\\
            &\stackrel{X\subseteq A}{\subseteq} \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega \winr{e}} \in A\}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A}}{=} \winrA{x:=e}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$

        To prove the monotonicity of the assignment game, the definition of its winning region is written out first. As can be seen now,  the goal $X$ can be replaced by a bigger set $A$, in order to increase the whole winning region. Consequently, the winning region is monotone.

        The proofs for the other games in the induction start have the same idea in mind. The goal $X$ can be replaced by $A$ and $Y$ can be replaced by $B$ to increase the winning regions. Therefore, the rest of the induction start is left uncommented.
         \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{x'=f(x)\&Q}{X}{Y}&\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A}}{=}\{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \& Q\}\\
            &\stackrel{X\subseteq A}{\subseteq} \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in A \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \& Q\}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A}}{=}\winrA{x'=f(x)\&Q}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{?Q}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A}}{=} \winr{Q} \cap X\\
            &\stackrel{X\subseteq A}{\subseteq} \winr{Q} \cap A\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A}}{=} \winrA{?Q}{X}{Y}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{x:=e}{X}{Y}&\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A}}{=}\{\omega \in \s \;|\;\omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in Y\}\\
            &\stackrel{Y\subseteq B}{\subseteq} \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega \winr{e}} \in B\}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A}}{=} \winrD{x:=e}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{x'=f(x) \& Q}{X}{Y}&\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A, Y \subseteq B}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in Y \text{ for all } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \& Q\}\\
                &\cup \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \cap Y \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \& Q \}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{X\subseteq A, Y \subseteq B}{\subseteq}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in B \text{ for all } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \& Q\}\\
                &\cup \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in A \cap B \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \& Q \}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A, Y \subseteq B}}{=} \winrD{x'=f(x) \& Q}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{?Q}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{Y \subseteq B}}{=} \winr{Q}^\C \cup Y\\
            &\stackrel{Y\subseteq B}{\subseteq} \winr{Q}^\C \cup B\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{Y \subseteq B}}{=} \winrD{?Q}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$
    \end{itemize}
    Induction hypothesis (IH):

    Assume that $\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y} \subseteq \winrA{\beta}{A}{B}$ and $\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} \subseteq \winrD{\beta}{A}{B}$ holds for sets $X\subseteq A$ and $Y\subseteq B$ and for all games $\beta$ that are structurally smaller than $\alpha$.

    Induction step:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{\alpha\cup\beta}{X}{Y} &= \winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq}\winrA{\alpha}{A}{B} \cup \winrA{\beta}{A}{B}\\
            &= \winrA{\alpha\cup\beta}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$

        First, the definition of the winning region of the choice game for Angel is written out. This disassembles the winning region into the union of the winning region of games $\alpha$ and $\beta$. On these smaller games, the IH can be used. By increasing the goals in these winning regions, the winning regions are consequently expanded. In addition, the size of the union of these winning regions increases. Therefore, the winning region for the choice game increases which proves the game's monotonicity.

        The other proofs in the induction step work the same as the one described above. Therefore, they are not commented anymore. Only the next proof is still commented because the induction hypothesis has to be used twice here.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{\alpha;\beta}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{IH},*}}{=} \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}}{\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH},*}{\subseteq} \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{A}{B}}{\winrD{\beta}{A}{B}}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{IH},*}}{=} \winrA{\alpha;\beta}{A}{B}\\
        \\
        &*\;\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y} \stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrA{\beta}{A}{B},\;
        \winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} \stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrD{\beta}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$

        In the proof for the sequential game for Angel, the IH is used twice. First, it is used to prove that the winning regions $\varsigma_\beta$ and $\delta_\beta$ increase with increasing goals. As these are the goals for game $\alpha$ in the definition of the sequential game's winning region, it can then be proved that $\alpha$'s winning region increases too. Consequently, the sequential game for Angel is monotone.

        The proof for Demon's sequential game is done similarly to this one.
        \item
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{\alpha^d}{X}{Y} &=\winrD{\alpha}{Y}{X}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrD{\alpha}{B}{A}\\
            &= \winrA{\alpha^d}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{\alpha^*}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A, Y\subseteq B}}{=} \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}\\
                &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\;  (X \cap Y) \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{X\subseteq A, Y\subseteq B}{\subseteq} 
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; A \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}\\
                &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\;  (A \cap b) \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned} \\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A, Y\subseteq B}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^*}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha\cup \beta}{X}{Y} &= \begin{aligned}[t]
                &(\winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X}{Y})\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y})\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} \winrA{\beta}{X}{Y})
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &(\winrD{\alpha}{A}{B} \cap \winrD{\beta}{A}{B})\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\alpha}{A}{B} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{A}{B})\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\beta}{A}{B}\cap \winrA{\beta}{A}{B})
            \end{aligned}\\
            &= \winrD{\alpha\cup \beta}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha;\beta}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{IH}, *}}{=} \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}}{\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}, *}{\subseteq} \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{A}{B}}{\winrD{\beta}{A}{B}}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{IH}, *}}{=} \winrD{\alpha;\beta}{A}{B}\\
            \\
            &*\; \winrA{\beta}{X}{Y} \stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrA{\beta}{A}{B}, 
            \winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} \stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrD{\beta}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha^d}{X}{Y} &= \winrA{\alpha}{Y}{X}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrA{\alpha}{B}{A}\\
            &= \winrD{\alpha^d}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha^*}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A, Y\subseteq B}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcup\{Z\subseteq\s \;|\; Z \subseteq Y \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}\}\\
                &\cup \bigcap\{Z\subseteq\s \;|\; (X\cap Y) \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{X\subseteq A, Y\subseteq B}{\subseteq}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcup\{Z\subseteq\s \;|\; Z \subseteq B \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}\}\\
                &\cup \bigcap\{Z\subseteq\s \;|\; (A\cap B) \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{X\subseteq A, Y\subseteq B}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^*}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$
    \end{itemize}
    \qed
\end{proofE}

The second lemma states that if Angel's and Demon's goals are disjoint, Angel's winning region expands by increasing her goal while Demon's goal may change arbitrarily. Intuitively, all parts in the semantics covering cooperation are empty since the goals are disjoint. Then, proving that the remaining sets grow by increasing the goal is fairly easy. After that, the parts where Angel and Demon cooperate, can be added. Thus, the winning region can only increase. The proof for Demon's statement is conducted similarly.

The following lemma is especially helpful if Angel and Demon have opposing goals, before and after the increase of one goal. In this case, enlarging one goal means that the other shrinks. Therefore, the first monotonicity lemma \ref{monotonicity} is not applicable but Lemma \ref{mon2} can be used.
\begin{lemmaE}[Disjoint Monotonicity][end]
    \label{mon2}
    If the goals of Angel and Demon are disjoint, then \dGLsc is monotone in each argument:
    \[
    \begin{aligned}
        &X\subseteq A, X\cap Y = \emptyset \text{ implies } \winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y} \subseteq \winrA{\alpha}{A}{B}
        \text{  and}\\
        &Y \subseteq B, X \cap Y = \emptyset \text{ implies } \winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \subseteq \winrD{\alpha}{A}{B}
    \end{aligned}
    \]
\end{lemmaE}
\begin{proofE}
    The proof is done by induction over the structure of the game $\alpha$ simultaneously for both claims.

    Induction start:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                \winrA{x:=e}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{X \subseteq A}}{=} \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in X\}\\
                &\stackrel{X\subseteq A}{\subseteq} \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in A\}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{X \subseteq A}}{=} \winrA{x:=e}{A}{B}
            \end{aligned}$

            In the first step, the definition of the winning region of the assignment game is used. Then the size of the obtained set is increased by replacing $X$ with its superset $A$. Afterwards, the definition of the winning region of the assignment game is used again, yielding as a result that $\winrA{x:=e}{X}{Y}$ is a subset of $\winrA{x:=e}{A}{B}$.

            The next proofs in the induction start follow the same proof idea: After using the definition of the respective winning region, the size of the obtained set can be increased by replacing $X$ by $A$ or $Y$ by $B$. Then the set is transformed back into a winning region to show that $\winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y} \subseteq \winrA{\alpha}{A}{B}$ for $X\subseteq A$, $X \cap Y = \emptyset$ and $\winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \subseteq \winrD{\alpha}{A}{B}$ for $Y \subseteq B$, $X \cap Y = \emptyset$. As all proofs follow this scheme, they are left uncommented.
        \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                \winrD{x:=e}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{X \subseteq A}}{=} \{\omega\in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in Y\}\\
                &\stackrel{Y \subseteq B}{\subseteq} \{\omega\in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in B\}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{X \subseteq A}}{=} \winrD{x:=e}{A}{B}
            \end{aligned}$
        \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                \winrA{x'=f(x) \& Q}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{X \subseteq A}}{=} \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}\\
                &\stackrel{X\subseteq A}{\subseteq} \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in A \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{X \subseteq A}}{=} \winrA{x'=f(x)\& Q}{A}{B}
            \end{aligned}$
        \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                \winrD{x'=f(x)\& Q}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{X \cap Y = \emptyset}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
                    &\{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in Y \text{ for all } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x)\land Q\}\\
                    &\cup \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \cap Y \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x)\land Q\}
                \end{aligned}\\
                &\stackrel{X \cap Y = \emptyset}{=} \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in Y \text{ for all } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x)\land Q\}\\
                &\stackrel{Y \subseteq B}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{X \cap Y = \emptyset}{=}$}]{\subseteq}} \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in B \text{ for all } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x)\land Q\}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{X \cap Y = \emptyset}}{\subseteq}
                \begin{aligned}[t]
                    &\{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in B \text{ for all } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x)\land Q\}\\
                    &\cup \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in A \cap B \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x)\land Q\}
                \end{aligned}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{X \cap Y = \emptyset}}{=} \winrD{x'=f(x)\& Q}{A}{B}
            \end{aligned}$
        \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                \winrA{?R}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{X \subseteq A}}{=} \winr{R} \cap X\\
                &\stackrel{X \subseteq A}{\subseteq} \winr{R} \cap A\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{X \subseteq A}}{=} \winrA{?R}{A}{B}
            \end{aligned}$
        \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                \winrD{?R}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{X \subseteq A}}{=}\winr{R}^\C \cup Y\\
                &\stackrel{Y \subseteq B}{\subseteq} \winr{R}^\C \cup B\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{X \subseteq A}}{=} \winrD{?R}{A}{B}
            \end{aligned}$
    \end{itemize}
    Induction hypothesis (IH):

     $\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y} \subseteq \winrA{\beta}{A}{B}$ for $X\subseteq A$, $X \cap Y = \emptyset$ and $\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} \subseteq \winrD{\beta}{A}{B}$ for $Y \subseteq B$, $X \cap Y = \emptyset$ hold for all games $\beta$ that are structurally smaller than $\alpha$.

    Induction step:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                \winrA{\alpha\cup\beta}{X}{Y} &= \winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrA{\alpha}{A}{B} \cup \winrA{\beta}{A}{B}\\
                &= \winrA{\alpha\cup\beta}{A}{B}
            \end{aligned}$

            By using the definition of the winning region for the choice game, the winning region can be split into two winning regions. These two winning regions are for game $\alpha$ and game $\beta$ respectively which are games structurally smaller than $\alpha \cup \beta$ because they have one choice game less. Therefore, the induction hypothesis is applicable. By replacing both parts of the union with a bigger set, the size of the whole union is increased. Then, the definition of the winning region for the choice game can be used again and $\winrA{\alpha\cup\beta}{A}{B}$ is obtained which proves the claim.
        \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                \winrD{\alpha\cup \beta}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{X \cap Y = \emptyset}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
                    &(\winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X}{Y})\\
                    &\cup (\winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y})\\
                    &\cup (\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} \cap \winrA{\beta}{X}{Y})
                \end{aligned}\\
                &\stackrel{X\cap Y = \emptyset}{=} \winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{X \cap Y = \emptyset}{=}$}]{\subseteq}} \winrD{\alpha}{A}{B} \cap \winrD{\beta}{A}{B}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{X \cap Y = \emptyset}}{\subseteq}
                \begin{aligned}[t]
                    &(\winrD{\alpha}{A}{B} \cap \winrD{\beta}{A}{B})\\
                    &\cup (\winrD{\alpha}{A}{B} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{A}{B})\\
                    &\cup (\winrD{\beta}{A}{B} \cap \winrA{\beta}{A}{B})
                \end{aligned}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{X \cap Y = \emptyset}}{=} \winrD{\alpha\cup \beta}{A}{B}
            \end{aligned}$

            The first step for the proof for Demon's winning region is the same as for Angel: The definition of the winning region for the choice game is used. Now, the second assumption $X \cap Y = \emptyset$ can be applied to see that $\winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y}$ and $\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} \cap \winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}$ are empty. The goals $X$ and $Y$ can never be reached at the same time. Therefore, Angel and Demon cannot both win a game at the same time with these goals. Consequently, the intersection of their winning regions must be empty. After eliminating the empty sets, the IH can be used on the remaining part $\winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}$. To bring the result back into a form that corresponds to the definition of the winning region for the choice game, some more sets need to be added. As they are appended by union, this set can only be expanded, so this is no problem. After that, the definition of the winning region is used to obtain $\winrD{\alpha\cup \beta}{A}{B}$ which proves the claim.
        \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                \winrA{\alpha;\beta}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{IH}, *}}{=} \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}}{\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}, *}{\subseteq} \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{A}{B}}{\winrD{\beta}{A}{B}}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{IH}, *}}{=} \winrA{\alpha;\beta}{A}{B}
            \end{aligned}$

            *  $\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y} \stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrA{\beta}{A}{B}$, $\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} \stackrel{X\cap Y = \emptyset}{=} \emptyset$

            In the proof for the sequential game, the induction hypothesis needs to be applied twice. But first, the definition of the winning region for the sequential game is used. It is assumed that  $X\subseteq A$ and $X \cap Y = \emptyset$. Therefore, the IH is applicable on the winning region for game $\beta$, $\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}$. As a result, $\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y} \subseteq \winrA{\beta}{A}{B}$ is obtained. Additionally, it is known that $\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} = \emptyset$. This is because $X\cap Y = \emptyset$. Consequently, it is impossible to achieve both goals simultaneously which is why Angel and Demon cannot win at the same time. With these two results, all preconditions are fulfilled to use the IH on $\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}}{\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}}$. After doing so and using the definition of the winning region for the sequential game, $\winrA{\alpha;\beta}{A}{B}$ is obtained which proves the claim.
        \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                \winrD{\alpha;\beta}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{IH}, *}}{=} \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}}{\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}, *}{\subseteq} \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{A}{B}}{\winrD{\beta}{A}{B}}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{IH}, *}}{=} \winrD{\alpha;\beta}{A}{B}
            \end{aligned}$

            *  $\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} \stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrD{\beta}{A}{B}$, $\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} \stackrel{X\cap Y = \emptyset}{=} \emptyset$

            The proof of the sequential game for Demon's winning region works just the same as the proof above for Angel's winning region.
        \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                \winrA{\alpha^d}{X}{Y} &= \winrD{\alpha}{Y}{X}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrD{\alpha}{B}{A}\\
                &= \winrA{\alpha^d}{A}{B}
            \end{aligned}$

            First, as usual, the definition of Angel's winning region for the dual game is used. Thus, Demon's winning region for the game $\alpha$ is obtained. To increase the size of the winning region, the IH for Demon's winning region is used. The preconditions are fulfilled: The set $X$ which is now Demon's goal, is a subset of set $A$ and $X\cap Y = \emptyset$. Afterwards, the definition of the dual game can be applied again to prove the claim.
        \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                \winrD{\alpha^d}{X}{Y} &= \winrA{\alpha}{Y}{X}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrA{\alpha}{B}{A}\\
                &= \winrD{\alpha^d}{A}{B}
            \end{aligned}$

            This proof works just the same as the one for Angel's winning region. Because the goals of Angel and Demon are swapped in the definition of the dual game, the IH for Angel's winning region can be applied to increase the size of the set. It can then be transformed back to Demon's winning region for the dual game which proves the claim.
        \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                \winrA{\alpha^*}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{X\cap Y = \emptyset}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
                    &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}\\
                    &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (X\cap Y) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z\}
                \end{aligned}\\
                &\stackrel{X\cap Y = \emptyset}{=} \bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}\\
                &\stackrel{X \subseteq A}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{X\cap Y = \emptyset}{=}$}]{\subseteq}} \bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; A \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{X\cap Y = \emptyset}}{\subseteq} 
                \begin{aligned}[t]
                    &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; A \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}\\
                    &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (A\cap B) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z\}
                \end{aligned}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{X\cap Y = \emptyset}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^*}{X}{Y}
            \end{aligned}$

            The proof for the repetition game works similar to the proof for Demon's winning region for the choice game. First, the definition of Angel's winning region for the repetition game is used. Now it can be seen that the second set in the union must be empty. The assumptions provide that $X\cap Y$ is empty. A least fixpoint that includes the empty set, must also be empty. The remaining part can be increased by replacing $X$ with its superset $Y$. Afterwards, the set can be further increased by uniting it with $\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (A\cap B) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z\}$. This is done to obtain the shape of the definition of the repetition game. Intuitively this means that with the sets $X$ and $Y$, no cooperation was possible. The sets $A$ and $B$ may allow for cooperation, but this can only increase the size of the winning region, never decrease it. The result can be transformed into the winning region for the repetition game which proves the claim.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha^*}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{X\cap Y = \emptyset}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; Z \subseteq Y \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}\}\\
                &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (X\cap Y) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{X\cap Y = \emptyset}{=} \bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; Z \subseteq Y \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}\}\\
            &\stackrel{Y \subseteq B}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{X\cap Y = \emptyset}{=}$}]{\subseteq}} \bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; Z \subseteq B \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}\}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{X\cap Y = \emptyset}}{\subseteq}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; Z \subseteq B \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}\}\\
                &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (A\cap B) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{X\cap Y = \emptyset}}{=} \winrD{\alpha^*}{A}{B}
        \end{aligned}$

        The proof for Demon's winning region for the repetition game works similarly to Angel's proof. First, the definition of the winning region for the repetition game is used. The second part of the definition is the same set as in Angel's winning region. As known, this set is empty. The remaining set can be increased by using the assumption $Y \subseteq B$. Afterwards, the result is united with $\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (A\cap B) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z\}$ to bring it in the shape that is needed for using the definition of the repetition game. Finally, $\winrD{\alpha^*}{A}{B}$ is obtained which proves the claim.
    \end{itemize}
    \qed
\end{proofE}

As \dGLsc is an extension of the logic \dGL, some results about the relationship between them will be proven. First, \dGLsc and \dGL are equivalent, if Angel and Demon have complementary goals. This property shows that the semantics of \dGLsc is reasonably defined: Both formulas express the same and have the same truth value as the following lemma shows:
\begin{lemmaE}[Complementary \dGL Equivalence][end]
    \label{CollapseDGL}
If Angel and Demon have complementary goals, the logic \dGLsc reduces to \dGL, i.e. it holds that
\[
\winrA{\alpha}{X}{X^\C} = \winrA{\alpha}{X} \quad\text{and}\quad
\winrD{\alpha}{X^\C}{X} = \winrD{\alpha}{X}
\]
\end{lemmaE}
\begin{proofE}
    The proof is done by induction over the structure of the game $\alpha$.

    Induction start:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{x:=e}{X}{X^\C} &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in X\}\\
            &= \winrA{x:=e}{X}
        \end{aligned}$

        For Angel, the definition of the winning region in \dGLsc is the same as the one in \dGL. This also true for Demon's winning regions for the assignment and the test game. Therefore, the claim is true directly.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{x'=f(x) \& Q}{X}{X^\C} &= \{\varphi(0) \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}\\
            &= \winrA{x'=f(x) \& Q}{X}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{?Q}{X}{X^\C} &= \winr{Q} \cap X
            &= \winrA{?Q}{X}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{x:=e}{X}{X^\C} &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in X^\C\}
            &= \winrD{x:=e}{X^\C}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{x'=f(x) \& Q}{X}{X^\C} &=\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X^C \text{ for all } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}\\
                &\cup \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \cap X^\C \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &= \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X^C \text{ for all } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}\\
            &= \winrD{x'=f(x) \& Q}{X^\C}
        \end{aligned}$

        In the definition of the continuous game for Demon, there is a part that is equal to the definition of the continuous game in \dGL. As the goals are $X$ and $X^\C$, the other part is the empty set because there are no states in the goals' intersection. Therefore, only the other part is left which corresponds to the game's definition in dGL.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{?Q}{X}{X^\C} = \winr{Q}^\C \cup X^\C
            = \winrD{?Q}{X^\C}
        \end{aligned}$
    \end{itemize}
    Induction hypothesis (IH):

    Assume that the statement holds for all games that are structurally smaller than $\alpha$.

    Induction step:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{\alpha\cup\beta}{X}{X^\C} &= \winrA{\alpha}{X}{X^\C} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}{X^\C}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{=} \winrA{\alpha}{X} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}\\
            &= \winrA{\alpha\cup\beta}{X}
        \end{aligned}$

        In this proof, the definition of the choice game for Angel is applied first to transform the winning region into the union of the winning regions of games $\alpha$ and $\beta$. Then the IH is applied to both of these winning regions. The result is the definition of the winning region of the choice game in \dGL which proves the claim.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{\alpha;\beta}{X}{X^\C} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{det.}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{X^\C}}{\winrD{\beta}{X}{X^\C}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{\text{det.}}}{=}$}]{=}} \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}}{\winrD{\beta}{X}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{det.}}{=} \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}}{\winrA{\beta}{X}^\C}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{\text{det.}}}{=}$}]{=}} \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{det.}}}{=}\winrA{\alpha;\beta}{X}
        \end{aligned}$

        In the proof for the sequential game for Angel, the definition of its winning region is used first. Then, the IH is applied to the winning regions of Angel and Demon for game $\beta$. This transforms the winning regions into $\winrA{\beta}{X}$ and $\winrD{\beta}{X^\C}$ respectively. By using the determinacy of \dGL (indicated by "det." in the proof), $\winrD{\beta}{X^\C}$ can be transformed into $(\winrA{\beta}{X})^\C$. In this way, complementary goals for game $\alpha$ can be obtained so that the IH can be applied a second time. Doing so, transforms the term into the definition of the winning region of the sequential game in \dGL which proves the claim.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{\alpha^d}{X}{X^\C} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{det.}}}{=} \winrD{\alpha}{X^\C}{X}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{\text{det.}}}{=}$}]{=}} \winrD{\alpha}{X}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{det.}}{=} (\winrA{\alpha}{X^\C}^\C\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{det.}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^d}{X}
        \end{aligned}$

        Using determinacy also does the trick for the dual game for Angel. First, the definition of the winning region for Angel is used. Then, the IH is applied which transforms the winning region into $\winrD{\alpha}{X}$. This is transformed into $(\winrA{\alpha}{X^\C})^\C$ by using the determinacy of \dGL resulting in the definition of the dual game in \dGL. Consequently, the claim is proved.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{\alpha^*}{X}{X^\C} &= \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z\subseteq \s \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}\\
                &\cup \bigcap \{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (X \cap X^\C) \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &= \bigcap\{Z\subseteq \s \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{=} \bigcap\{Z\subseteq \s \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z} \subseteq Z\}\\
            &= \winrA{\alpha^*}{X}
        \end{aligned}$

        The definition of the winning region of the repetition game for Angel is a union of two sets. The first set corresponds to the definition of the repetition game in \dGL. The second set is empty because the intersection of $X$ and $X^\C$ is empty. Using the IH on the first set yields the definition of the winning region for the repetition game in \dGL which proves the claim.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha\cup \beta}{X}{X^\C} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{det.}}}{=} \begin{aligned}[t]
                &(\winrD{\alpha}{X}{X^\C} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X}{X^\C})\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\alpha}{X}{X^\C} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{X}{X^\C})\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\beta}{X}{X^\C} \cap \winrA{\beta}{X}{X^\C})
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{\text{det.}}}{=}$}]{=}}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &(\winrD{\alpha}{X^\C} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X^\C}\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\alpha}{X^\C}\cap \winrA{\alpha}{X})\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\beta}{X^\C} \cap \winrA{\beta}{X})
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{det.}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &(\winrD{\alpha}{X^\C} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X^\C}\\
                &\cup((\winrA{\alpha}{X})^\C \cap \winrA{\alpha}{X})\\
                &\cup ((\winrA{\beta}{X})^\C \cap \winrA{\beta}{X})
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{det.}}}{=} (\winrD{\alpha}{X^\C} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X^\C}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{det.}}}{=} \winrD{\alpha\cup\beta}{X^\C}
        \end{aligned}$

        The definition of the choice game for Demon consists of a union of three sets. All three sets are intersections of winning regions of smaller games. First, the IH is used on all of these winning regions. Using determinacy of \dGL shows that the last second intersections in the union are empty because complementary sets are intersected. The remaining intersection is the definition of the choice game in \dGL which proves the claim.

        The rest of the proofs work just the same as the corresponding proofs for Angel. Therefore, they are not commented.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha;\beta}{X}{X^\C} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{det.}}}{=} \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{X^\C}}{\winrD{\beta}{X}{X^\C}} \\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{\text{det.}}}{=}$}]{=}} \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}}{\winrD{\beta}{X^\C}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{det.}}{=} \winrD{\alpha}{(\winrD{\beta}{X^\C})^\C}{\winrD{\beta}{X^\C}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{\text{det.}}}{=}$}]{=}} \winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{X^\C}}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{det.}}}{=} \winrD{\alpha; \beta}{X^\C}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha^d}{X}{X^\C} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{det.}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha}{X^\C}{X}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{\text{det.}}}{=}$}]{=}} \winrA{\alpha}{X^\C}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{det.}}{=} (\winrD{\alpha}{(X^\C)^\C})^\C\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{det.}}}{=} \winrD{\alpha^d}{X^\C}
        \end{aligned}$
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha^*}{X}{X^\C} &=\begin{aligned}
                &\bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; Z \subseteq X^\C \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}\}\\
                &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (X \cap X^\C) \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &= \bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; Z \subseteq X^\C \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}\}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{=} \bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; Z \subseteq X^\C \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}\}\\
            &= \winrD{\alpha^*}{X^\C}
        \end{aligned}$
    \end{itemize}
    \qed
\end{proofE}

In the proof it is shown via structural induction that all parts in the semantics that differ between \dGLsc and \dGL cancel out, if the goals oppose.
\begin{remark}
    If there were a super-logic that contains both \dGLsc and \dGL, the previous lemma could be rendered as the syntactic equivalences
    \[\angel{\alpha}{P}{\lnot P} \leftrightarrow \angel{\alpha}{P} \quad\text{and}\quad \demon{\alpha}{\lnot P}{P} \leftrightarrow \demon{\alpha}{P}\]
\end{remark}

\begin{notation}
    In the following, $\angel{\alpha}{P}{\lnot P}$ will be abbreviated with $\angel{\alpha}{P}$ and $\demon{\alpha}{\lnot Q}{Q}$ with $\demon{\alpha}{Q}$, as well as $\winrA{\alpha}{X}{X^\C}$ with $\winrA{\alpha}{X}$ and $\winrD{\alpha}{Y^\C}{Y}$ with $\winrD{\alpha}{Y}$. These formulas express the same and have the same winning regions in \dGLsc and \dGL (Lem.~\ref{CollapseDGL}). The abbreviations allow for staying in the logic \dGLsc without having to write $P, Q, X, Y$ twice.
\end{notation}

Consequently, determinacy holds even if only for complementary goals, which will be useful for the proof calculus later on.
\begin{corollaryE}[Complementary Determinacy][end]
\label{determinancy}
    Determinacy holds if Angel and Demon have complementary goals, i.e. the following equivalence is valid:
    \[
    \lnot \angel{\alpha}{P}{\lnot P} \leftrightarrow \demon{\alpha}{P}{\lnot P}
    \]
\end{corollaryE}
\begin{proofE}
    To prove this corollary, it is shown that the winning regions of both sides of the equivalence are equal. Using Lemma \ref{CollapseDGL}, the winning region on the left-hand side of the equivalence is converted into a winning region of \dGL. As determinacy holds for \dGL, Angel's winning region can then be transformed into a winning region for Demon. Using Lemma \ref{CollapseDGL} again converts this winning region back to \dGLsc. The result is the winning region of the right-hand side of the equivalence which proves the claim.
    \[
    \begin{aligned}
        \winr{\lnot \angel{\alpha}{P}{\lnot P}} &\stackrel{\phantom{L. \ref{CollapseDGL}}}{\leftrightarrow}
        \winrA{\alpha}{\winr{P}}{\winr{P}^\C}^\C \\&\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{CollapseDGL}}}{\leftrightarrow} \winrA{\alpha}{\winr{P}}\\
        &\stackrel{\text{det.}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{L. \ref{CollapseDGL}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}\winrD{\alpha}{\winr{P}^\C} \\
        &\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{CollapseDGL}}}{\leftrightarrow} \winrD{\alpha}{\winr{P}}{\winr{P}^\C}\\
        &\stackrel{\phantom{L. \ref{CollapseDGL}}}{\leftrightarrow}\winr{\demon{\alpha}{P}{\lnot P}}
    \end{aligned}
    \]
    \qed
\end{proofE}

The next lemma proves another important relationship between \dGLsc and \dGL. It states that every winning region in \dGLsc can be rewritten to  \dGL winning regions. This shows that there are only two possibilities for the players to win a game: Either they can win against an adversarial player or both players cooperate and win the game. Cooperation is here expressed by letting Angel take all control (indicated by the $^{-d}$ which removes all ${}^d$ dual operators) and her trying to achieve both goals simultaneously. These equations also show that all of \dGLsc can be expressed in \dGL. Although this is possible, there are two winning regions in \dGL instead of one in \dGLsc which means double the amount of work for proving formulas. Additionally, \dGLsc offers a much more intuitive way of expressing games where both players have an individual goal.

\begin{lemmaE}[General \dGL Equivalence][end]
\label{rewriting}
    Winning regions in \dGLsc correspond to those in \dGL as follows:
    \[\winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y} = \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{X} \quad\text{and}\quad
    \winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} = \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Y}\]
    where $\alpha$ is a hybrid game, $X,Y$ are sets of states and $\alpha^{-d}$ is defined in Table~\ref{tab:d_def}.

    \begin{table}[b]
        \caption{Hybrid system $\alpha^{-d}$ is the \emph{systematization} of hybrid game $\alpha$}
        \label{tab:d_def}
    \begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{X X}
            $(x:=e)^{-d} \equiv x:=e$ & $(\alpha;\beta)^{-d} \equiv \alpha^{-d};\beta^{-d}$ \\
            $(x'=f(x)\& Q)^{-d} \equiv x'=f(x)\& Q$ & $(\alpha^d)^{-d} \equiv \alpha^{-d}$ \\
            $(?Q)^{-d} \equiv ?Q$ & $(\alpha^*)^{-d} \equiv (\alpha^{-d})^*$ \\
            $(\alpha \cup \beta)^{-d} \equiv \alpha^{-d} \cup \beta^{-d}$ & \\
    \end{tabularx} 
\end{table}
\end{lemmaE}
\begin{proofE}
    First, three small results are proven that are needed to complete the proof of Lemma \ref{rewriting}. These results prove some relationships between winning regions with games  with and without the $-d$ operator.

    The first result shows that if Angel and Demon can win against an adversarial player, they can win by playing together as a coalition. This is indicated by the fact that Angel can take over both controls via the $-d$ operator and has to reach the intersection of both goals.
    \begin{lemma}
    \label{dgl cut rewrite}
        It holds that:
        \[ \winrA{\alpha}{X} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Y}\subseteq \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \]
    \end{lemma}
    \begin{proof}
        The proof is done by induction over the structural complexity of $\alpha$.

        Induction start:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item $\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{x:=e}{X} \cap \winrD{x:=e}{Y}\\
                &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in X\} \cap \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in X\}\\
                &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in X\cap Y\}\\
                &= \winrA{x:=e}{X\cap Y}\\
                &= \winrA{(x:=e)^{-d}}{X\cap Y}
            \end{aligned} $\\
            For the assignment game, the claim holds true even for equality. Calculating the intersection of the winning regions directly yields $\winrA{x:=e}{X\cap Y}$. In the end, using the definition of the $-d$ operator proves the claim.
            \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{x'=f(x)\&Q}{X} \cap \winrD{x'=f(x)\&Q}{Y}\\
                &= \begin{aligned}[t]
                    &\{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}\\
                    &\cap \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in Y \text{ for all } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}
                \end{aligned}\\
                &\subseteq \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X\cap Y \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}\\
                &= \winrA{x'=f(x)\&Q}{X\cap Y}\\
                &= \winrA{(x'=f(x) \& Q)^{-d}}{X\cap Y}
            \end{aligned}$

            First, the definition of both winning regions is used. Now comes the most important step in this proof.
            \[\{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in X\cap Y \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q\}\] 
            is used as an upper bound of the intersection of the two original winning regions. The original intersection expresses that there has to exist an $r$ such that $X$ is true in the state $\varphi(r)$ and that for all $r$ the state $\varphi(r)$ needs to be in $Y$. Therefore, there exists a state where both $X$ and $Y$ are true. This is the state where $X$ is true, whose existence is guaranteed. Additionally, $Y$ is true in all states, consequently also in this one. By dropping the condition that $Y$ has to  be true for all states $\varphi(r)$ and only demanding that there exists an $r$ with $\varphi(r) \in X \cap Y$, an upper bound for the intersection is obtained. The last two steps then use the definition of the continuous game and the definition of the $-d$ operator respectively to prove the claim.
            \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{?Q}{X} \cap \winrD{?Q}{Y}\\
                &= (\winr{Q} \cap X) \cap (\winr{Q}^\C \cup Y)\\
                &= (\winr{Q} \cap X \cap Y ) \cup (\winr{Q} \cap X \cap \winr{Q}^\C)\\
                &= \winr{Q} \cap X \cap Y\\
                &= \winrA{?Q}{X\cap Y} \\
                &= \winrA{?Q^{-d}}{X\cap Y}
            \end{aligned}$

            Like the proof for the assignment game, the proof for the test game is also very straightforward. The claim can be proven directly with equality by calculating the intersection of Angel's and Demon's winning region and then using the definition of the $-d$ operator.
        \end{itemize}
        Induction hypothesis (IH):

        The claim holds true for all structurally less complex games.

        Induction step:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha\cup\beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\alpha\cup \beta}{Y}\\
                &= (\winrA{\alpha}{X} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}) \cap (\winrD{\alpha}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{Y})\\
                 &= (\winrA{\alpha}{X} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{Y}) \cup (\winrA{\beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{Y})\\
                 &\subseteq (\winrA{\alpha}{X} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Y}) \cup (\winrA{\beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\beta}{Y})\\
                 &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq}\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}\\ 
                 &= \winrA{\alpha^{-d}\cup \beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}\\
                 &= \winrA{(\alpha \cup \beta)^{-d}}{X\cap Y}
            \end{aligned}$

            In the proof for the choice game, the intersection of the two winning regions of Angel and Demon is calculated first. To bring the result into the right shape for using the IH, $\winrD{\alpha}{Y}$, respectively $\winrD{\beta}{Y}$ are dropped from the now two intersections, increasing the set. After that, the IH can be used on both intersections. By using the definition of the choice game, the result is transformed into the winning region $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}\cup \beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$. Finally, the claim can be proven by using the definition of the $-d$ operator.
            \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha; \beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\alpha; \beta}{Y}\\
                &= \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}) \cap \winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}}}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\beta}{Y}}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}\\
                &= \winrA{\alpha^{-d};\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}\\
                &= \winrA{(\alpha;\beta)^{-d}}{X\cap Y}
            \end{aligned}$\\
           First, the definition of both winning regions is used. Then, the IH is applied to the outer game $\alpha$ to obtain $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\beta}{Y}}$. Now, the IH can be used a second time for game $\beta$. After that, the definition of the sequential game and the $-d$ operator is used to transform the result into $\winrA{(\alpha;\beta)^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$ which proves the claim.
           \item
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha^d}{X} \cap \winrD{\alpha^d}{Y}\\
                &= (\winrA{\alpha}{X^\C})^\C \cap (\winrD{\alpha}{Y^\C})^\C\\
                &\stackrel{\text{det}}{=} \winrD{\alpha}{X} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{Y}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y}\\
                &= \winrA{(\alpha^d)^{-d}}{X\cap Y}
            \end{aligned}$

            In this proof, the definition of the dual game's winning region is applied first. Then determinacy is used to remove the complementary sets. Consequently, this yields $\winrD{\alpha}{X} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{Y}$. Now, the IH can be used as the intersection is commutative. Consequently, the order of $X$ and $Y$ does not matter. Applying the definition of the $-d$ operator proves the claim.
            \item 
            $\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha^*}{X} \cap \winrD{\alpha^*}{Y}\\
                &= \begin{aligned}[t]
                    &\bigcap\{Z_1 \subseteq \s \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z_1} \subseteq Z_1\}\\
                    &\cap \bigcup\{Z_2 \subseteq \s \;|\; Z_2 \subseteq Y \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2}\}
                \end{aligned}\\
                &\subseteq \begin{aligned}
                    &\bigcap\{Z_1 \subseteq \s \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z_1} \subseteq Z_1\}\\
                    &\cap \bigcap\{Z_2 \subseteq\s\;|\; Y\cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2} \subseteq Z_2\}
                \end{aligned}\\
                &= \bigcap\{Z_1, Z_2 \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z_1} \subseteq Z_1 \text{ and } Y\cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2} \subseteq Z_2\}\\
                &\subseteq \bigcap\{Z_1, Z_2 \;|\; (X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z_1}) \cap Y\cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2} \subseteq Z_1 \cap Z_2\}\\
                &= \bigcap\{Z_1, Z_2 \;|\ (X \cap Y\cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2}) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z_1}\cap Y\cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2}) \subseteq Z_1 \cap Z_2\}\\
                &\subseteq \bigcap\{Z_1, Z_2 \subseteq \s \;|\; (X\cap Y) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z_1} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2})\subseteq Z_1 \cap Z_2\}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\subseteq} \{Z_1, Z_2 \subseteq \s \;|\; (X \cap Y) \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z_1 \cap Z_2} \subseteq Z_1 \cap Z_2\}\\
                &= \{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (X\cap Y) \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z} \subseteq Z\}\\
                &= \winrA{(\alpha^{-d})^*}{X \cap Y}\\
                &= \winrA{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{X \cap Y}
            \end{aligned}$

            To prove the claim for the repetition game, first, the definitions for Angel's and Demon's winning regions are used. Now an upper bound for Demon's winning region of the repetition game has to be inserted. Here, Demon's greatest fixpoint is replaced by changing the union of all the pre-fixpoints to an intersection and stating that the pre-fixpoint should be a superset of $Y\cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2}$. Intuitively, intersecting all sets that are supersets of  $Y\cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2}$ will yield a greater or equal set than the union of all subsets of $Y\cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2}$. After bounding Demon's winning region by above, both fixpoints can be merged to a single one. This set is then bounded above by intersecting both sides of the two inequalities, yielding $(X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z_1}) \cap Y\cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2} \subseteq Z_1 \cap Z_2$. After expanding the left side, $(X \cap Y\cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2}) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z_1}\cap Y\cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2}) \subseteq Z_1 \cap Z_2$ is obtained. This can again be bound by above by dropping $\winrD{\alpha}{Z_2}$ and $Y$ respectively from the intersections. As a consequence, the IH can be used on $\winrA{\alpha}{Z_1} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Z_2}$. After that, $Z_1 \cap Z_2$ is renamed to $Z$. Then, the definition of the repetition game is used. To finally prove the claim, the definition of the $-d$ operator is applied to put the game into the right shape.
        \end{itemize}
        \qed
    \end{proof}
    The situation for the second result is as follows: Angel plays two games sequentially. In the first game, she does not play in a coalition with Demon. Afterwards, she can either play in a coalition with Demon or continue on her own. Additionally, it is known that she cannot win both games on her own. The lemma now states that this implies that she can play both games in a coalition with Demon.
    \begin{lemma}
    \label{incl_Angel}
        It holds that
        \[\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X \cap Y}} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}) \cap (\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}})^\C \subseteq \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X \cap Y}}\]
    \end{lemma}
    \begin{proof}
        \[
        \begin{aligned}
            &\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}} \cap (\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}})^\C\\
            &\stackrel{\text{det.}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{\subseteq}$}]{=}} \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{(\winrA{\beta}{X})^\C}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{det.}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{\subseteq}$}]{=}} \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}\cup \winrA{\beta}{X}} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{X^\C}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{\subseteq}$}]{\subseteq}}(\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{(\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}) \cap \winrD{\beta}{X^\C}}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X^\C}\cup (\winrA{\beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X^\C})} \\
            &\stackrel{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{\subseteq}\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{(\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X^\C}) \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X \cap X^{-d}}}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X^\C}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{Mon.}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{\subseteq}$}]{\subseteq}}\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}        \end{aligned}
        \]
        In this proof, the left-hand side of the set inclusion is directly transformed into the right-hand side. To do this, the complement set operator is dragged inside by using the determinacy from \dGL. Thus, both instances of Angel's winning region $\varsigma$ are transformed to Demon's winning region $\delta$ with the goal of $X^\C$. This yields Angel's winning region for $\alpha $ and Demon's winning region for $\alpha$, which can be merged to Angel's winning region for $\alpha^{-d}$ with Lemma \ref{dgl cut rewrite}. After that, the intersection that forms the new goal is expanded. One of the parts obtained is $\winrA{\beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X^\C}$ on which Lemma \ref{dgl cut rewrite} can be used again, yielding $\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X \cap X^\C}$. This is the empty set because the goal is empty and Angel cannot win automatically due to Demon failing a test or being unable to evolve an ODE because the whole game is under her control. Alternatively, the same insight can be produced by rewriting $\winrA{\beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X^\C}$ to $\winrA{\beta}{X} \cap (\winrA{\beta}{X})^\C$ using determinacy yielding the same result. Widening the set by dropping $\winrD{\beta}{X^\C}$ from the remaining part proves the claim. All these transformations inside the function $\varsigma_{\alpha^{-d}}$ preserve the set inclusion relation because the function is monotone.
        \qed
    \end{proof}
    This lemma states the same as the previous lemma but for Demon. Demon plays two games sequentially. He plays the first one without forming a coalition with Angel. For the second game he can choose to play in a coalition with Angel or to keep playing on his own. Additionally, it is known that he cannot win both games on his own. The lemma states that this implies that he can win both games while playing in a coalition with Angel.
    \begin{lemma}
    \label{incl_Demon}
        It holds that:
        \[
        \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cap (\winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}})^\C \subseteq \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X \cap Y}}
        \]
    \end{lemma}
    \begin{proof}
        \[
        \begin{aligned}
            &\winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cap (\winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}})^\C\\
            &\stackrel{\text{det.}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{\subseteq}$}]{=}} \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}^\C}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{det.}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{\subseteq}$}]{=}} \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{Y^\C}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{\subseteq} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cap \winrA{\beta}{Y^\C}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{(\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cap \winrA{\beta}{Y^\C}) \cup (\winrD{\beta}{Y} \cap \winrA{\beta}{Y^\C})}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{(\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cap \winrA{\beta}{Y^\C}) \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{Y \cap Y^\C}}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{\subseteq} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cap \winrA{\beta}{Y^\C}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{Mon.}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{Mon., L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{\subseteq}$}]{\subseteq}} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}
        \end{aligned}
        \]
        The idea for this proof is very similar to the one for Lemma \ref{incl_Angel}. First, the set complement is dragged inside the function by using determinacy, transforming Demon's winning region $\delta$ to Angel's winning region $\varsigma$. After that Lemma \ref{dgl cut rewrite} can be used. The resulting goal can be further transformed and upper bounded, yielding $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}$.
        \qed
    \end{proof}
    With all those preliminaries proven, the actual proof can be conducted. Both equalities are shown by structural induction over the complexity of the hybrid game $\alpha$.

    Induction start:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{x:=e}{X}{Y} &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega \winr{e}} \in X\}\\
            &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega \winr{e}} \in X \cup (X \cap Y)\}\\
            &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega \winr{e}} \in X\} \cup \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega \winr{e}} \in X\cap Y\}\\
            &= \winrA{x:=e}{X} \cup \winrA{x:=e}{X \cap Y}\\
            &= \winrA{(x:=e)^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cup \winrA{x:=e}{X}
        \end{aligned}$

        This proof is done by first using the definition of the assignment game for Angel. Inside the assignment game, the set $X$ is rewritten as $X \cup (X\cap Y)$. The winning region of the assignment game is then split into a union of two sets. The first set contains all states from which a state in $X$ can be reached by assigning the value of $e$ to $x$ while the second set contains the states from which $X\cap Y$ can be reached. These two sets are the definitions for the winning regions $\winrA{x:=e}{X}$ and $\winrA{x:=e}{X\cap Y}$. As the game $x:=e$ is the same as the game $(x:=e)^{-d}$ by definition of the $-d$ operator, the winning region $\winrA{x:=e}{X\cap Y}$ can be rewritten to $\winrA{(x:=e)^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$ to prove the claim.

        The next three proofs have the exact same proof idea of dividing the original winning region into two sets, one where the goal is $X$ and one where the goal is $X\cap Y$. Therefore, these proofs are not further commented.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{x'=f(x) \& Q}{X}{Y} &= \{\varphi(0) \in \s\;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q \}\\
            &= \{\varphi(0) \in \s\;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \cup (X \cap Y) \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q \}\\
            &=\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\{\varphi(0) \in \s\;|\; \varphi(r) \in X \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q \}\\
                &\cup \{\varphi(0) \in \s\;|\; \varphi(r) \in X\cap Y \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q \}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &= \winrA{x'=f(x) \& Q}{X} \cup \winrA{x'=f(x) \& Q}{X\cap Y}\\
            &= \winrA{(x'=f(x) \& Q)^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{x'=f(x) \& Q}{X}
         \end{aligned}$
         \item 
         $\begin{aligned}[t]
             \winrA{?Q}{X}{Y} &= \winr{Q} \cap X\\
             &= \winr{Q} \cap (X\cup (X \cap Y))\\
             &= (\winr{Q} \cap X) \cup (\winr{Q} \cap (X \cap Y))\\
             &= \winrA{?Q}{X} \cup \winrA{?Q}{X\cap Y}\\
             &= \winrA{(?Q)^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{?Q}{X}
         \end{aligned}$
         \item 
         $\begin{aligned}[t]
             \winrD{x:=e}{X}{Y} &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega \winr{e}} \in Y\}\\
             &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega \winr{e}} \in Y \cup (Y \cap X)\}\\
             &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega \winr{e}} \in Y\} \cup \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega \winr{e}} \in X\cap Y\}\\ 
             &=  \winrD{x:=e}{Y} \cup  \winrA{x:=e}{X \cap Y}\\
             &=  \winrA{(x:=e)^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cup  \winrD{x:=e}{Y}
         \end{aligned}$
         \item 
         $\begin{aligned}[t]
             \winrD{x'=f(x)\&Q}{X}{Y} &=\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\{\varphi(0) \in \s\;|\; \varphi(r) \in X\cap Y \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q \}\\
                &\cup \{\varphi(0) \in \s\;|\; \varphi(r) \in Y \text{ for all } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x) \land Q \}
             \end{aligned}\\
             &= \winrA{x'=f(x)\& Q}{X \cap Y} \cup \winrD{x'=f(x)\&Q}{Y}\\
             &= \winrA{(x'=f(x)\& Q)^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cup \winrD{x'=f(x)\&Q}{Y}
         \end{aligned}$
         In this proof, first, the definition of the semantics of the continuous game for Demon is applied. The two obtained sets are the definition of $\winrA{x'=f(x)\&Q}{X \cap Y}$ and $\winrA{x'=f(x)\&Q}{X}$, respectively. Then, applying the definition of the $-d$ operator proves the claim.
         \item 
         $\begin{aligned}[t]
             \winrD{?Q}{x}{Y} &= \winr{Q}^\C \cup Y\\
             &= \winr{Q}^\C \cup (Y \cup (Y\cap X))\\
             &= (\winr{Q}^\C \cup (X\cap Y)) \cup Y\\
             &= ((\winr{Q}^\C \cup (X\cap Y))\cap \s) \cup Y\\
             &= ((\winr{Q}^\C \cup (X\cap Y))\cap (\winr{Q}^\C \cup \winr{Q})) \cup Y\\
             &=(\winr{Q}^\C \cup (\winr{Q} \cap (X \cap Y))) \cup Y\\
             &= (\winr{Q} \cap (X \cap Y)) \cup (\winr{Q}^\C \cup Y)\\
             &= \winrA{?Q}{X \cap Y} \cup \winrD{?Q}{Y}\\
             &= \winrA{(?Q)^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cup \winrD{?Q}{Y}
         \end{aligned}$

         In this proof, the definition of the test game for Demon is applied first. The set $Y$ is then rewritten to $Y \cup (Y \cap X)$. Afterwards, the set $\winr{Q}^\C \cup (X\cap Y)$ is intersected with the set of all states $\s$, in the form of $\winr{Q}^\C \cup \winr{Q}$ to insert the set $\winr{Q}$. Expanding and simplifying the term yields $(\winr{Q} \cap (X \cap Y)) \cup (\winr{Q}^\C \cup Y)$ which is the definition of the union of the winning region $\winrA{?Q}{X \cap Y}$ and $\winrD{?Q}{Y}$. The definition of the $-d$ operator is then used to transform $\winrA{?Q}{X \cap Y}$ into $\winrA{(?Q)^{-d}}{X \cap Y}$ which proves the claim.
    \end{itemize}

    Induction hypothesis (IH):

    The claim holds for all structurally less complex games.

    Induction step:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{\alpha \cup \beta}{X}{Y} &= \winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{=}(\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{X}) \cup (\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X})\\
            &= (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{X} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X})\\
            &= \winrA{\alpha^{-d}\cup \beta^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha\cup \beta}{X}\\
            &= \winrA{(\alpha\cup\beta)^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha\cup \beta}{X}
        \end{aligned}$

        For the proof for the choice game for Angel, the definition of its semantics is used first. Thus, the union of the winning regions for games $\alpha$ and $\beta$ is obtained. These games are structurally smaller than the game $\alpha \cup \beta$ because they contain one choice game less. Therefore, the IH is applicable. After applying the IH, the order of the resulting winning regions is changed. This is not absolutely necessary but helps to see that $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$ is the definition of $\winrA{\alpha^{-d} \cup \beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$ and that $\winrA{\alpha}{X} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}$ is the definition of $\winrA{\alpha\cup \beta}{X}$. After applying these definitions, the definition of the $-d$ operator can be used to prove the claim.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{\alpha;\beta}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}}{\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=}$}]{=}} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=}$}]{=}} 
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{(\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}} \cap (\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}))\\
                &\cup \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} 
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup (\winrA{\beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\beta}{Y})}\\
                &\cup \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} 
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}\\
                &\cup (\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}}) \cap (\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}^\C})
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}} \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X})^\C}\\
                &\cup (\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{Mon.}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=}$}]{=}}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}} \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X})^\C}\\
                &\cup \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{incl_Angel}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}; \beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha;\beta}{X}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} \winrA{(\alpha;\beta)^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha;\beta}{X}
        \end{aligned}$

        In the proof for the sequential game, the definition of the semantics for the sequential game is used first. This decomposes the winning regions of the sequential game into winning regions of smaller games. On these winning regions, the IH is applied twice to transform all winning regions of \dGLsc into winning region of \dGL. For being able to transform the resulting winning regions into the winning region of $(\alpha;\beta)^{-d}$, the goals of the games $\alpha^{-d}$ and $\alpha$ have to be transformed in such a way that only $\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$, resp. $\winrA{\beta}{X}$ remains.

        In the goal for $\alpha^{-d}$, $\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$ is factored out. This transforms the goal into the form $\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup (\winrA{\beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\beta}{Y})$. Lemma \ref{dgl cut rewrite} states that $\winrA{\beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\beta}{Y}\subseteq\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$, i.e. the right-hand side of the union is included in the left-hand side. Consequently, $\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup (\winrA{\beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\beta}{Y})$ is just $\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$ which is the desired form of the goal.

        To bring the goal of the game $\alpha$ into the right format, the whole winning region is intersected with the set of all states $\s$ in the form of $\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}} \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}})^\C$. As $\s$ is the superset of any region, this transformation changes nothing. Expanding the resulting term yields \[(\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}} \cap (\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}}^\C) \cup (\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}) \cap \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}})\] $\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}}$ is a subset of $\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}}$ because, clearly, $\winrA{\beta}{X}$ is a subset of $\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}$. The subset relation of the two winning regions then follows by monotonicity. Therefore, the intersection of these two winning regions collapses to $\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}}$. Lemma \ref{incl_Angel} states that $(\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}} \cap (\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}})^\C)$ is a subset of $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}$. Consequently, the union of $(\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}} \cap (\winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}})^\C)$ and $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}$ collapses to $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}$.

        Now, all the terms have the right format to use the definition of the semantics of the sequential game. After that, the definition of the $-d$ operator is applied to finally prove the claim.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{\alpha^d}{X}{Y} &= \winrD{\alpha}{Y}{X}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{X}\\
            &= \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha^d}{X}\\
            &= \winrA{(\alpha^d)^{-d}}{X \cap Y}\cup \winrA{\alpha^d}{X}
        \end{aligned}$

        After using the definition of the semantics of the dual game, the IH is applied to the resulting winning region. First, the right-hand side of the union is transformed into the right format. This can be done easily by using the definition of the dual game in \dGL and determinacy. Thus, $\winrD{\alpha}{X}$ can directly be transformed into $\winrA{\alpha^d}{X}$. On the left-hand side, the definition of the $-d$ operator is applied to obtain $\winrA{(\alpha^d)^{-d}}{X \cap Y}$ which proves the claim.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrA{\alpha^*}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{CollapseDGL}}}}{=} \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\;(X\cap Y) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z\}\\
                &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq\s \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{ Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=}$}]{=}}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\;(X\cap Y) \cup ((\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z\cap Z} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}) \cap (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z\cap Z} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Z}))\subseteq Z\}\\
                &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq\s \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{ Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{CollapseDGL}}}}{=}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\;(X\cap Y) \cup (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z} \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z})) \subseteq Z\}\\
                &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq\s \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{ Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=} \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\;(X\cap Y) \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z} \subseteq Z \}\\
                &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq\s \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{ Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{CollapseDGL}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\;(X\cap Y) \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z} \subseteq Z \}\\
                &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq\s \;|\; X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z} \subseteq Z\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{CollapseDGL}}}}{=} \winrA{(\alpha^{-d})^*}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha^*}{X}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{CollapseDGL}}}}{=} \winrA{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha^*}{X}
        \end{aligned}$

        In the proof for the repetition game, the definition of the semantics of the repetition game is used first to obtain a union of two fixpoints. Then the IH is applied to all winning regions in that definition. In the term of the left-hand side fixpoint, $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$ can be factored out to transform $(\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z\cap Z} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}) \cap (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z\cap Z} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Z})$ into $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z} \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z})$. Lemma \ref{dgl cut rewrite} then states that this union is equivalent to $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z}$ because $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z}$ is a superset of $\winrA{\alpha}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}$. After that, Lemma \ref{CollapseDGL} is applied to the right-hand side fixpoint to transform $\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C}$ into $\winrA{\alpha}{Z}$. Now both fixpoints have the form of the semantics of the repetition game of \dGL. Applying this definition and afterwards the definition of the $-d$ operator proves the claim.

        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha\cup\beta}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &(\winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X}{Y})\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y})\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y} \cap \winrA{\beta}{X}{Y})
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=}$}]{=}}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &((\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Y}) \cap (\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}))\\
                &\cup ((\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Y}) \cap (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{X}))\\
                &\cup ((\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}) \cap (\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}))
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &(\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Y}) \cap (\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}))\\
                &\cup (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup (\winrD{\alpha}{Y} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{X}))\\
                &\cup (\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup (\winrD{\beta}{Y} \cap \winrA{\beta}{X}))
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &(\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Y}) \cap (\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}))\\
                &\cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &(\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y})\\
                &\cap (\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y})
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &(\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Y})\\
                &\cap (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y})
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}) \cup (\winrD{\alpha}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{Y})\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}\cup\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha\cup \beta}{Y}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} \winrA{(\alpha\cup\beta)^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha\cup \beta}{Y}
        \end{aligned}$

        For this proof, the definition of the semantics of the choice game for Demon is used first. Then, the obtained winning regions are transformed using the IH. This results in a union of three sets. From the last two sets $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$, respectively $\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$ are factored out. The terms for the sets are thus transformed to $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup (\winrD{\alpha}{Y} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{X})$, resp. $\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup (\winrD{\beta}{Y} \cap \winrA{\beta}{X})$. These two terms can be reduced to $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$, resp. $\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$ because Lemma \ref{dgl cut rewrite} confirms that $\winrD{\alpha}{Y} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{X}$, resp. $\winrD{\beta}{Y} \cap \winrA{\beta}{X}$ are subsets of those. Afterwards, the remaining term is expanded. After some simplifications, $(\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Y})
        \cap (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y})$ is obtained. From this term $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$ is factored out which yields the definitions for the winning regions $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}\cup\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}$ and $\winrD{\alpha\cup \beta}{Y}$. Applying the definition of the $-d$ operator to the former winning region proves the claim.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha;\beta}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y}}{\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=}$}]{=}} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta}{X}{Y} \cap \winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{X}{Y}}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=}$}]{=}}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{(\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\beta}{X}) \cap (\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y})}\\
                &\cup \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^-{d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup (\winrA{\beta}{X} \cap \winrD{\beta}{Y})}\\
                &\cup \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}\\
                &\cup \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cap (\winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}}^\C))
            \end{aligned}\\
             &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=}
             \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}}^\C)\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}})
             \end{aligned}\\
             &\stackrel{\text{Mon.}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=}$}]{=}}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}\\
                &\cup (\winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}}^\C)\\
                &\cup \winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}}
             \end{aligned}\\
             &\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{incl_Demon}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}}\\
             &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}; \beta^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha;\beta}{Y}\\
             &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} \winrA{(\alpha;\beta)^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha;\beta}{Y}
        \end{aligned}$

        The proof for the sequential game for Demon's winning region is very similar to the one for Angel's winning region. First, the definition of the semantics of the sequential game is used. Then, the IH is applied to all resulting winning regions in two steps. By using Lemma \ref{dgl cut rewrite}, the goal of the winning region on the left-hand side of the union is reduced to $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}$ just like in the proof for Angel's winning region. The idea for transforming the winning region on the right-hand side is also the same as for Angel. The winning region is intersected with the set of all states, now written as $\winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cup (\winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}})^\C$. The term is then expanded, yielding 
        \[(\winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cap (\winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}})^\C) \cup (\winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}}\] 
        The right-hand side collapses to $\winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}}$ because of monotonicity, just like in the proof for Angel. Afterwards, Lemma \ref{incl_Demon} states that $\winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\beta}{Y}} \cap (\winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\beta}{Y}})^\C$ is a subset of $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}$. Therefore, the union of the two sets collapses to $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{\beta^{-d}}{X\cap Y}}$. Afterwards the definition for the sequential game and the $-d$ operator can be applied to prove the claim.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha^d}{X}{Y} &= \winrA{\alpha}{Y}{X}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Y}\\
            &= \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha^d}{Y}\\
            &= \winrA{(\alpha^d)^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha^d}{Y}
        \end{aligned}$

        This proof also works similar to the proof for Angel's winning region for the dual game. First, the definition of the semantics for the dual game and the IH are used. Then, $\winrA{\alpha}{Y}$ is transformed to $\winrD{\alpha^d}{Y}$ by using the definition of the semantics of the dual game and determinacy of \dGL. Afterwards, the definition of the $-d$ operator is applied to the winning region on the left-hand side of the union to prove the claim.
        \item 
        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winrD{\alpha^*}{X}{Y} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (X\cap Y) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z\}\\
                &\cup \bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; Z \subseteq Y \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=}$}]{=}}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (X\cap Y) \cup ((\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z\cap Z} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}) \cap (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Z})) \subseteq Z\}\\
                &\cup \bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; Z \subseteq Y \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=}
            \begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (X\cap Y) \cup (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z} \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z})) \subseteq Z\}\\
                &\cup \bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; Z \subseteq Y \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (X\cap Y) \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z}\}\\
                &\cup \bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; Z \subseteq Y \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{CollapseDGL}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
                &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (X\cap Y) \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z}\}\\
                &\cup \bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; Z \subseteq Y \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}\}
            \end{aligned}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} \winrA{(\alpha^{-d})^*}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha^*}{Y}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}}}{=} \winrA{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha^*}{Y}
        \end{aligned}$

        This proof also works similar to the proof for Angel's winning region for the repetition game. First, the definition of the semantics of the repetition game is used to obtain the union of two fixpoints. Afterwards, the IH is applied to the winning regions inside the fixpoints. Then, $(\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z\cap Z} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}) \cap (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Z})$ from the left-hand side fixpoint is transformed into $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z} \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z})$ by factoring out $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z}$. This term collapses to $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z}$ because Lemma \ref{dgl cut rewrite} states that $\winrA{\alpha}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}$ is a subset of $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z}$. In the next step, Lemma \ref{CollapseDGL} is applied to the right-hand side fixpoint to transform $\winrD{\alpha}{Z^\C}{Z}$ to $\winrD{\alpha}{Z}$. Afterwards, the definition of the semantics for the repetition game in \dGL and the definition of the $-d$ operator can be applied to prove the claim.   
    \end{itemize}
    \qed
\end{proofE}

As a corollary for Lemma \ref{rewriting}, it can be stated that players will cooperate, iff both can win the game.
\begin{corollaryE}[Joint Cooperation][end]
\label{A+D}
    It holds that
    \[
    \winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y} = \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y}
    \]
\end{corollaryE}
\begin{proofE}
    \[
    \begin{aligned}
        \winrA{\alpha}{X}{Y} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{X}{Y}
        &\stackrel{L. \ref{rewriting}}{=} (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{X}) \cap (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y} \cup \winrD{\alpha}{Y})\\
        &\stackrel{\phantom{L. \ref{rewriting}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X \cap Y} \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{X} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Y})\\
        &\stackrel{L. \ref{dgl cut rewrite}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X\cap Y}
    \end{aligned}
    \]
    To prove the claim, the rewriting from Lemma \ref{rewriting} is used first and then $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X \cap Y}$ is factored out. As Lemma \ref{dgl cut rewrite}, states that $\winrA{\alpha}{X} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Y}$ is a subset of $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X \cap Y}$, the union collapses to $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{X \cap Y}$. This proves the claim.
    \qed
\end{proofE}

\section{Proof Calculus}

For making practical use of \dGLsc, a proof calculus is provided in this section. The proof calculus includes two axioms for each hybrid game, one for Angel and one for Demon. They correspond to the game's semantical definitions and decompose formulas into smaller parts.

Angel's assignment axiom $\asA$ replaces an assignment by a substitution of $x$ by $e$ for all unbound occurrences of $x$. $x$ is bound in the scope of quantifiers and modalities that contain $x:=$ or $x'=$ outside the scope of tests and evolution domain constraints because this might change the value of $x$.
In Angel's continuous axiom $\conA$, $x$ is replaced by the unique solution $x(t)$ of the ODE at time $t$ with $t$ being a fresh variable. $t$ is existentially quantified because Angel can choose when to stop evolving the ODE.
Angel's test axiom $\tA$ states that she can win the game if both $R$ and $P$ are true, i.e. she passes the test and is in her goal.
Angel's choice axiom $\chA$ states that she has a winning strategy, if she has one for $\alpha$ or $\beta$.
Angel's sequential axiom $\seqA$ states that she has a winning strategy if she can achieve $\angel{\beta}{P}{Q}$ for $\alpha$ while knowing that Demon's goal is $\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}$.
Angel's dual axiom $\dA$ states that she can win the dual game if Demon can win $\alpha$ with her goal $P$ because the control swap is simulated by Demon playing for Angel with her goals.
Angel's repetition axiom $\rA$ states that Angel can win the game if she is in her goal now or if she can win the repetition game after one round of $\alpha$. This axiom can be used multiple times to "unwind" the loop, until a point is reached where $P$ is true.

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{>{\color{gray}}l X}
        $\asA$ & $\angel{x:=e}{p(x)}{q(x)} \leftrightarrow p(e)$ \\
        $\conA$ & $\angel{x'=f(x)}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow \exists t{\geq} 0\, \angel{x:= x(t)}{P}{Q}$ \hfill $(x' = f(x))$ \\
        $\tA$ & $\angel{?R}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow R \land P$ \\
        $\chA$ & $\angel{\alpha\cup\beta}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow \angel{\alpha}{P}{Q} \lor \angel{\beta}{P}{Q}$ \\
        $\seqA$ & $\angel{\alpha; \beta}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow \angel{\alpha}{\angel{\beta}{P}{Q}}{\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}}$ \\
        $\dA$ & $\angel{\alpha^d}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow \demon{\alpha}{Q}{P}$ \\
        $\rA$ & $\angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow P \lor \angel{\alpha; \alpha^*}{P}{Q}$ \\
        $\FP$ & \AxiomC{$P\lor \angel{\alpha}{R_1}\rightarrow R_1$}
        \AxiomC{$(P\land Q) \lor (\angel{\alpha}{R_2}{R_2} \land \demon{\alpha}{R_2}{R_2})   \rightarrow R_2$}
        \BinaryInfC{$\angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q} \rightarrow R_1 \lor R_2$}
        \DisplayProof \\
        $\asD$ & $\demon{x:=e}{p(x)}{q(x)} \leftrightarrow q(e)$ \\
        $\conD$ & $\demon{x'=f(x)}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow \forall t{\geq} 0\,\demon{x{:}{=} x(t)}{P}{Q} \lor \exists t{\geq} 0\,\angel{x{:}{=} x(t)}{P \land Q}{Q}$ \hfill$(x' = f(x))$ \\
        $\tD$ & $\demon{?R}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow \lnot R \lor Q$ \\
        $\chD$ & $\demon{\alpha \cup \beta}{P}{Q}\leftrightarrow \big(\demon{\alpha}{P}{Q} \land \demon{\beta}{P}{Q}\big) \lor \big(\demon{\alpha}{P}{Q} \land \angel{\alpha}{P}{Q}\big) \lor \big(\demon{\beta}{P}{Q} \land \angel{\beta}{P}{Q}\big)$ \\
