Demon's assignment axiom $\asD$ replaces the assignment by a substitution of $x$ by $e$. 
Demon's continuous axiom $\conD$ states that Demon can win the game if his goal is fulfilled for any value $x$ can take by following the ODE or there is a time $t$ where both Angel and Demon win. In the latter case, the assignment game only evaluates Angel's goal $P\land Q$, so Demon's goal can stay just $Q$.
Demon's test axiom $\tD$ states that he can win the test game if $R$ is false because then Angel fails the test, and he wins automatically, or he can win because his goal $Q$ is true.
Demon's choice axiom $\chD$ states that he has a winning strategy if he has a one for both $\alpha$ and $\beta$ or if he and Angel both have a winning strategy for the same game. In the former case, Demon can win no matter what game Angel chooses. In the latter case, Demon and Angel will cooperate to win.
Demon's sequential axiom $\seqD$ states that Demon has a winning strategy if he can achieve $\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}$ for $\alpha$ while knowing that Angel's goal is $\angel{\beta}{P}{Q}$.
Demon's repetition axiom $\rD$ states that Demon can win the game if either Demon's and Angel's goals are fulfilled now or both can win the repetition game after one more round of $\alpha$ or Demon' goal $Q$ is true now, and he can win the repetition game after another round of $\alpha$ as well.

Additionally, there is the determinacy axiom $\det$ that links Angel and Demon's modality, if they have opposing goals.
The fixpoint rule \FP{} is, like the other rules, written with the premises at the top and the conclusion at the bottom. The rule characterizes $\angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$ as the union of two least fixpoints. If $R_1$ is a pre-fixpoint for the first fixpoint and $R_2$ is a pre-fixpoint for the second, then $R_1\lor R_2$ holds whenever $\angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$ holds. As a subtle detail, $R_1$ and $R_2$ do not need to be the same because Angel can rarely achieve the same by competing and by cooperating with Demon. Unlike $\rA$, the rule does not feature more instances of repetition games in the premises. Demon's induction rule is not included because it can be derived from \FP{}.
The other two proof rules correspond to the two monotonicity lemmas. $\MA$ states that Angel's winning region can be expanded by increasing both goals. $\MAtwo$ states that her winning region can also be increased by only increasing her goal, and changing Demon's goal arbitrarily if their goals are disjunct initially. Unlike the axioms, the monotonicity rules for Demon can be derived by using $\dA$. For the monotonicity rules, it is irrelevant that this changes the game. But for Demon's axioms, the dual game introduced by $\dA$ prevents the use any other axiom to derive them.

Additionally, the proof calculus includes all rules for first-order logic. For reasons of readability and space limitations, these rules are not listed. Apart from the first-order rules, the proof calculus' rules are listed in Fig. \ref{dglA_calculus}.

To be of any use, soundness of the proof calculus is crucial. Soundness means that all formulas that can be proven in the proof calculus are actually valid. If a false formula could be proven, anything could be, rendering it useless. Therefore, the soundness of the proof calculus is proven in the following theorem:
\begin{theoremE}[Soundness][end]
\label{soundness}
    The \dGLsc proof calculus is sound.
\end{theoremE}
\begin{proofE}
    The proof is done by showing that the semantics of the left-hand side and the right-hand side of all equivalences match. For the proof rules it is proved that the semantics of the premise is a subset of the semantics of the conclusion.
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Proof for rule $\asA$:

        $\begin{aligned}[t]
            \winr{\angel{x:=e}{p(x)}{q(x)}} &= \winrA{x:=e}{\winr{p(x)}}{\winr{q(x)}}\\
            &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in \winr{p(x)}\}\\
            &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega \in \winr{p(e)}\}\\
            &= \winr{p(e)}
        \end{aligned}$

        To prove the rule for the assignment game, the definition of the winning region is used on the left-hand side of the equivalence first. This yields a set consisting of all states $\omega$ so that $\omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in \winr{p(x)}$. Instead of asking if $p(x)$ holds in a state where the value of $x$ has been replaced by the value of $e$ in $\omega$, it can directly be asked if $p(e)$ holds in $\omega$. The set of all states that are in $\winr{p(e)}$, is just $\winr{p(e)}$ which proves the claim.
    \item Proof for rule $\conA$:

    To prove the equality of the semantics for the continuous game, it is proven that both set inclusions hold. Expanding the right-hand side of the equality yields:
    \[
    \begin{aligned}[t]
        \winr{\exists t\geq 0 \angel{x:=e}{P}{Q}} &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_t^r \in \winr{\angel{x:=x(t)}{P}{Q}} \text{ for some } r\geq 0\}\\
        &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_t^r \in \winrA{x:=x(t)}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}} \text{ for some } r\geq 0\}\\
        &= \{ \omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_t^r \in \{\nu \in \s \;|\; \nu_x^{\nu\winr{x(t)}} \in \winr{P}\} \text{ for some } r\geq 0\}\\
        &= \{\omega \in \s \;|\; (\omega_t^r)_x^{\omega_t^r\winr{x(t)}} \in \winr{P} \text{ for some } r\geq0\}
    \end{aligned}
    \]
    Define now the function $\varphi(s) = (\omega_t^s)_x^{\omega_t^s\winr{x(t)}}$. This is a function of states. All these states are equal except that the value of $x$ is $x(s)$ in state $\varphi(s)$. The condition $\varphi \models x'=f(x)$ is fulfilled by $\varphi$ because $x'= f$ by assumption. As the semantics of the left-hand side of the equality is
    \[
    \winr{\angel{x'=f(x)}{P}{Q}} = \{\varphi(0) \in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in \winr{P} \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x)\}
    \]
    the semantics of the right-hand side is included in it. The other inclusion holds because the solution of $x'=f(x)$ is unique as proven in \cite{Platzer10}.
    Therefore, both inclusions hold which proves the claim.
    \item Proof for rule $\tA$:

    $\begin{aligned}[t]
        \winr{\angel{?R}{P}{Q}} &= \winrA{?R}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}\\
        &= \winr{R} \cap \winr{P}\\
        &= \winr{R \land P}
    \end{aligned}$

    To prove this rule, the definition of the winning region is used on the left-hand side of the equivalence. After breaking it down to $\winr{R} \cap \winr{P}$, the definition of the semantics for AND is used to prove the claim.

    The next few proofs follow the same proof idea. First break the semantics of the left-hand side of the equation down to its definition. Then reassemble the sets to match the semantics of the right-hand side. Therefore, these proofs are left uncommented.
    \item Proof for rule $\chA$:

    $\begin{aligned}[t]
        \winr{\angel{\alpha\cup\beta}{P}{Q}} &= \winrA{\alpha\cup\beta}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}\\
        &= \winrA{\alpha}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}} \cup \winrA{\beta}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}\\
        &= \winr{\angel{\alpha}{P}{Q}} \cup \winr{\angel{\beta}{P}{Q}}\\
        &= \winr{\angel{\alpha}{P}{Q} \lor \angel{\beta}{P}{Q}}
    \end{aligned}$
    \item Proof for rule $\seqA$:

    $\begin{aligned}[t]
        \winr{\angel{\alpha;\beta}{P}{Q}} &= \winrA{\alpha;\beta}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}\\
        &= \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}}{\winrD{\beta}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}}\\
        &= \winrA{\alpha}{\winr{\angel{\beta}{P}{Q}}}{\winr{\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}}}\\
        &= \winr{\angel{\alpha}{\angel{\beta}{P}{Q}}{\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}}}
    \end{aligned}$
    \item Proof for rule $\dA$:

    $\begin{aligned}[t]
        \winr{\angel{\alpha^d}{P}{Q}} &= \winrA{\alpha^d}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}\\
        &= \winrD{\alpha}{\winr{Q}}{\winr{P}}\\
        &= \winr{\demon{\alpha}{Q}{P}}
    \end{aligned}$
    \item Proof for rule $\rA$:

    $\begin{aligned}[t]
        \winr{\angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{rewriting}}}}{=} \winrA{\alpha^*}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}\\
        &\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{rewriting}}}{=} \winrA{(\alpha^{-d})^*}{\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q}}{(\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q})^\C} \cup \winrA{\alpha^*}{\winr{P}}{(\winr{P})^\C} \\
        &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{rewriting}}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
            &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q}) \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}\\
            &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; ((\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q}) \cap (\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q})^\C) \cup (\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha^{-d}}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z\}\\
            &\cup \bigcap \{Z\subseteq \s\;|\; \winr{P} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C}\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C}\subseteq Z\}\\
            &\cup \bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (\winr{P} \cap \winr{P}^\C) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z}\cup \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z\}
        \end{aligned}\\
        &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{rewriting}}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
            &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q}) \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z}{Z^\C} \subseteq Z\}\\
            &\cup \bigcap \{Z\subseteq \s\;|\; \winr{P} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z^\C}\subseteq Z\}
        \end{aligned}\\
        &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{rewriting}}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
            &(\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q}) \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q}}{\\
            &(\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q})^\C}}{\winrA{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q}}{(\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q})^\C}^\C}\\
            &\cup \winr{P} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\alpha^*}{\winr{P}}{\winr{P}^\C}}{\winrA{\alpha^*}{\winr{P}}{\winr{P}^\C}^\C}
        \end{aligned}\\
        &\stackrel{\text{C. \ref{determinancy}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
            &\winr{P} \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q}}{(\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q})^\C}}{\winrD{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q}}{(\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q})^\C}}\\
            &\cup \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\alpha^*}{\winr{P}}{\winr{P}^\C}}{\winrD{\alpha^*}{\winr{P}}{, \winr{P}^\C}}
        \end{aligned}\\
        &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{rewriting}}}}{=} \winr{P} \cup \winrA{(\alpha; \alpha^*)^{-d}}{\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q}}{(\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q})^\C} \cup \winrA{\alpha;\alpha^{*}}{\winr{P}}{\winr{P}^\C}\\
        &\stackrel{\text{L. \ref{rewriting}}}{=} \winr{P} \cup \winrA{\alpha; \alpha^*}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}\\
        &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{L. \ref{rewriting}}}}{=} \winr{P \lor \angel{\alpha; \alpha^*}{P}{Q}}
    \end{aligned}$

    To prove the rule for the repetition game for Angel, the winning region on the left-hand side of the equality is split using Lemma \ref{rewriting}. Then, the definition of the winning region for the repetition game is used. Two fixpoints in the resulting term can be eliminated because they are empty, namely the ones containing $\winr{P} \cap \winr{P}^\C$ and $(\winr{P}\cap \winr{Q}) \cap (\winr{P}\cap \winr{Q})^\C$. After that, only two least fixpoints left are left. One property of these fixpoints is that the equation $\winrA{\alpha^*}{X}{X^\C} = X \cup \winrA{\alpha}{\winrA{\alpha^*}{X}{X^\C}}{\winrA{\alpha^*}{X}{X^\C}^C}$ holds. Therefore, the fixpoints are replaced with the respective right-hand side in the next step. Afterwards, determinacy is used to get rid of the complement sets in the goals. Finally, the definition of the sequential game and Lemma \ref{rewriting} are applied again to bring the winning region into the same shape as the one on the right-hand side of the equivalence.
    \item Proof for rule $\FP$:

    Assume that the two premises are valid, i.e. $P\lor \angel{\alpha}{R_1}\rightarrow R_1$ and $(P\land Q)\lor (\angel{\alpha}{R_2}{R_2} \land \demon{\alpha}{R_2}{R_2}) \rightarrow R_2$ are true. This means that $\winr{P} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{\winr{R_1}} \subseteq \winr{R_1}$ holds. Consequently, $\winr{R_1}$ is a pre-fixpoint. Therefore, $\winr{R_1}$ is a superset of $\bigcap\{Z\subseteq \s\;|\; \winr{P} \cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}\subseteq Z\}$ because this set is the intersection of all pre-fixpoints. For a similar reason, $\winr{R_2}$ is a superset of $\bigcap\{Z \subseteq\s \;|\; (\winr{P}\cap \winr{Q}) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z\}$. Taken together, this yields: 
    \[
    \begin{aligned}
        \winrA{\alpha^*}{P}{Q} &=
        \begin{aligned}[t]
            &\bigcap\{Z\subseteq \s\;|\; \winr{P}\\
            &\cup \winrA{\alpha}{Z}\subseteq Z\} \cup\bigcap\{Z \subseteq\s \;|\; (\winr{P}\cap \winr{Q}) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z\}
        \end{aligned}\\
        &\subseteq \winr{R_1} \cup \winr{R_2}
    \end{aligned}
    \]
    which is the conclusion of the proof rule. Therefore, the conclusion is valid.
    \item Proof for rule $\asD$:

    \[\begin{aligned}[t]
        \winr{\demon{x:=e}{p(x)}{q(x)}} &= \winrD{x:=e}{\winr{p(x)}}{\winr{q(x)}}\\
        &= \{\omega\in \s \;|\; \omega_x^{\omega\winr{e}} \in \winr{p(x)} \}\\
        &= \{\omega\in \s\;|\; \omega \in \winr{p(e)} \}\\
        &= \winr{p(e)}
    \end{aligned}\]
    This proof works exactly like the proof for the assignment rule for Angel because the semantics of the assignment game is similar for both.

    \item Proof for rule $\conD$:

    To prove the claim for the continuous game for Demon, the semantics of the left-hand side and the right-hand side of the equality are expanded. This yields
    \[\begin{aligned}[t]
        \winr{\demon{x'=f(x)}{P}{Q}} &= \{\varphi(0)\in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in \winr{Q} \text{ for all } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x)\}\\
        &\cup \{\varphi(0) \in \s\;|\; \varphi(r) \in \winr{P} \cap \winr{Q} \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi\models x'=f(x)\} 
    \end{aligned}
    \]
    and
    \[\begin{aligned}[t]
        &\winr{\forall t{\geq} 0 \,\demon{x:=x(t)}{P}{Q} \lor \exists t\geq 0 \angel{x:=x(t)}{P\land Q}{Q}} =\\
        &\{\omega \in \s\;|\; \omega_t^r \in \winr{\demon{x:=x(t)}{P}{Q}} \text{ for all } r\geq 0\} \\
        &\cup \{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_t^r \in \winr{\angel{x:=x(t)}{P\land Q}{Q}} \text{ for some }r\geq 0\}
    \end{aligned}\]
    As can be seen, both results consist of a union of two sets. It will therefore be proven that these sets correspond to each other in order to prove the claim. First, the set $\{\omega \in \s\;|\; \omega_t^r \in \winr{\demon{x:=x(t)}{P}{Q}} \text{ for all } r\geq 0\}$ will be expanded further.
    \[
    \begin{aligned}
       \{\omega \in \s\;|\; \omega_t^r \in \winr{\demon{x:=x(t)}{P}{Q}} \text{ for all } r\geq 0\} &= \{\omega \in \s\;|\; \omega_t^r \in \{\nu \in\s\;|\; \nu_x^{\nu\winr{x(t)}} \in \winr{Q}\}\text{ for all } r\geq 0\}\\
       &= \{\omega\in\s\;|\; (\omega_t^r)_x^{\omega_r^t\winr{x(t)}} \in \winr{Q} \text{ for all } r\geq 0\}
    \end{aligned}
    \]
    Now, the function can be defined as $\varphi(s) = (\omega_t^s)_x^{\omega_t^s\winr{x(t)}}$. Just like in the proof for Angel's winning region for the continuous game, this is a function of states. The states only differ in the value of $x$ which is $x(s)$ in the state $\varphi(s)$. This function also satisfies $\varphi \models x'=f(x)$ because $x'(t) = f(x)$ by definition. Consequently, the expanded set is a subset of $\{\varphi(0)\in \s \;|\; \varphi(r) \in \winr{Q} \text{ for all } r \text{ with } \varphi \models x'=f(x)\}$. The other inclusion holds because the solution of $x'=f(x)$ is unique, as proven in \cite{Platzer10}.

    The other set in the union $\{\varphi(0) \in \s\;|\; \varphi(r) \in \winr{P} \cap \winr{Q} \text{ for some } r \text{ with } \varphi\models x'=f(x)\}$ which can also be written as $\winrA{x'=f(x)}{\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q}}{\winr{Q}}$. In the proof for Angel's continuous axiom, it has already been proven that this winning region is equal to the set $\{\omega \in \s \;|\; \omega_t^r \in \winr{\angel{x:=x(t)}{P\land Q}{Q}} \text{ for some }r\geq 0\}$. Consequently, the claim is proven.

    The next few proofs consist of using the definitions of the winning regions to first disassemble the left-hand side of the equivalence and then reassemble it in the form of the right-hand side, just like in the proofs for Angel. Therefore, these proofs are not commented.

    \item Proof for rule $\tD$:

    $\begin{aligned}[t]
        \winr{\demon{?R}{P}{Q}} &= \winrD{?R}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}\\
        &= \winr{R}^\C \cup \winr{Q}\\
        &= \winr{\lnot R} \cup \winr{Q}\\
        &= \winr{\lnot R \lor Q}
    \end{aligned}$
    \item Proof for rule $\chD$:

    $\begin{aligned}[t]
        \winr{\demon{\alpha \cup \beta}{P}{Q}} &= \winrD{\alpha \cup \beta}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}\\
        &= \begin{aligned}[t]
            &(\winrD{\alpha}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}} \cap \winrD{\beta}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}})\\
            &\cup (\winrD{\alpha}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}} \cap \winrA{\alpha}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}})\\
            &\cup (\winrD{\beta}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}} \cap \winrA{\beta}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}})
        \end{aligned}\\
        &=\begin{aligned}[t]
            &(\winr{\demon{\alpha}{P}{Q}} \cap \winr{\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}})\\
            &\cup (\winr{\demon{\alpha}{P}{Q}} \cap \winr{\angel{\alpha}{P}{Q}})
        \end{aligned}\\
        &= (\winr{\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}} \cap \winr{\angel{\beta}{P}{Q}})\\
        &= \winr{(\demon{\alpha}{P}{Q} \land \demon{\beta}{P}{Q}) \lor (\demon{\alpha}{P}{Q} \land \angel{\alpha}{P}{Q}) \lor (\demon{\beta}{P}{Q} \land \angel{\beta}{P}{Q})}
    \end{aligned}$
    \item Proof for rule $\seqD$:

    $\begin{aligned}[t]
        \winr{\demon{\alpha;\beta}{P}{Q}} &= \winrD{\alpha;\beta}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}\\
        &= \winrD{\alpha}{\winrA{\beta}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}}{\winrD{\beta}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}}\\
        &= \winrD{\alpha}{\winr{\angel{\beta}{P}{Q}}}{\winr{\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}}}\\
        &=\winr{\demon{\alpha}{\angel{\beta}{P}{Q}}{\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}}}
    \end{aligned}$
    \item Proof for rule $\dD$:\\
    $\begin{aligned}[t]
        \winr{\demon{\alpha^d}{P}{Q}} &= \winrD{\alpha^d}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}\\
        &= \winrA{\alpha}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}\\
        &= \winr{\angel{\alpha}{P}{Q}}
    \end{aligned}$
    \item Proof for rule $\rD$:

    $\begin{aligned}
        \winr{\demon{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}} &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{C. \ref{A+D}}}}{=} \winrD{\alpha^*}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}}\\
        &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{C. \ref{A+D}}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
            &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (\winr{P}\cap \winr{Q}) \cup (\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}) \subseteq Z\}\\
            &\cup \bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; \winr{Q} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z} \subseteq Z\}
        \end{aligned}\\
        &\stackrel{\text{C. \ref{A+D}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
            &\bigcap\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; (\winr{P}\cap \winr{Q}) \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z} \subseteq Z\}\\
            &\cup \bigcup\{Z \subseteq \s \;|\; \winr{Q} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z} \subseteq Z\}
        \end{aligned}\\
        &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{C. \ref{A+D}}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
            &(\winr{P}\cap \winr{Q}) \cup \winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{\winrA{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{\winr{P}\cap \winr{Q}}}\\
            &\cup (\winr{Q} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{\winrD{\alpha^*}{\winr{Q}}})
        \end{aligned}\\
        &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{C. \ref{A+D}}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
            &(\winr{P}\cap \winr{Q}) \cup \winrA{(\alpha;\alpha^*)^{-d}}{\winr{P}\cap \winr{Q}}\\
            &\cup (\winr{Q}\cap \winrD{\alpha;\alpha^*}{\winr{Q}})
        \end{aligned}\\
        &\stackrel{\text{C. \ref{A+D}}}{=}\begin{aligned}[t]
            &(\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q}) \cup (\winrA{\alpha; \alpha^*}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}} \cap \winrD{\alpha; \alpha^*}{\winr{P}}{\winr{Q}})\\
            &\cup (\winr{Q}\cap \winrD{\alpha;\alpha^*}{\winr{Q}})
        \end{aligned}\\
        &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{C. \ref{A+D}}}}{=}\winr{(P\land Q) \lor (\angel{\alpha;\alpha^*}{P}{Q}\land \demon{\alpha; \alpha^*}{P}{Q}) \lor  (Q \land \demon{\alpha; \alpha^*}{Q})}
    \end{aligned}$

    First, the definition of the repetition game for Demon is used. Afterwards, Corollary \ref{A+D} is applied to rewrite $\winrA{\alpha}{Z}{Z} \cap \winrD{\alpha}{Z}{Z}$ to $\winrA{\alpha^{-d}}{Z}$. Then, the properties of the two fixpoints are used: The least fixpoint is equal to $(\winr{P} \cap \winr{Q})\cup \winrA{(\alpha;\alpha^*)^{-d}}{\winr{P}\cap \winr{Q}}$. The greatest fixpoint is equal to $\winr{Q} \cap \winrD{\alpha;\alpha^*}{\winr{Q}}$. After this step, Corollary \ref{A+D} is applied again to get rid of the $-d$ operator and put everything back into one set to obtain the right-hand side of the equivalence.
    \item Proof for rule $\MA$:

    Assume that the two premises are valid. Consequently, $\winr{P_1} \subseteq \winr{P_2}$ and $\winr{Q_1} \subseteq \winr{Q_2}$. According to Lemma \ref{monotonicity}, this implies that $\winrA{\alpha}{\winr{P_1}}{\winr{Q_2}} \subseteq \winrA{\alpha}{\winr{P_2}}{\winr{Q_2}}$. Therefore, the conclusion $\angel{\alpha}{P_1}{Q_2} \rightarrow \angel{\alpha}{P_2}{Q_2}$ is valid.
    \item Proof for rule $\MAtwo$:

        Assume that the two premises are valid. Then, $\winr{P_1} \subseteq \winr{P_2}$. Additionally, $\winr{P_1} \cap \winr{Q_1} \subseteq \emptyset$ which implies $\winr{P_1} \cap \winr{Q_1} = \emptyset$ because the other inclusion is trivial. Therefore, all preconditions of Lemma \ref{mon2} are fulfilled. According to Lemma \ref{mon2}, this implies that $\winrA{\alpha}{\winr{P_1}}{\winr{Q_1}} \subseteq \winrA{\alpha}{\winr{P_2}}{\winr{Q_2}}$ holds. Consequently, the conclusion of the proof rule holds which proves that the proof rule is valid.
    \item Proof for rule $\detR$:

    This rule is a direct consequence of Corollary \ref{determinancy}.
    \end{itemize}
    \qed
\end{proofE}

For easier reasoning, a few more proof rules are added below. These rules are all admissible or derivable from the rules in the proof calculus.
The axiom $\langle\land]$ can be derived using the complementarization axioms (Theorem \ref{Complementarization}). It states that Angel and Demon will cooperate, if both can win the game. This axiom also corresponds to C. \ref{A+D}.
Using $\langle\land]$, some more rules for the repetition game can be derived. The rules $\langle^*]$ and $\FPtwo$ cover the cases when both Angel and Demon can win the repetition game. The rule ind is an induction rule for Demon for the repetition game.
The axiom $\langle \land ]_2$ is also admissible. It states that Angel and Demon can cooperate, if each of them can win against an adversarial player. Thus, both do not care about the other's moves, so they can as well work together. Note that the other implication does not hold.
Using $\langle\land]_2$, the axioms $\spA$, $\id$, $\re$ and $\reD$ can be derived. These rules help handle the $-d$ operator and are mainly used to facilitate the proof for Theorem \ref{Complementarization}.

\begin{lemmaE}[][end]
    \label{devRules}
    The following rules are admissible or derivable in the proof calculus:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item {\makebox[\cuswidth]{$\impAD$\hfill} $\angel{\alpha}{P} \land \demon{\alpha}{Q} \rightarrow \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}$}
        \item {\makebox[\cuswidth]{$\spA$\hfill} $\angel{\alpha}{(\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}\lor \angel{\beta}{P})} \land \lnot \angel{\alpha}{\angel{\beta}{P}} \rightarrow \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}}$}
        \item {\makebox[\cuswidth]{$\id$\hfill} $\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \leftrightarrow \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor (\angel{\alpha}{P} \land \demon{\alpha}{Q})$}
        \item {\makebox[\cuswidth]{$\re$\hfill}$\begin{aligned}[t]
            &\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \lor \angel{\alpha}{(\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta}{P})}\\[-3pt]
            &\leftrightarrow \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \lor \angel{\alpha}{\angel{\beta}{P}}
        \end{aligned}$}
        \item {\makebox[\cuswidth]{$\reD$\hfill} $\begin{aligned}[t]
            &\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \lor \demon{\alpha}{(\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\beta}{Q})}\\[-3pt]
            &\leftrightarrow \angel{\alpha}{\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \lor \demon{\alpha}{\demon{\beta}{Q}}
        \end{aligned}$}
        \item {\makebox[\cuswidth]{$\AD$\hfill} $\angel{\alpha}{P}{Q} \land \demon{\alpha}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P \land Q)}$}
        \item {\makebox[\cuswidth]{$\langle^*]$\hfill} $\angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}\land \demon{\alpha^*}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow (P\land Q) \lor (\angel{\alpha;\alpha^*}{P}{Q} \land \demon{\alpha;\alpha^*}{P}{Q})$}
        \item \AxiomC{$(P\land Q) \lor (\angel{\alpha}{R}{R} \land \demon{\alpha}{R}{R}) \rightarrow R$}
        \LeftLabel{$\FPtwo$}
        \UnaryInfC{$(\angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q} \land \demon{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}) \rightarrow R$}
        \DisplayProof
        \item \AxiomC{$Q\rightarrow \demon{\alpha}{Q}$}
        \LeftLabel{$\ind$}
        \UnaryInfC{$Q \rightarrow \demon{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
        \DisplayProof
    \end{itemize}
\end{lemmaE}
\begin{proofE}
    In the following, the derived rules are proved:
    \begin{itemize}
    \item Proof for $\impAD$:

    The proof is conducted by induction over the structure of the game $\alpha$. It is shown that the equivalence holds for all games $\alpha$.

    Induction start:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $\alpha \equiv x:=e$
        \[
        \begin{aligned}
            \angel{x:=e}{p(x)} \land \demon{x:=e}{q(x)} &\stackrel{\asA, \asD}{\leftrightarrow} p(e) \land q(e)\\
            &\stackrel{\asA}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\asA, \asD}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}\angel{x:=e}{(p(x) \land q(x))}\\
            &\stackrel{\phantom{\asA, \asD}}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{(x:=e)^{-d}}{(p(x) \land q(x))}
        \end{aligned}
        \]
        For the assignment game, even the equivalence of both sides of the implication can be shown. Using the rules for the assignment game, the left-hand side of the implication is transformed into $p(e) \land q(e)$. This can also be treated as one statement of the form $r(e)$, so the assignment rule for Angel can be applied again but now in the other direction. Then, the definition of the $-d$ operator is used to prove the claim.
        \item $\alpha\equiv x'=f(x)$
        \begin{figure}[]
            \centering
            \resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{  
                \AxiomC{$*$}
                \LeftLabel{id}
                \UnaryInfC{$p(x(s)), q(x(s)) \vdash p(x(s))$}
                \AxiomC{$*$}
                \LeftLabel{id}
                \UnaryInfC{$p(x(s)), q(x(s)) \vdash q(x(s))$}
                \LeftLabel{$\land R$}
                \BinaryInfC{$p(x(s)), q(x(s)) \vdash p(x(s)) \land q(x(s))$}
                \LeftLabel{$\exists{R}$}
                \UnaryInfC{$p(x(s)), q(x(s)) \vdash \exists t{\geq} 0\,(p(x(t)) \land q(x(t)))$}
                \LeftLabel{$\forall L$}
                \UnaryInfC{$p(x(s)), \forall t{\geq} 0\, q(x(t)) \vdash \exists t{\geq} 0\, (p(x(t)) \land q(x(t)))$}
                \LeftLabel{$\exists{L}$}
                \UnaryInfC{$\exists t{\geq} 0\,p(x(t)), \forall t{\geq} 0\, q(x(t)) \lor \bot \vdash \exists t{\geq} 0\, (p(x(t)) \land q(x(t)))$}
                \LeftLabel{$\asA$, $\asD$}
                \UnaryInfC{$\begin{aligned}[b]
                    \exists t{\geq} 0\, \angel{x:=x(t)}{p(x)},\\
                    \forall t{\geq} 0\, \demon{x:=x(t)}{q(x)}\\
                    \lor \exists t{\geq} 0\, \angel{x:=x(t)}{\lnot q(x) \land q(x)}{q(x)}
                \end{aligned}\ \vdash\ \exists t{\geq} 0\,\angel{x:=x(t)}{(p(x) \land q(x))}$ }
                \LeftLabel{$\conA$, $\conD$}
