                &(\lnot Q \land Q) \lor\\
                &(\angel{\alpha}{\bot}{\bot} \land\\
                &\demon{\alpha}{\bot}{\bot}) \rightarrow \bot
            \end{aligned}$}
            \LeftLabel{\FP}
            \BinaryInfC{$\vdash \angel{\alpha^*}{\lnot Q} \rightarrow \lnot Q \lor \bot$}
            \LeftLabel{prop. L.}
            \UnaryInfC{$\vdash \lnot Q \lor \lnot \angel{\alpha^*}{\lnot Q}$}
            \LeftLabel{\detR}
            \UnaryInfC{$\vdash \lnot Q \lor \demon{\alpha^*}{Q}$}
            \LeftLabel{wR}
            \UnaryInfC{$\vdash \lnot Q \lor \demon{\alpha^*}{Q}, \angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P \land Q)}$}
            \LeftLabel{$\lor R$}
            \UnaryInfC{$\vdash \lnot Q \lor \angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P \land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha^*}{Q}$} 
            \LeftLabel{prop. L.}
            \UnaryInfC{$\vdash Q \rightarrow (\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P \land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha^*}{Q})$}
            \LeftLabel{C.A. 2}
            \UnaryInfC{$\vdash Q \rightarrow \demon{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
            \DisplayProof
            }
        \end{figure}
        The proof rule ind is proven starting at the conclusion. First, the second complementarization axiom is used to disassemble $\demon{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$. Then, the part where Angel and Demon cooperate, $\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P \land Q)}$, is weakened away. Through determinacy and by extension with $\lor \bot$, the rest is brought into a shape that matches the \FP{} rule. Using propositional logic and determinacy, the left branch can partly be closed, and the remaining part is the premise of ind. The right branch can be closed because of \emph{false} in the antecedent.
    \end{itemize}
    \qed
\end{proofE}

A core insight is proved in Theorem \ref{Complementarization}: The two complementarization axioms (C.A. 1, 2) that convert one modality with two individual player goals into two modalities with complementary goals, are admissible. The axioms state that Angel and Demon can win a game either by cooperating or by competing. This matches their semi-competitive behavior which shows that the semantics are defined suitably. As the axioms are admissible, the set of provable formulas is unaffected by adding them, i.e. all these properties already apply to the proof calculus. On top, the relative completeness can now be proven almost for free, because the axioms link \dGLsc to \dGL for which such a proof already exists.
\begin{theoremE}[Complementarization Elimination][end]
    \label{Complementarization}
    The complementarization axioms are admissible in the proof calculus:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Complementarization axiom 1: $\angel{\alpha}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha}{P}$
        \item Complementarization axiom 2: $\demon{\alpha}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha}{Q}$
    \end{itemize}  
\end{theoremE}
\begin{proofE}
    Induction start:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item $\alpha \equiv x:=e$:
                \[
                \begin{aligned}
                    \angel{x:=e}{p(x)}{q(x)} &\stackrel{\asA}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} p(e)\\
                    &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} (p(e)\land q(e))\lor p(e)\\
                    &\stackrel{\asA}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}\angel{x:=e}{(p(x)\land q(x))} \lor \angel{x:=e}{p(x)}\\
                    &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{prop. L.}}}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{(x:=e)^{-d}}{(p(x)\land q(x))}\lor \angel{x:=e}{p(x)}
                \end{aligned}
                \]
                The proof for the assignment game is done by first using $\asA$ on the left-hand side of the equivalence and rewriting the result to $(p(e)\land q(e))\lor p(e)$. Then, the same rule is used into the other direction. After that, the definition of the $-d$ operator is applied to obtain the other side of the equivalence.

                The proof for Demon works similarly by rewriting $q(e)$ to $(p(e) \land q(e)) \lor q(e)$.
                \[
                \begin{aligned}
                    \demon{x:=e}{p(x)}{q(x)}&\stackrel{\asD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\asA, \asD}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} q(e)\\
                    &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\asA, \asD}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} (p(e) \land q(e))\lor q(e)\\
                    &\stackrel{\asA, \asD}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{x:=e}{(p(x) \land q(x))} \lor \demon{x:=e}{p(x)}\\
                    &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{prop. L.}}}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{(x:=e)^{-d}}{(p(x) \land q(x))} \lor \demon{x:=e}{p(x)}
                \end{aligned}
                \]
            \item $\alpha \equiv x'=f(x)$:
                \[
                \begin{aligned}
                    \angel{x'=f(x)}{p(x)}{q(x)} &\stackrel{\conA}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \exists t{\geq} 0\, \angel{x:=x(t)}{p(x)}{q(x)}\\
                    &\stackrel{\asA}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \exists t{\geq} 0\, p(x(t))\\
                    &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} \exists t{\geq}0\, ((p(x(t)) \land q(x(t))) \lor p(x(t)))\\
                    &\stackrel{\asA}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \exists t{\geq}0\,(\angel{x:=x(t)}{p(x)\land q(x)} \lor \angel{x:=x(t)}{p(x)})\\
                    &\stackrel{\text{FOL}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \exists t{\geq}0\,\angel{x:=x(t)}{p(x)\land q(x)} \lor \exists t{\geq}0\,\angel{x:=e}{p(x)}\\
                    &\stackrel{\conA}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}\angel{x'=f(x)}{(p(x)\land q(x))} \lor \angel{x'=f(x)}{p(x)}\\
                    &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{prop. L.}}}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{(x'=f(x))^{-d}}{(p(x)\land q(x))} \lor \angel{x'=f(x)}{p(x)}
                \end{aligned}
                \]
                The proof for the continuous game for Angel works similarly to the one for the assignment game. First, the rules $\conA$ and $\asA$ are used to remove all games from the formula. Then, $p(x(t))$ is rewritten as $(p(x(t))\land q(x(t))) \lor p(x(t))$. Afterwards, $\asA$ is used backwards and the existential quantifier is divided into two parts. For each part, $\conA$ is used. Applying the definition of the $-d$ operator, yields the right-hand side of the equivalence which proves the claim.
                \[
                \begin{aligned}
                    \demon{x'=f(x)}{p(x)}{q(x)}&\stackrel{\conD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\asA, \asD}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \forall t{\geq}0\, \demon{x:=x(t)}{p(x)}{q(x)} \lor \exists t{\geq}0\, \angel{x:=x(t)}{p(x)\land q(x)}{q(x)}\\
                    &\stackrel{\asA, \asD}{\leftrightarrow} \forall t{\geq}0\,q(x(t)) \lor \exists t{\geq}0\,(p(x(t)) \land q(x(t)))\\
                    &\stackrel{\text{FOL}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\asA, \asD}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \forall t{\geq}0\,q(x(t)) \lor \exists t{\geq}0\, (\lnot q(x(t)) \land q(x(t))) \lor \exists t{\geq}0\,(p(x(t)) \land q(x(t)))\\
                    &\stackrel{\asA, \asD}{\leftrightarrow}
                    \begin{aligned}[t]
                        &\forall t{\geq}0\, \demon{x:=x(t)}{q(x)} \lor \exists t{\geq}0\,\angel{x:=e}{\lnot q(t)\land q(t)}{q(t)} \lor\\
                        &\exists t{\geq} 0\, \angel{x:=x(t)}{(p(x) \land q(x))}
                    \end{aligned}\\
                    &\stackrel{\conA, \conD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\asA, \asD}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \demon{x'=f(x)}{q(x)} \lor \angel{x'=f(x)}{(p(x)\land q(x))}\\
                    &\stackrel{\phantom{\asA, \asD}}{\leftrightarrow} \demon{x'=f(x)}{q(x)} \lor \angel{(x'=f(x))^{-d}}{(p(x)\land q(x))}
               \end{aligned}
                \]
                In the proof for the continuous game for Demon, the formulas are first simplified as much as possible using $\asD, \asA, \asD$. Then, a $\lor \bot$ in the form $\lor \exists t{\geq}0\, (\lnot q(x(t)) \land q(x(t)))$ is appended. This brings the formula into the right shape for being able to use $\conA$ and $\conD$ after applying $\asA, \asD$ backwards. Using the definition of the $-d$ operator yields the right-hand side of the equivalence.
            \item $\alpha \equiv ?R$:
            \[
            \begin{aligned}
                \angel{?R}{P}{Q} &\stackrel{\tA}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} R \land P\\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} R \land ((P\land Q) \lor P)\\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} (R\land P\land Q) \lor (R\land P)\\
                &\stackrel{\tA}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}\angel{?R}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{?R}{P}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{prop. L.}}}{\leftrightarrow}\angel{(?R)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{?R}{P}
            \end{aligned}
            \]
            The proof for the test game for Angel works similarly to the proof for the assignment game with the main idea being to rewrite $P$ to $(P\land Q) \lor P$.
            \[
            \begin{aligned}
                \demon{?R}{P}{Q} &\stackrel{\tD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \lnot R \lor Q\\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} \lnot R \lor (P\land Q) \lor Q\\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} ((\lnot R \lor (P\land Q)) \land \top) \lor Q\\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} ((\lnot R \lor (P\land Q)) \land (\lnot R \lor R)) \lor Q\\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} ((\lnot R \lor (P\land Q))\land \lnot R) \lor (P\land Q \land R) \lor Q\\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} \lnot R \lor (P\land Q\land R) \lor Q\\
                &\stackrel{\tA, \tD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{?R}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{?R}{Q}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{prop. L.}}}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{(?R)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{?R}{Q}
            \end{aligned}
            \]
            The proof for the test game for Demon works similarly to Angel's. The main idea is to rewrite $Q$ to $(P\land Q) \lor Q$. Additionally, $\lnot R \lor (P\land Q)$ is extended with $\land (R \lor \lnot R)$ to insert $R$. After that step, the resulting formula can be transformed to the right-hand side of the equivalence straightforwardly.
        \end{itemize}

        Induction hypothesis:

        All formulas $\angel{\beta}{P}{Q}$ or $\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}$ with a game $\beta$ structurally smaller than $\alpha$ can be rewritten to $\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta}{P}$ and $\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\beta}{Q}$, respectively.

        Induction step:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item $\alpha \equiv \alpha\cup\beta$:
            \[
            \begin{aligned}
                \angel{\alpha\cup \beta}{P}{Q} &\stackrel{\chA}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{\alpha}{P}{Q} \lor \angel{\beta}{P}{Q}\\
                &\stackrel{IH}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha}{P} \lor \angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta}{P}\\
                &\leftrightarrow \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha}{P} \lor \angel{\beta}{P}\\
                &\stackrel{\chA}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{\alpha^{-d} \cup \beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha\lor \beta}{P}\\
                &\leftrightarrow \angel{(\alpha\cup \beta)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha\cup \beta}{P}
            \end{aligned}
            \]
            By decomposing the choice game with $\chA$, the IH hypothesis can be applied on the resulting parts. After a little rearrangement, it is easy to see that $\chA$ can be used again to obtain two modalities with choice games. After using the definition of the $-d$ operator, the result equals the right-hand side of the equivalence.
            \[
            \begin{aligned}
                \demon{\alpha\cup \beta}{P}{Q} &\stackrel{\chD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} (\demon{\alpha}{P}{Q} \land \demon{\beta}{P}{Q}) \lor (\demon{\alpha}{P}{Q} \land \angel{\alpha}{P}{Q}) \lor (\demon{\beta}{P}{Q} \land \angel{\beta}{P}{Q})\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \begin{aligned}[t]
                    &((\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha}{Q}) \land (\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\beta}{Q}))\\
                    &\lor ((\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha}{Q}) \land (\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha}{P}))\\
                    &\lor ((\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\beta}{Q}) \land (\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta}{P}))
                \end{aligned} \\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}
                \begin{aligned}[t]
                    &((\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha}{Q}) \land (\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\beta}{Q}))\\
                    &\lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor (\demon{\alpha}{Q} \land \angel{\alpha}{P})\\
                    &\lor \angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor (\demon{\beta}{Q} \land \angel{\beta}{Q})
                \end{aligned}\\
                &\stackrel{\id}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}\begin{aligned}[t]
                    &((\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha}{Q}) \land (\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\beta}{Q}))\\
                    &\lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}
                \end{aligned} \\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}
                \begin{aligned}[t]
                    &(\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \land \angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}) \lor (\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \land \demon{\beta}{Q})\\
                    &\lor (\demon{\alpha}{Q} \land \angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}) \lor (\demon{\alpha}{Q} \land \demon{\beta}{Q})\\
                    &\lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}
                \end{aligned} \\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}
                \begin{aligned}[t]
                    &\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}\\
                    &\lor (\demon{\alpha}{Q} \land \demon{\beta}{Q})
                \end{aligned}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}
                \begin{aligned}[t]
                    &\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}\\
                    &\lor (\demon{\alpha}{Q} \land \demon{\beta}{Q}) \lor \bot \lor \bot
                \end{aligned} \\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}
                \begin{aligned}[t]
                    &\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}\\
                    &\lor (\demon{\alpha}{Q} \land \demon{\beta}{Q})\\
                    &\lor (\demon{\alpha}{Q} \land \lnot \demon{\alpha}{Q}) \lor (\demon{\beta}{Q} \land \lnot \demon{\beta}{Q})
                \end{aligned} \\
                &\stackrel{\detR}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}
                \begin{aligned}[t]
                    &\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}\\
                    &\lor (\demon{\alpha}{Q} \land \demon{\beta}{Q})\\
                    &\lor (\demon{\alpha}{Q} \land \angel{\alpha}{\lnot Q}) \lor (\demon{\beta}{Q} \land \angel{\beta}{\lnot Q})
                \end{aligned}\\
                &\stackrel{\chA, \chD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}\angel{\alpha^{-d} \cup \beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha\cup \beta}{Q}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{prop. L.}}}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{(\alpha\cup \beta)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha\cup \beta}{Q}
            \end{aligned}
            \]
            In this proof, first, the choice game is disassembled with the rule $\chD$. Then, the IH is used on all resulting modalities. Thus, three parts connected by OR, are obtained. By use of the rule $\id$, the latter two parts can be reduced to $\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}$, resp. $\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P \land Q)}$. Then, the first part is expanded and simplified to $\demon{\alpha}{Q} \land \angel{\beta}{Q}$. Afterwards, the formula is extended by appending $\lor \bot$ two times, one in the form of $\demon{\alpha}{Q} \land \lnot \demon{\alpha}{Q}$ and the other as $\demon{\beta}{Q} \land \lnot \demon{\beta}{Q}$. To change some of these modalities to Angel's modalities, the NOT is pulled inside, using determinacy. Now the formula has the right shape to use $\chA, \chD$ and the definition of the $-d$ operator to transform it to the right-hand side of the equivalence.
            \item $\alpha \equiv \alpha;\beta$
            \[
            \begin{aligned}
                \angel{\alpha;\beta}{P}{Q} &\stackrel{\seqA}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha}{\angel{\beta}{P}{Q}}{\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(\angel{\beta}{P}{Q} \land \demon{\beta}{P}{Q})} \lor \angel{\alpha}{\angel{\beta}{P}{Q}}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}\begin{aligned}
                    &\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{((\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta}{P}) \land (\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\beta}{Q}))}\\
                    &\lor \angel{\alpha}{(\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta}{P})}
                \end{aligned}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor (\angel{\beta}{P} \land \demon{\beta}{Q}))} \lor \angel{\alpha}{(\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta}{P})}\\
                &\stackrel{\id}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \lor \angel{\alpha}{(\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta}{P})}\\
                &\stackrel{\re}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha}{\angel{\beta}{P}}}\\
                &\stackrel{\detR}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}}{\demon{\beta^{-d}}{\lnot(P\land Q)}} \lor \angel{\alpha}{\angel{\beta}{P}}{\demon{\beta}{\lnot P}}\\
                &\stackrel{\seqA}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha^{-d};\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha;\beta}{P}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{prop. L.}}}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{(\alpha;\beta)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha;\beta}{P}
            \end{aligned}
            \]
            To prove the claim for the sequential game for Angel, first, the game is broken into parts by using the rule $\seqA$. Then the induction hypothesis can be applied twice: first for the outer game $\alpha$ and then for $\beta$. Afterwards, the result is simplified with the rules $\id$ and $\re$. To achieve the right format for using $\seqA$ again, Demon's goal is transformed from $\lnot \angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P \land Q)}$ to $\demon{\beta^{-d}}{(\lnot(P\land Q))}$, resp. from $\lnot \angel{\beta}{P}$ to $\demon{\beta}{\lnot P}$ through determinacy. After applying $\seqA$ and the definition of the $-d$ operator, the right-hand side of the equivalence is obtained.

            The proof for Demon is conducted analogously:
            \[
            \begin{aligned}
                \demon{\alpha;\beta}{P}{Q} &\stackrel{\seqD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \demon{\alpha}{\angel{\beta}{P}{Q}}{\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}}\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(\angel{\beta}{P}{Q} \land \demon{\beta}{P}{Q})} \lor \demon{\alpha}{\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{((\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\beta}{P}) \land (\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\beta}{Q}))} \lor \demon{\alpha}{\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor (\angel{\beta}{P} \land \demon{\beta}{Q}))} \lor \demon{\alpha}{\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}}\\
                &\stackrel{\id}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \lor \demon{\alpha}{\demon{\beta}{P}{Q}}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \lor \demon{\alpha}{(\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q} \lor \demon{\beta}{Q})}\\
                &\stackrel{\reD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \lor \demon{\alpha}{\demon{\beta}{Q}}\\
                &\stackrel{\detR}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}}{\demon{\beta^{-d}}{(\lnot (P\land Q))}} \lor \demon{\alpha}{\angel{\beta}{(\lnot Q)}}{\demon{\beta}{Q}}\\
                &\stackrel{\seqD}{\mathmakebox[\widthof{$\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}$}]{\leftrightarrow}} \angel{\alpha^{-d};\beta^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha;\beta}{Q}\\
                &\stackrel{\phantom{\text{prop. L.}}}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{(\alpha;\beta)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha;\beta}{Q}
            \end{aligned}
            \]
            \item $\alpha \equiv \alpha^d$:
            \[
            \begin{aligned}
                \angel{\alpha^d}{P}{Q} &\stackrel{\dA}{\leftrightarrow} \demon{\alpha}{Q}{P}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha}{P}\\
                &\stackrel{\dA}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha^d}{P}\\
                &\leftrightarrow \angel{(\alpha^d)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha^d}{P}
            \end{aligned}
            \]
            Both proofs for the dual game work straightforwardly: The appropriate dual axiom is used, then the IH can be applied. Afterwards, the result is transformed back by utilizing the dual axiom again. Lastly, the definition of the $-d$ operator is used to derive the right-hand side of the equivalence.
            \[
            \begin{aligned}
                \demon{\alpha^d}{P}{Q} &\stackrel{\dD}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{\alpha}{Q}{P}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{IH}}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha}{Q}\\
                &\stackrel{\dD}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha^d}{Q}\\
                &\leftrightarrow \angel{(\alpha^d)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha^d}{Q}
            \end{aligned}
            \]
            \item $\alpha \equiv \alpha^*$:
            \begin{figure}[H]
                \centering
                \resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{
                    \begin{tabular}{@{}c@{}}
                    \AxiomC{$*$}
                    \LeftLabel{id}
                    \UnaryInfC{$P \lor \angel{\alpha; \alpha^*}{P} \vdash P \lor \angel{\alpha; \alpha^*}{P}$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\detR$, $\seqA$}
                    \UnaryInfC{$P \lor \angel{\alpha}{\angel{\alpha^*}{P}} \vdash P\lor \angel{\alpha; \alpha^*}{P}$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\rA$}
                    \UnaryInfC{\circled{2}  $P \lor \angel{\alpha}{\angel{\alpha^*}{P}} \vdash \angel{\alpha^*}{P}$}
                    \DisplayProof\\[1.25cm]
                    \AxiomC{$*$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\bot L$}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\bot \vdash \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \LeftLabel{\circled{$*$}}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\bot \lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\bot} \vdash \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\id$}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\bot \lor (\angel{\alpha^{d}}{\bot} \lor (\angel{\alpha}{\bot} \land \demon{\alpha}{\bot})) \vdash \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \LeftLabel{prop. L.}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\bot \lor ((\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\bot} \lor \angel{\alpha}{\bot}) \land (\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\bot} \lor \demon{\alpha}{\bot})) \vdash \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \LeftLabel{IH}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\vdash \bot \lor (\angel{\alpha}{\bot}{\bot} \land \demon{\alpha}{\bot}{\bot}) \rightarrow \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\rightarrow R$, prop. L.}
                    \UnaryInfC{\circled{4}  $\vdash (P \land \lnot P) \lor (\angel{\alpha}{\bot}{\bot} \land \demon{\alpha}{\bot}{\bot}) \rightarrow \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \DisplayProof\\[2cm]
                    \AxiomC{$*$}
                    \LeftLabel{id}
                    \UnaryInfC{$P \lor \angel{\alpha; \alpha^*}{P}{Q} \vdash P \lor \angel{\alpha; \alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \LeftLabel{det, $\seqA$}
                    \UnaryInfC{$P \lor \angel{\alpha}{\angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}} \vdash P \lor \angel{\alpha; \alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\rA$, ${\rightarrow} R$}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\vdash P \lor \angel{\alpha}{\angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}} \rightarrow \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \AxiomC{\circled{4}}
                    \LeftLabel{$\FP$}
                    \BinaryInfC{$\vdash\angel{\alpha^*}{P} \rightarrow \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q} \lor \bot$}
                    \LeftLabel{prop. L.}
                    \UnaryInfC{\circled{3}  $\vdash\angel{\alpha^*}{P} \rightarrow \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \DisplayProof\\[2cm]
                    \AxiomC{$*$}
                    \LeftLabel{id}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}, \demon{\alpha^*}{P}{Q} \vdash \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\land L$}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q} \land \demon{\alpha^*}{P}{Q} \vdash \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \LeftLabel{IH}
                    \UnaryInfC{$(\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha^*}{P}) \land (\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha^*}{Q}) \vdash \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \LeftLabel{prop. L.}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor (\angel{\alpha^*}{P} \land \demon{\alpha^*}{Q}) \vdash \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\id$}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \vdash \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\rightarrow R$}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \rightarrow \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \AxiomC{\circled{3}}
                    \LeftLabel{prop. L., $\land R$}
                    \BinaryInfC{\circled{1}   $\vdash\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha^*}{P} \rightarrow \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q}$}
                    \DisplayProof\\[2cm]
                    \AxiomC{\circled{2}}
                    \AxiomC{$*$}
                    \LeftLabel{id}
                    \UnaryInfC{$(P\land Q) \lor \angel{(\alpha;\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \vdash (P\land Q) \lor  \angel{(\alpha;\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\detR$, $\seqA$}
                    \UnaryInfC{$(P\land Q) \lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \vdash (P\land Q) \lor \angel{(\alpha;\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\rightarrow R$}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\vdash (P\land Q) \lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \rightarrow (P\land Q) \lor \angel{(\alpha;\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\rA$}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\vdash \ \begin{aligned}[b]
                        &(P\land Q) \lor \angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \rightarrow \\
                        &\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}
                    \end{aligned}$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\id$}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\vdash \ \begin{aligned}[b]
                        &(P\land Q) \lor (\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \lor\\
                        &(\angel{\alpha}{\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \land \demon{\alpha}{\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}})) \rightarrow\\
                        &\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}
                    \end{aligned}$}
                    \LeftLabel{prop. L.}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\vdash \ \begin{aligned}[b]
                        &(P\land Q) \lor ((\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \lor \angel{\alpha}{\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}}) \land\\
                        &(\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \lor \demon{\alpha}{\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}})) \rightarrow\\
                        &\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}
                    \end{aligned} $}
                    \LeftLabel{IH}
                    \UnaryInfC{$\vdash \ \begin{aligned}[b]
                        &(P\land Q) \lor (\angel{\alpha}{\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}}
                        {\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}} \land\\
                        &\demon{\alpha}{\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}}{\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}}) \rightarrow\\
                        &\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}
                    \end{aligned}$}
                    \LeftLabel{$\FP$}
                    \BinaryInfC{$\vdash \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q} \rightarrow \angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha^*}{P}$}
                    \AxiomC{\circled{1}}
                    \LeftLabel{prop. L., $\land R$}
                    \BinaryInfC{$\vdash \angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q} \leftrightarrow \angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha^*}{P}$}
                    \DisplayProof
                    \end{tabular}
                }
            \end{figure}

            \circled{$*$}: $\angel{\alpha^{-d}}{\bot}$ evaluates to \emph{false} because Angel can only win a game with such a goal, if Demon automatically loses. This can only happen, if he fails a test or is unable to run an ODE. But Angel has the control over the whole game $\alpha$, so this is impossible.

            To prove the equivalence for the repetition game for Angel, both implications are proved separately. The implication $\angel{\alpha^*}{P}{Q} \rightarrow \angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \angel{\alpha^*}{P}$ is shown by first applying the fixpoint rule. Then, both branches can easily be closed by using the rule $\rA$. The other implication is proved in the same way. To make the implication fit the \FP{} rule, the right-hand side of the implication is extended with $\lor \bot$. The left branch can then be closed as the others before, while for the right branch, the antecedent can be reduced to \emph{false}.
            \[
            \begin{aligned}
                \demon{\alpha^*}{P}{Q} &\stackrel{\rD}{\leftrightarrow} (Q \land \demon{\alpha;\alpha^*}{Q}) \lor (P\land Q) \lor \angel{(\alpha;\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}\\
                &\stackrel{\rA}{\leftrightarrow} (Q \land \demon{\alpha;\alpha^*}{Q}) \lor \angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L.}}{\leftrightarrow}  (Q \land \demon{\alpha;\alpha^*}{Q}) \lor \bot \lor \bot \lor \angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}\\
                &\stackrel{\text{prop. L., \circled{$*\!*$}}}{\leftrightarrow} (Q \land \demon{\alpha;\alpha^*}{Q}) \lor (Q\land \lnot Q) \lor \angel{(\alpha;\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(Q\land \lnot Q)} \lor \angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)}\\
                &\stackrel{\rD}{\leftrightarrow} \angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P\land Q)} \lor \demon{\alpha^*}{Q}
            \end{aligned}
            \]
            \circled{$*\!*$}: For the same reasons as noted above, $\angel{(\alpha;\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(Q \land \lnot Q)}$ also evaluates to \emph{false}.

            To prove the equivalence for the repetition game for Demon, first, the rule $\rD$ is used. One part of the result can then be converted to $\angel{(\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(P \land Q)}$ via $\rA$. The other part is extended twice with $\lor \bot$ to achieve the right shape for applying $\rD$ backwards. Therefore, one of them is rendered as $Q \land \lnot Q$ and the other as $\angel{(\alpha;\alpha^*)^{-d}}{(Q \land \lnot Q)}$. Using $\rD$ then yields the right-hand side of the equivalence which proves the claim.
        \end{itemize}
        \qed
\end{proofE}
\begin{example}
    These proof rules can now be used for inferences about Example~\ref{kayak}. See Fig.~\ref{exProof} for the proof tree. The abbreviations are $\Gamma \equiv h_A = h, h>0, h_D = 0, w_A >0, w_D>0, r=1, x>0$, $\alpha_1 \equiv r:=0 \;\cup\; ? \top$, $\alpha_2 \equiv h'_A = min(0, -w_A + r \cdot w_D), h'_D = max(0, r(w_A-w_D))$, $P \equiv h_A = 0 \land h\cdot w_A < x$, $Q \equiv h_D = h$. First, the choice in $\alpha_1$ is split up. Then, L. \ref{cutRopeCase} and L. \ref{cooperateCase} (see Appendix) are used to determine the ODEs in $\alpha_2$. These are then treated with $\conA$. Finally, both formulas resulting from splitting $\alpha_1$ might be proven in the case of $w_A > w_D$ with the additional assumptions that either Demon is heavy enough to act as a counterweight, or it is not too high to cut the rope and jump. If $w_A \leq w_D$, the part where Angel does not cut the rope  is reduced to \emph{false}, i.e. cooperating with Demon is not an option.
\end{example}

